\nWait, breaking down the second number 360,102,348:.\n\nBillions place: 3 (since it's 3 digits before the first comma: 3 (hundred millions?), 6 (ten millions?), 0 (millions?), then comma, then 1 (hundred thousands?), 0 (ten thousands), 2 (thousands), comma, 3 (hundreds), 4 (tens), 8 (ones). Hmm maybe better to count each digit's position.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps it's better to write both numbers digit by digit without commas:.\n\nFirst number: 7 5 0 2 8 7 6 2 1.\n\nSecond number: 3 6 0 1 0 2 3 4 8.\n\nWait, but that may not align properly. Let me think again.\n\nWait, perhaps the confusion is arising because of the commas. Let me write both numbers without commas and then add:.\n\nFirst number: 750287621.\n\nSecond number: 360102348.\n\nLet's write them aligned:.\n\n          7 5 0 2 8 7 6 2 1.\n        + 3 6 0 1 0 2 3 4 8.\n        ---------------------.\n\nStarting from the rightmost digit (units):.\n\n1st digit (units place): 1 + 8 = 9. Write down 9.\n\n2nd digit (tens): 2 + 4 = 6. Write 6.\n\n3rd digit (hundreds): 6 + 3 = 9. Write 9.\n\n4th digit (thousands): 7 (from first number) + 2 (from second) = 9. Write 9.\n\n5th digit (ten thousands): 8 (first) + 0 (second) = 8. Write 8.\n\n6th digit (hundred thousands): 2 (first) + 1 (second) = 3. Write 3.\n\n7th digit (millions): 0 (first) + 0 (second)? Wait, let's check the digits again.\n\nWait, let's list each digit position properly:.\n\nFirst number: 7 (hundred millions), 5 (ten millions), 0 (millions), 2 (hundred thousands), 8 (ten thousands), 7 (thousands), 6 (hundreds), 2 (tens), 1 (units).\n\nWait, perhaps breaking down each digit:.\n\nFirst number (750,287,621):.\n\nPositions (from right, starting at 1):.\n\n1 (units): 1.\n\n2 (tens): 2.\n\n3 (hundreds): 6.\n\n4 (thousands):7.\n\n5 (ten thousands):8.\n\n6 (hundred thousands):2.\n\n7 (millions):0.\n\n8 (ten millions):5.\n\n9 (hundred millions):7.\n\nSecond number (360,102,348):.\n\n1 (units):8.\n\n2 (tens):4.\n\n3 (hundreds):3.\n\n4 (thousands):2.\n\n5 (ten 