ver from position 9)\n\nSo the digits from position 10 to 1 are:\n\n1 (billions), 0 (hundred millions), 2 (ten millions), 7 (millions), 7 (hundred thousands), 0 (ten thousands), 4 (thousands), 8 (hundreds), 1 (tens), 3 (ones).\n\nTherefore, the number is 1,027,070,4813? Wait, no, commas are placed every three digits. Let's break it down:\n\nBillions digit:1\n\nThen the next three digits (hundred millions, ten millions, millions): 027\n\nWait, 0 (hundred millions), 2 (ten millions), 7 (millions) → 027, but leading zero can be omitted, so 27?\n\nWait, perhaps better to write the number as 1,027,070,481.3? Wait, no decimals. Wait, the digits are:\n\n1 (billions), 0 (hundred millions), 2 (ten millions), 7 (millions), 0 (hundred thousands), 7 (ten thousands?), Wait, no, let's see:\n\nWait, positions:\n\nPosition 10:1 (billions)\n\nPosition 9:0 (hundred millions)\n\nPosition8:2 (ten millions)\n\nPosition7:7 (millions)\n\nPosition6:7 (hundred thousands)\n\nPosition5:0 (ten thousands)\n\nPosition4:4 (thousands)\n\nPosition3:8 (hundreds)\n\nPosition2:1 (tens)\n\nPosition1:3 (ones)\n\nSo the number is:\n\nBillions:1\n\nHundred millions:0\n\nTen millions:2\n\nMillions:7\n\nHundred thousands:7\n\nTen thousands:0\n\nThousands:4\n\nHundreds:8\n\nTens:1\n\nOnes:3\n\nSo written out, it's 1,027,704,813.\n\nWait, let's see:\n\nBreaking into groups:\n\n1,027,704,813.\n\nYes, that makes sense:\n\n- The first comma separates billions: 1,\n\nThen the next three digits: 027 (but leading zeros can be omitted, so 27),\n\nWait, actually, standard formatting would be:\n\nStarting from the right, every three digits. Let's see:\n\n1,027,704,813.\n\nBreaking down:\n\n- The digits are 1 followed by 027, then 704, then 813.\n\nWait, perhaps:\n\n1,027,704,813 is correct. Let's confirm with another method:\n\nCompute 824,190,622 + 203,514,191.\n\nLet me add them numerically:\n\n824,190,622\n\n+203,514,191\n\nAdding the millions:\n\n824,190,622 is 824 million plus 190,622.\n\n203,514,191 is 203 milli