tmost is digit9 (ones). So:\n\nDigits:\n\n1:6 (100,000,000)\n\n2:4 (10,000,000)\n\n3:5 (1,000,000)\n\n4:1 (100,000)\n\nWait no, that can't be. Wait, perhaps better to list all digits:\n\nNumber: 6 (digit1),4 (digit2),5 (digit3),1 (digit4),5 (digit5),7 (digit6),2 (digit7),4 (digit8),5 (digit9).\n\nSo:\n\nDigit1: 6 (hundred millions place? Wait 9-digit number's first digit is hundred millions? Wait 10^8 is hundred millions, so yes.\n\nWait let's see:\n\nDigit positions for a 9-digit number:\n\nDigit 1: 100,000,000 (10^8) place (hundred millions)\n\nDigit 2:10,000,000 (ten millions)\n\nDigit3:1,000,000 (millions)\n\nDigit4:100,000 (hundred thousands)\n\nDigit5:10,000 (ten thousands)\n\nDigit6:1,000 (thousands)\n\nDigit7:100 (hundreds)\n\nDigit8:10 (tens)\n\nDigit9:1 (ones)\n\nTherefore, the second number (645,157,245):\n\nDigit1:6 (hundred millions)\n\nDigit2:4 (ten millions)\n\nDigit3:5 (millions)\n\nDigit4:1 (hundred thousands)\n\nDigit5:5 (ten thousands)\n\nDigit6:7 (thousands)\n\nDigit7:2 (hundreds)\n\nDigit8:4 (tens)\n\nDigit9:5 (ones)\n\nWait that makes sense. So:\n\nBreaking down the two numbers:\n\nFirst number (553,233,942):\n\nDigit1:5 (hundred millions)\n\nDigit2:5 (ten millions)\n\nDigit3:3 (millions)\n\nDigit4:2 (hundred thousands)\n\nDigit5:3 (ten thousands)\n\nDigit6:3 (thousands)\n\nDigit7:9 (hundreds)\n\nDigit8:4 (tens)\n\nDigit9:2 (ones)\n\nWait wait, let me confirm:\n\nFirst number:553,233,942.\n\nBreaking digits:\n\nDigit1:5 (hundred millions)\n\nDigit2:5 (ten millions)\n\nDigit3:3 (millions)\n\nDigit4:2 (hundred thousands)\n\nDigit5:3 (ten thousands)\n\nDigit6:3 (thousands)\n\nDigit7:9 (hundreds)\n\nDigit8:4 (tens)\n\nDigit9:2 (ones)\n\nWait but the original number is 553,233,942. Let me parse with commas:\n\n553,233,942 is 553 million, 233 thousand, 942.\n\nSo:\n\n553,000,000 + 233,000 + 942.\n\nBreaking down:\n\n553,000,000 is 5 (hundred millions), 5 (ten millions), 3 (millions).\n\nThen 233,000 is 2 (hundred thousands), 3 (ten thousands), 3 (thousands).\n\nThen 942 is 9 (hundreds), 4 (tens), 2 (ones). So that matches the digit breakdown above.\n\nOkay, so now I can proceed to add each digit place by place.\n\nLet me list all digits from both numbers in each place:\n\nStarting from the ones place (digit9):\n\nFirst number digit9:2\n\nSecond number digit9:5\n\nSum:2+5=7. Write down 7, no carryover.\n\nTens place (digit8):\n\nFirst number:4\n\nSecond number:4 (since second number's digit8 is 4, as per above breakdown)\n\nWait wait, second number's digit8 is 4 (tens place). First number's digit8 is 4 (tens). So 4+4=8. Write 8, no carryover.\n\nHundreds place (digit7):\n\nFirst number:9\n\nSecond number:2 (second number's digit7 is hundreds place, which is 2).\n\n9+2=11. Write 1, carryover 1.\n\nThousands place (digit6):\n\nFirst number:3\n\nSecond number:7 (second's digit6 is thousands place:7).\n\n3+7=10 plus carryover 1 is 11. Write 1, carryover 1.\n\nTen thousands place (digit5):\n\nFirst number:3\n\nSecond number:5 (second's digit5 is ten thousands place:5).\n\n3+5=8 plus carryover 1 =9. Write 9, no carryover.\n\nHundred thousands place (digit4):\n\nFirst number:2\n\nSecond number:1 (second's digit4 is hundred thousands place:1).\n\n2+1=3. Write 3.\n\nMillions place (digit3):\n\nFirst number:3\n\nSecond number:5 (second's digit3 is millions place:5).\n\n3+5=8. Write 8.\n\nTen millions place (digit2):\n\nFirst number:5\n\nSecond number:4 (second's digit2 is ten millions place:4).\n\n5+4=9. Write 9.\n\nHundred millions place (digit1):\n\nFirst number:5\n\nSecond number:6 (second's digit1 is hundred millions place:6).\n\n5+6=11. Since this is the highest digit, we need to handle this. 11 would mean we have to carryover to a new digit (billions place). So write 1 in the billions place and 1 in the hundred millions. Wait, actually, adding 5 and 6 gives 11, so the hundred millions place becomes 1 and carryover 1 to the billions place. Since there were no higher digits before, the total sum will have a billions place now.\n\nSo putting it all together:\n\nStarting from the highest digit (billions place):\n\nCarryover from hundred millions:1.\n\nThen the hundred millions place is 1 (from 11), but wait no:\n\nWait, when adding the hundred millions digits (digit1):\n\nFirst number's digit1 (hundred millions):5\n\nSecond's digit1 (hundred millions):6\n\nTotal:5+6=11. So we write 1 in the hundred millions place and carryover 1 to the billions place. But since there was no previous billions digit, this becomes the new highest digit.\n\nSo the digits from highest to lowest are:\n\nBillions place:1 (from carryover)\n\nHundred millions:1 (from 11)\n\nTen millions:9\n\nMillions:8\n\nHundred thousands:3\n\nTen thousands:9\n\nThousands:1 (from digit6's result)\n\nHundreds:1 (from digit7's result)\n\nTens:8\n\nOnes:7\n\nWait let me reconstruct step by step:\n\nStarting from the right:\n\nOnes:7\n\nTens:8\n\nHundreds:1 (with carryover 1 to thousands)\n\nThousands:1 (with carryover 1 to ten thousands)\n\nTen thousands:9 (no carryover)\n\nHundred thousands:3\n\nMillions:8\n\nTen millions:9\n\nHundred millions:1 (with carryover 1 to billions)\n\nBillions:1 (the carried over 1)\n\nSo the total digits are:\n\nBillions:1\n\nHundred millions:1\n\nTen millions:9\n\nMillions:8\n\nHundred thousands:3\n\nTen thousands:9\n\nThousands:1\n\nHundreds:1\n\nTens:8\n\nOnes:7\n\nPutting this all together as a number:\n\n1 (billions), 1 (hundred millions), 9 (ten millions), 8 (millions), 3 (hundred thousands), 9 (ten thousands), 1 (thousands), 1 (hundreds), 8 (tens), 7 (ones).\n\nSo the number is 1,198,391,187?\n\nWait let me write that out:\n\nBillions digit:1\n\nHundred millions:1 → so first three digits:119 (since 1 (billions), 1 (hundred millions), 9 (ten millions))\n\nThen millions place:8 → next three digits: 8 (millions), 3 (hundred thousands), 9 (ten thousands). Wait, perhaps breaking down into groups:\n\nThe number is:\n\n1,198,391,187.\n\nBreaking it down:\n\n1 (billions),\n\n198 (millions: 1 hundred million, 9 ten millions, 8 millions),\n\n391 (thousands: 3 hundred thousands, 9 ten thousands, 1 thousand),\n\n187 (units: 1 hundred, 8 tens, 7 ones).\n\nSo the total sum is 1,198,391,187.\n\nWait let me check with another method to confirm.\n\nAlternatively, adding the two numbers numerically:\n\nFirst number: 553,233,942\n\nSecond number: 645,157,245\n\nAdding them:\n\n553,233,942\n\n+645,157,245\n\n=?\n\nLet me add them step by step:\n\nStart with 553,233,942 + 645,157,245.\n\nAdding units digits: 2+5=7.\n\nTens:4+4=8.\n\nHundreds:9+2=11 → 1, carryover 1.\n\nThousands:3+7=10 +1=11 →1, carryover1.\n\nTen thousands:3+5=8 +1=9.\n\nHundred thousands:2+1=3.\n\nMillions:3+5=8.\n\nTen millions:5+4=9.\n\nHundred millions:5+6=11 →1, carryover1.\n\nAdding the carryover to the billions place: 1.\n\nSo the result is 1,198,391,187. That seems consistent.\n\nAlternatively, maybe I can add them as whole numbers:\n\n553,233,942 + 645,157,245.\n\nLet me add the millions first:\n\n553,233,942 is 553 million plus 233,942.\n\n645,157,245 is 645 million plus 157,245.\n\nAdding the millions:553M +645M = 1,198 million.\n\nThen the remaining parts:233,942 +157,245.\n\n233,942 +157,245:\n\n233,942 +150,000 = 383,942\n\nThen add 7,245: 383,942 +7,245 = 391,187.\n\nSo total is 1,198,000,000 +391,187 =1,198,391,187. Yes, that matches.\n\nTherefore, the final answer is 1,198,391,187. So the answer should be written in the boxed format as \\boxed{1198391187} or with commas, but in boxed form usually without commas. Let me confirm the exact number.\n\nYes, 1,198,391,187 is the correct sum. So boxed, it's \\boxed{1198391187}.\n\nWait but let me check once again with calculator-like addition:\n\n553233942\n\n+645157245\n\nLet's add digit by digit from right:\n\nStarting from the rightmost digit:\n\nPosition 1 (units):2+5=7\n\nPosition2 (tens):4+4=8\n\nPosition3 (hundreds):9+2=11 →1, carryover 1\n\nPosition4 (thousands):3+7=10 +1=11 →1, carryover1\n\nPosition5 (ten thousands):3+5=8 +1=9\n\nPosition6 (hundred thousands):2+1=3\n\nPosition7 (millions):3+5=8\n\nPosition8 (ten millions):5+4=9\n\nPosition9 (hundred millions):5+6=11 →1, carryover1\n\nPosition10 (billions): carryover1\n\nSo the digits from right to left are:\n\n7 (units),\n\n8 (tens),\n\n1 (hundreds),\n\n1 (thousands),\n\n9 (ten thousands),\n\n3 (hundred thousands),\n\n8 (millions),\n\n9 (ten millions),\n\n1 (hundred millions),\n\n1 (billions).\n\nThus, written left to right (billions first):\n\n1 (billions),\n\n1 (hundred millions),\n\n9 (ten millions),\n\n8 (millions),\n\n3 (hundred thousands),\n\n9 (ten thousands),\n\n1 (thousands),\n\n1 (hundreds),\n\n8 (tens),\n\n7 (units).\n\nSo that's 1,198,391,187. Correct.\n\nTherefore the answer is correct.\n"]  2%|1         | 8/500 [09:02<11:10:07, 81.72s/it]
