llions addition becomes the billions digit.\n\nPutting it all together:\n\nStarting from the right, the digits are:\n\n0 (ones), 2 (tens), 9 (hundreds), 7 (thousands), 8 (ten-thousands), 4 (hundred-thousands), 6 (millions), 0 (ten-millions), 1 (hundred-millions), and the carryover 1 in the billions place.\n\nSo the digits from right to left:\n\nBillions: 1\n\nHundred-millions: 1\n\nTen-millions: 0\n\nMillions: 6\n\nHundred-thousands: 4\n\nTen-thousands:8\n\nThousands:7\n\nHundreds:9\n\nTens:2\n\nOnes:0\n\nWait, actually, when building the number, the positions are:\n\nBillions place (1), hundred millions (1), ten millions (0), millions (6), hundred thousands (4), ten thousands (8), thousands (7), hundreds (9), tens (2), ones (0).\n\nSo the number is 1,106,487,920.\n\nWait, let me verify this with another method. Let's add them as whole numbers:\n\n415,705,416 + 690,782,504\n\nAdding the millions:\n\n415,705,416 is 415 million plus 705,416.\n\n690,782,504 is 690 million plus 782,504.\n\nAdding the millions: 415 + 690 = 1,105 million.\n\nThen adding the remaining parts: 705,416 + 782,504.\n\n705,416 + 700,000 is 1,405,416, plus 82,504 more: 1,405,416 + 82,504 = 1,487,920.\n\nSo total is 1,105,000,000 + 1,487,920 = 1,106,487,920. That matches the previous result. So the answer is correct.\n\nTherefore, the final sum is 1,106,487,920.\n\n"]
