(ten thousands):3\n\n6 (hundred thousands):8\n\n7 (millions):7\n\n8 (ten millions):3\n\n9 (hundred millions):6\n\nSecond number (796686200):\n\nPosition 1 (ones):0\n\n2 (tens):0\n\n3 (hundreds):2\n\n4 (thousands):6\n\n5 (ten thousands):8\n\n6 (hundred thousands):6\n\n7 (millions):9\n\n8 (ten millions):6\n\n9 (hundred millions):7\n\nNow adding each position:\n\nPosition 1:5+0=5\n\nPosition 2:6+0=6\n\nPosition3:8+2=10 \u21920, carryover1.\n\nPosition4:6+6=12 +1=13 \u21923, carryover1.\n\nPosition5:3+8=11 +1=12 \u21922, carryover1.\n\nPosition6:8+6=14 +1=15 \u21925, carryover1.\n\nPosition7:7+9=16 +1=17 \u21927, carryover1.\n\nPosition8:3+6=9 +1=10 \u21920, carryover1.\n\nPosition9:6+7=13 +1=14 \u21924, carryover1.\n\nThen the final carryover1 becomes a new digit (position10) \u21921.\n\nSo the result digits from position1 to position10 are:\n\n1:5\n\n2:6\n\n3:0\n\n4:3\n\n5:2\n\n6:5\n\n7:7\n\n8:0\n\n9:4\n\n10:1\n\nTherefore, the number is:\n\nPosition10 (billions):1,\n\nPosition9 (hundred millions):4,\n\nPosition8 (ten millions):0,\n\nPosition7 (millions):7,\n\nPosition6 (hundred thousands):5,\n\nPosition5 (ten thousands):2,\n\nPosition4 (thousands):3,\n\nPosition3 (hundreds):0,\n\nPosition2 (tens):6,\n\nPosition1 (ones):5.\n\nSo arranging them: 1 (billion), 4 (hundred millions), 0 (ten millions),7 (millions),5 (hundred thousands),2 (ten thousands),3 (thousands),0 (hundreds),6 (tens),5 (ones).\n\nThus the number is 1,407,523,065.\n\nBut when I added the millions parts earlier, I thought:\n\nFirst number:637 million (but wait, 637,836,865 is actually 637 million plus 836 thousand 865. Wait no: 637,836,865 is 637 million (637,000,000) plus 836,865. The second number is 796,686,200 which is 796,000,000 plus 686,200.\n\nSo adding the millions parts: 637,000,000 + 796,000,000 = 1,433,000,000.\n\nAdding the remaining parts: 836,865 +686,200 = 1,523,065.\n\nTotal sum:1,433,000,000 +1,523,065 =1,434,523,065.\n\nWait, this contradicts the digit-by-digit result of 1,407,523,065.