2 (tens):6+0=6\n\nDigit3 (hundreds):8+2=10 →0, carryover1.\n\nDigit4 (thousands):6 (first) +6 (second)=12 +1=13 →3, carryover1.\n\nDigit5 (ten thousands):3 (first) +8 (second)=11 +1=12 →2, carryover1.\n\nDigit6 (hundred thousands):8 (first) +6 (second)=14 +1=15 →5, carryover1.\n\nDigit7 (millions):7 (first) +6 (second)=13 +1=14 →4, carryover1? Wait, wait, here\'s the mistake!\n\nWait digit7 is the millions place:\n\nFirst number\'s digit7:7 (millions).\n\nSecond number\'s digit7:6 (millions). Wait no, the second number\'s millions digit is 6? Wait let\'s clarify:\n\nWait for the second number: 796,686,200.\n\nBreaking down:\n\nHundred millions:7,\n\nTen millions:9,\n\nMillions:6,\n\nHundred thousands:6,\n\nTen thousands:8,\n\nThousands:6,\n\nHundreds:2,\n\nTens:0,\n\nOnes:0.\n\nAh! Here\'s the mistake. The millions digit of the second number is 6, not 9. Wait, the ten millions digit is 9, millions is6.\n\nSo when adding the millions place (digit7):\n\nFirst number\'s millions digit is7, second is6. 7+6=13, plus carryover1 (from hundred thousands place which was 15, carryover1).\n\nWait, let me retrace:\n\nDigit7 (millions):\n\nFirst number:7 (millions place)\n\nSecond number:6 (millions place).\n\nSo adding 7+6=13 plus the previous carryover1 gives 14. So digit is4, carryover1.\n\nAh! So that\'s where the mistake was in the previous method. I had assumed the second number\'s millions digit was9, but actually it\'s6 (since the ten millions is9, and millions is6).\n\nSo the millions place addition is 7+6=13 +1=14, not 7+9=16. That\'s the error.\n\nSo correcting that:\n\nDigit7 (millions):\n\n7 (first) +6 (second) =13 +1 (carryover) =14 →4, carryover1.\n\nProceeding:\n\nDigit8 (ten millions):\n\nFirst number:3 (ten millions)\n\nSecond number:9 (ten millions).\n\n3+9=12 +1 (carryover) =13 →3, carryover1? Wait:\n\nWait digit8 is ten millions:\n\nFirst number\'s ten millions digit is3,\n\nSecond\'s is9,\n\nso 3+9=12, plus carryover1 (from millions place) gives13 →3, carry