them:\n\nStart from the right:\n\n5 +0 =5\n\n6+0=6\n\n8+2=10 (0, carryover1)\n\n6+6=12+1=13 (3, carryover1)\n\n3+8=11+1=12 (2, carryover1)\n\n8+6=14+1=15 (5, carryover1)\n\n7 (millions place first) +6 (millions place second)=13 +1=14 (4, carryover1)\n\n3 (ten millions first) +9 (ten millions second)=12 +1=13 (3, carryover1)\n\n6 (hundred millions first) +7 (hundred millions second)=13 +1=14 (4, carryover1)\n\nFinally carryover1.\n\nSo the result is 1 followed by 4,0,7,5,2,3,0,6,5 → 1,407,523,065.\n\nWait, so that\'s correct. So where was the error in the millions method?\n\nAh, because when I said the first number is 637 million plus 836,865, but actually, the 637 million is already including the hundred millions and ten millions. Wait, the breakdown is:\n\nFirst number: 637,836,865 is 637,000,000 (which is 6 hundred millions, 3 ten millions,7 millions) plus 836,865.\n\nSecond number:796,686,200 is 796,000,000 (7 hundred millions,9 ten millions,6 millions) plus 686,200.\n\nSo adding the 637,000,000 and 796,000,000 gives 1,433,000,000.\n\nThen adding the remaining parts: 836,865 +686,200 =1,523,065.\n\nTotal:1,433,000,000 +1,523,065 =1,434,523,065.\n\nBut this contradicts the digit-by-digit. There\'s a problem here. Wait, maybe the breakdown is wrong?\n\nWait, the first number\'s 637 million is actually 637,000,000, but the actual number is 637,836,865. So the "remaining" part after the 637,000,000 is 836,865. That\'s correct.\n\nSecond number:796,686,200 is 796,000,000 plus686,200. Correct.\n\nSo adding those parts gives 1,433,000,000 +1,523,065 =1,434,523,065.\n\nBut digit-by-digit gives 1,407,523,065.\n\nThe only way these can both be true is if there\'s a miscalculation somewhere. Let me compute 637,836,865 +796,686,200 using a calculator approach step by step:\n\nAdding the two numbers:\n\n637,836,865\n\n+796,686,200\n\nStart from the right:\n\nOnes place:5+0=5.\n\nTens:6+0=6.\n\nHundreds:8+2=10 →0, carryover1.\n\nThousands:6+6=12 +1=13 →3, carryover1.\n\nTen thousands:3+8=11 +1=12 →2, carryover1.\n\nHundred thousands:8+6=14 +1=15 →5, carryover1.\n\nMillions:7+6=13 +1=14 →4, carryover1.\n\nTen millions:3+9=12 +1=13 →3, carryover1.\n\nHundred millions:6+7=13 +1=14 →4, carryover1.\n\nBillions: carryover1 →1.\n\nSo the total is 1,407,523,065.\n\nBut according to the other method, it\'s 1,434,523,065. These differ by 27,000,000. Wait, 1,434,523,065 minus1,407,523,065 is 27,000,000. Where is this coming from?\n\nAh! Wait a second, in the millions addition approach, I added 637 million (from first number) and 796 million (from second) to get 1,433 million. But the first number is 637,836,865, which is actually 637 million plus 836,865. So the total is indeed 637,836,865 =637,000,000 +836,865.\n\nSimilarly, second number is796,686,200=796,000,000 +686,200.\n\nThus adding the 637,000,000 +796,000,000 =1,433,000,000.\n\nAdding the rest:836,865 +686,200 =1,523,065.\n\nTotal sum:1,433,000,000 +1,523,065 =1,434,523,065.\n\nBut the digit-by-digit gives1,407,523,065. The difference is 27,000,000. Where is this discrepancy?\n\nWait, the digit-by-digit result is 1,407,523,065. Let\'s subtract the two results:\n\n1,434,523,065 (from millions method)\n\n-1,407,523,065 (digit-by-digit) =27,000,000.\n\nAh, so there must be an error in one of the methods. Let me check the first number\'s value:\n\nFirst number:637,836,865.\n\nSecond number:796,686,200.\n\nAdding them:\n\nLet me do this step by step in another way:\n\nTake first number:637,836,865.\n\nAdd 700,000,000 to it: 637,836,865 +700,000,000 =1,337,836,865.\n\nThen add 96,686,200 (since the second number is796,686,200 =700,000,000 +96,686,200):\n\n1,337,836,865 +96,686,200.\n\nAdding 96,000,000 first:\n\n1,337,836,865 +96,000,000 =1,433,836,865.\n\nThen add 686,200:\n\n1,433,836,865 +686,200 =1,434,523,065.\n\nSo this confirms the millions method result.\n\nTherefore, the digit-by-digit method must have an error. Let me re-examine the digit-by-digit steps again.\n\nWait, let\'s write both numbers with their digits in order:\n\nFirst number: 6 3 7 8 3 6 8 6 5 (from hundred millions to ones).\n\nSecond number:7 9 6 6 8 6 2 0 0.\n\nAdding each digit:\n\nPosition9 (hundred millions):6+7=13 +1 (carryover from ten millions? Wait let\'s retrace step by step.\n\nStarting over:\n\nPosition1 (ones):\n\n5+0=5.\n\nPosition2 (tens):\n\n6+0=6.\n\nPosition3 (hundreds):\n\n8+2=10 →0, carryover1.\n\nPosition4 (thousands):\n\n6+6=12 +1=13 →3, carryover1.\n\nPosition5 (ten thousands):\n\n3+8=11 +1=12 →2, carryover1.\n\nPosition6 (hundred thousands):\n\n8+6=14 +1=15 →5, carryover1.\n\nPosition7 (millions):\n\n7 (first) +6 (second)=13 +1=14 →4, carryover1.\n\nPosition8 (ten millions):\n\n3 (first) +9 (second)=12 +1=13 →3, carryover1.\n\nPosition9 (hundred millions):\n\n6+7=13 +1=14 →4, carryover1.\n\nFinal carryover:1.\n\nThus the digits are:\n\nBillions:1,\n\nHundred millions:4,\n\nTen millions:3,\n\nMillions:4,\n\nHundred thousands:5,\n\nTen thousands:2,\n\nThousands:3,\n\nHundreds:0,\n\nTens:6,\n\nOnes:5.\n\nWait, so arranging them:\n\nBillions (1),\n\nHundred millions (4),\n\nTen millions (3),\n\nMillions (4),\n\nHundred thousands (5),\n\nTen thousands (2),\n\nThousands (3),\n\nHundreds (0),\n\nTens (6),\n\nOnes (5).\n\nWait, so that would be:\n\n1 (billions),\n\n4 (hundred millions),\n\n3 (ten millions),\n\n4 (millions),\n\n5 (hundred thousands),\n\n2 (ten thousands),\n\n3 (thousands),\n\n0 (hundreds),\n\n6 (tens),\n\n5 (ones).\n\nWhich translates to 1,434,523,065! Wait a minute! I think I made a mistake in the digit positions when I wrote the final number earlier.\n\nWait, let\'s list the digits from highest to lowest place:\n\nPosition10 (billions):1,\n\nPosition9 (hundred millions):4,\n\nPosition8 (ten millions):3,\n\nPosition7 (millions):4,\n\nPosition6 (hundred thousands):5,\n\nPosition5 (ten thousands):2,\n\nPosition4 (thousands):3,\n\nPosition3 (hundreds):0,\n\nPosition2 (tens):6,\n\nPosition1 (ones):5.\n\nTherefore, the number is:\n\n1 (billion),\n\n4 (hun