\n\n- Thousands:4.\n\n- Hundreds:4.\n\n- Tens:5.\n\n- Ones:3.\n\nYes, so when written as positions from right to left (for addition):.\n\nPositions 1-9:.\n\n1:3 (ones).\n\n2:5 (tens).\n\n3:4 (hundreds).\n\n4:4 (thousands).\n\n5:2 (ten thousands).\n\n6:7 (hundred thousands).\n\n7:0 (millions).\n\n8:3 (ten millions).\n\n9:7 (hundred millions).\n\nOkay, now adding each position:.\n\nStarting from Position 1 (ones):.\n\n3 (from first) + 3 (from second) = 6. Write down 6. No carryover.\n\nPosition 2 (tens):.\n\n7 (first) +5 (second) =12. Write 2, carryover 1.\n\nPosition3 (hundreds):.\n\n9 (first) +4 (second) =13, plus carryover 1 =14. Write 4, carryover1.\n\nPosition4 (thousands):.\n\n0 (first) +4 (second)=4 +1 (carryover)=5. Write 5, no carryover.\n\nPosition5 (ten thousands):.\n\n5 (first) +2 (second)=7. Write 7, no carryover.\n\nPosition6 (hundred thousands):.\n\n5 (first) +7 (second)=12. Write 2, carryover1.\n\nPosition7 (millions):.\n\n0 (first) +0 (second)=0 +1 (carryover)=1. Write 1, no carryover.\n\nPosition8 (ten millions):.\n\n6 (first) +3 (second)=9. Write9, no carryover.\n\nPosition9 (hundred millions):.\n\n7 (first) +7 (second)=14. Write 4, carryover 1. But since there's no higher position, we need to add that 1 as a new digit at the beginning.\n\nWait, but the original numbers are 9 digits each, so adding them could give up to 10 digits. So the carryover here would create a tenth digit.\n\nSo after adding the hundred millions (position9):.\n\n7+7=14, plus any carryover? Wait, in the previous step (position7 and 8), there was no carryover except from position6.\n\nWait let's go step by step again to make sure.\n\nWait let's retrace step by step:.\n\nPosition1: 3+3=6, no carryover.\n\nPosition2:7+5=12. So 2, carryover 1.\n\nPosition3:9+4=13 +1 (carryover) =14. So 4, carryover1.\n\nPosition4:0+4=4 +1=5, no carryover.\n\nPosition5:5+2=7, no carryover.\n\nPosition6:5+7=12, so write 2, carryover1.\n\nPosition7:0 (from first number) +0 (second's millions place)=0