ond has a 9. 2 + 9 = 11 again. Another carry-over situation. So we write down 1 and carry over 1 again. .\n\nNext is the ten millions place. The first number has a 1, and the second has an 8. Adding those gives 1 + 8 = 9. Adding the carried-over 1 gives 10. Wait, that's 10, so we write down 0 and carry over 1 to the next place. Hmm, need to remember that.\n\nNow the hundred millions place. The first number has an 8, and the second also has an 8. 8 + 8 = 16. Plus the carried-over 1 makes 17. So we write down 7 and carry over 1 again.\n\nFinally, the billions place. The first number has an 8, and the second has another 8. Adding those gives 8 + 8 = 16. Adding the carried-over 1 gives 17. So we write down 17 here. Since there are no more digits to the left, we just take that 17 as is. .\n\nPutting it all together, starting from the right, the digits we got were:.\nOnes:7, Tens:9, Hundreds:3, Thousands:8, Ten thousands:1 (with a carryover), Hundred thousands:8, Millions:1 (with carryover), Ten millions:0 (with carryover), Hundred millions:7 (with carryover), and then the billions place 17. .\n\nWait, maybe I should write this out step by step more carefully to ensure I didn't mix up any digits. Let me try aligning them properly.\n\nLet me write both numbers again:.\n\n801,225,344.\n+889,293,053.\n-------------.\n\nLet me break down each digit position:.\n\nStarting from the right (ones place):.\n\nPosition 1 (ones): 4 + 3 = 7.\nPosition 2 (tens): 4 + 5 = 9.\nPosition 3 (hundreds): 3 + 0 = 3.\nPosition 4 (thousands): 5 + 3 = 8.\nPosition 5 (ten thousands): 2 + 9 = 11 → write 1, carryover 1.\nPosition 6 (hundred thousands): 5 + 2 + 1 (carryover) = 8 → 8, no carryover.\nWait, hold on. Wait, maybe I messed up the positions here. Let me check again.\n\nWait, perhaps I need to label each digit's place correctly. Let me count the digits properly. Let's see:.\n\nFirst number: 801,225,344. Let's count the digits: 8 (billion), 0 (hundred million), 1 (ten million), 2 (million), 2 (hundred thousand), 2 (ten thousand), 5 (thousand), 3 (hundred), 4 (ten), 4 (one). Wait, actually, commas separate the digits into groups, so 801,225,344 is 8 (hundred million?), no, wait. Wait, the standard way is:.\n\nIn the US numbering system, commas are placed every three digits starting from the right. So 801,225,344 would be:.\n\nBreaking it down:.\n\n- 801 (millions part): 801 million.\n- 225 (thousands part): 225 thousand.\n- 344 (units): 344.\n\nSo each digit's place:.\n\nStarting from the rightmost digit (position 1 is ones):.\n\nNumber1: 8 0 1 2 2 5 3 4 4.\n\nWait, actually, writing the number digit by digit:.\n\n8 (hundred millions place?), wait perhaps I need to think of each digit's position properly. Let's consider both numbers digit by digit from right to left (since we add right to left):.\n\nFirst number: 801,225,344.\n\nBreaking down each digit from right to left:.\n\nPosition 1 (ones): 4.\n\nPosition 2 (tens): 4.\n\nPosition 3 (hundreds): 3.\n\nPosition 4 (thousands): 5.\n\nPosition 5 (ten thousands): 2.\n\nPosition 6 (hundred thousands): 2.\n\nPosition 7 (millions): 1.\n\nPosition 8 (ten millions): 0.\n\nPosition 9 (hundred millions): 8.\n\nSimilarly, the second number: 889,293,053.\n\nBreaking down from right to left:.\n\nPosition 1 (ones): 3.\n\nPosition 2 (tens): 5.\n\nPosition 3 (hundreds): 0.\n\nPosition 4 (thousands): 3.\n\nPosition 5 (ten thousands): 9.\n\nPosition 6 (hundred thousands): 2.\n\nPosition 7 (millions): 9.\n\nPosition 8 (ten millions): 8.\n\nPosition 9 (hundred millions): 8.\n\nPosition 10 (billions): 8?.\n\nWait, hold on, let me count again. Wait, 889,293,053. Let's parse this:.\n\nBreaking down 889,293,053:.\n\nThe number is 889,293,053. The commas are placed every three digits from the right, so:.\n\nThe first comma separates millions, so:.\n\n889 (millions part) followed by 293 (thousands) and 053 (units). Wait, but 889 million is 889,000,000. Then 293,053 is the rest. So total digits:.\n\n8 (hundred millions), 8 (ten mi