s), 7 (tens), 4 (hundreds), 9 (thousands), 4 (ten thousands), 4 (hundred thousands), 5 (millions), 7 (ten millions), 9 (hundred millions).\n\nSo adding them digit by digit starting from the ones:.\n\nOnes place: 7 + 3 = 10. Write 0, carryover 1.\n\nTens: 4 +7 = 11 +1 =12. Write 2, carryover 1.\n\nHundreds:4 +4 =8 +1=9. Write 9, no carryover.\n\nWait, wait, earlier I thought the hundreds place for first number was 6, but no. Wait, maybe I made a mistake here. Let me check again.\n\nWait, perhaps I need to list all digits in order. Let's write both numbers with all their digits:.\n\nFirst number: 1 7 8 8 2 6 4 4 7 (from hundred millions to ones).\nWait, actually, 178,826,447 is 1 (hundred millions), 7 (ten millions), 8 (millions), 8 (hundred thousands), 2 (ten thousands), 6 (thousands), 4 (hundreds), 4 (tens), 7 (ones).\n\nSecond number: 975,449,473 is 9 (hundred millions), 7 (ten millions),5 (millions),4 (hundred thousands),4 (ten thousands),9 (thousands),4 (hundreds),7 (tens),3 (ones).\n\nSo aligning each digit:.\n\nHundred millions: 1 +9 = 10.\nTen millions:7 +7 =14.\nMillions:8 +5 =13.\nHundred thousands:8 +4 =12.\nTen thousands:2 +4 =6.\nThousands:6 +9 =15.\nHundreds:4 +4 =8.\nTens:4 +7 =11.\nOnes:7 +3 =10.\n\nWait, but when adding, we start from the right (ones) and move left, carrying over as needed. So let's do it step by step properly.\n\nStarting with Ones place:.\n7 (from first) +3 (second) =10. Write down 0, carryover 1.\n\nTens place: 4 +7 =11 plus the carried 1 is 12. Write 2, carryover 1.\n\nHundreds:4 +4 =8 plus 1 is 9. Write 9, no carryover.\n\nThousands:6 +9 =15. Write 5, carryover 1.\n\nTen thousands:2 +4 =6 plus 1 is 7. Write 7, no carryover.\n\nHundred thousands:8 +4 =12. Write 2, carryover 1.\n\nMillions:8 +5 =13 plus 1 is 14. Write 4, carryover 1.\n\nTen millions:7 +7 =14 plus 1 is 15. Write 5, carryover 1.\n\nHundred millions:1 +9 =10 plus 1 is 11. Write 1 and carryover 1. But since there are no more digits, that carryover 1 becomes a new digit