ition8 (ten millions):6+8=14 \u21924, carryover1.\n\nPosition9 (hundred millions):2+1 +1(carry)=4 \u21924, no carry.\n\nThus, the digits are as before, giving 445,394,450. But according to the other method, it's 427,394,450. The difference is in the hundred millions place: 4 vs 4, but the ten millions place in the first method is 4, but according to the other method, the total is 427 million, so the ten millions digit should be 2? Wait, no:\n\nWait, 427,394,450 would be broken down as:\n\n4 (hundred millions), 2 (ten millions), 7 (millions)... Wait, no, 427 million is 427,000,000. So 427,394,450 is 4 (hundred millions), 2 (ten millions),7 (millions), etc.\n\nBut according to digit-wise calculation, the hundred millions digit is 4, ten millions is4, so that would be 445 million something. So conflicting results.\n\nTherefore, one of the methods is wrong. Let me do the actual addition with numbers:\n\n246,666,704\n\n+180,727,746\n\nLet me add them numerically:\n\n246,666,704\n\n+180,727,746\n\n= ?\n\nAdding the numbers:\n\nStart with the units:\n\n4 +6 =10 \u21920, carryover1.\n\nTens:0 +4 +1=5.\n\nHundreds:7+7=14 \u21924, carryover1.\n\nThousands:6+7=13 +1=14 \u21924, carryover1.\n\nTen thousands:6+2=8 +1=9.\n\nHundred thousands:6+7=13 \u21923, carryover1.\n\nMillions:4 +0 +1=5.\n\nTen millions:6 +8=14 \u21924, carryover1.\n\nHundred millions:2 +1 +1=4.\n\nThus, the result is 445,394,450.\n\nWait, but when I did the other approach, I had 427 million. Wait, maybe I made a mistake in that approach. Let me recalculate that method again.\n\nThe first number is 246,666,704. So that's 246 million plus 666 thousand 704.\n\nThe second number is 180,727,746, which is 180 million plus 727 thousand 746.\n\nAdding the millions parts: 246 + 180 = 426 million.\n\nAdding the thousands parts: 666,704 + 727,746.\n\nWait, 666,704 +727,746.\n\nLet me compute that separately:\n\n666,704\n\n+727,746\n\n=?\n\nAdding units digits:\n\n4 +6 =10 \u21920, carryover1.\n\nTens:0+4 +1=5.\n\nHundreds:7+7=14 \u21924, carryover1.\n\nThousands:6+2=8 +1=9.\n\nTen thousands:6+7=13 \u21923, carryover1.\n\nHundred thousands:6+7=13 +1=14 \u21924, carryover1.\n\nMillions:0 (since both are six-digit numbers here) plus carryover1 \u21921.\n\nWait, this is getting confusing. Let me do it step by step:\n\n666,704 +727,746.\n\nAdding digit-wise:\n\nPosition1 (ones):4+6=10 \u21920, carryover1.\n\nPosition2 (tens):0+4 +1=5.\n\nPosition3 (hundreds):7+7=14 \u21924, carryover1.\n\nPosition4 (thousands):6+2=8 +1=9.\n\nPosition5 (ten thousands):6+7=13 \u21923, carryover1.\n\nPosition6 (hundred thousands):6+7=13 +1=14 \u21924, carryover1.\n\nPosition7 (millions):0 (since 666,704 is only up to hundred thousands) plus 0 (since 727,746 is also up to hundred thousands) plus carryover1 \u21921.\n\nSo total of this addition is 1,394,450 (since the 1 is in the millions place). So the total of the two numbers is:\n\n246,000,000 +180,000,000 =426,000,000.\n\nPlus the 1,394,450 gives 427,394,450.\n\nWait a second, but according to the digit-wise addition, the total is 445,394,450. So now I'm really confused. There must be an error in one of the calculations.\n\nWait, let's do the addition using another method:.\n\n246,666,704\n\n+180,727,746\n\nLet me add them in parts:\n\nBreak down the first number into 200,000,000 +46,666,704.\n\nSecond number:100,000,000 +80,727,746.\n\nAdding 200M +100M =300M.\n\n46,666,704 +80,727,746 = ?\n\n46,666,704 +80,727,746.\n\nAdding:\n\n46,666,704\n\n+80,727,746\n\n=127,394,450.\n\nSo total is 300M +127,394,450 =427,394,450.\n\nHmm, so now this method gives the same as the second approach, so that must be correct. Therefore the digit-wise addition must have an error.\n\nWhere did I go wrong in the digit-wise approach?\n\nLooking back:\n\nPosition8 (ten millions place): first number has 6, second has8. 6+8=14. So writing 4 and carryover1.\n\nPosition9 (hundred millions place): first is2, second is1, plus carryover1 \u21922+1+1=4.\n\nSo that gives the hundred 