messages:  [["Please act as a math teacher and solve the addition problem in the given template.\n####\n# Question:\nWhat is the sum of <<number1>> and <<number2>>?\n# Reasoning:\nLet's add the two numbers digit by digit.\n1. The ones place: <<digit1>>\n2. The tens place: <<digit2>>\n<<other digits>>\nAnswer:\nTherefore, the final computed sum is <<answer>>.\n####\n# Question:\nWhat is the sum of 745138857 and 996137931?\n# Reasoning:", "<think>\nOkay, so I need to add these two big numbers: 745,138,857 and 996,137,931. Hmm, let me write them down to make sure I have them right. .\n\nFirst, aligning the numbers by their place values. Let me start from the rightmost digit, which is the ones place. .\n\nStarting with the ones place: the first number has a 7, and the second has a 1. Adding those gives 7 + 1 = 8. So the ones digit of the sum is 8. No carry-over here.\n\nNext, the tens place: the first number has a 5, and the second has a 3. 5 + 3 is 8. So the tens digit is 8. Still no carry-over.\n\nMoving to the hundreds place: 8 (from the first number) and 9 (from the second). 8 + 9 = 17. Oh, that's more than 10, so I need to write down 7 and carry over 1 to the next column. Let me note that down: hundreds digit is 7, carryover 1.\n\nNow the thousands place: The first number has an 8, the second has a 7. Adding those gives 8 + 7 = 15, plus the carried-over 1 makes 16. So the thousands digit is 6, and carry over 1 again.\n\nTens of thousands place: First number's digit is 3, second is 3. 3 + 3 = 6, plus the carried-over 1 is 7. So that's 7 here, no carry-over.\n\nHundreds of thousands place: 1 (from first number) and 1 (from second). 1 + 1 = 2. No carry-over here.\n\nMillions place: The first number has a 5, the second has a 6. 5 + 6 = 11. So we write 1 and carry over 1.\n\nTen millions place: 4 (first number) and 9 (second). 4 + 9 = 13, plus the carried-over 1 makes 14. So we write 4 and carry over 1 again.\n\nHundred millions place: 7 (first number) and 9 (second). 7 + 9 = 16, plus the carried-over 1 is 17. So that's 7 in this place, carry over 1.\n\nFinally, the billions place: The first number has nothing here (since it's 7 digits?), wait, actually wait, let me check the original numbers again. Wait, 745,138,857 is 9 digits, right? 7 (hundred millions), 4 (ten millions), etc. And 996,137,931 is also 9 digits. So the hundred millions place was handled, and now the next is the billions place. The first number's hundred millions digit is 7, and the second's is 9. Wait, no, actually, let me recount:.\n\nWait, the first number is 745,138,857. Breaking it down:.\n- Billions place: 7 (since 700,000,000).\n- Hundred millions: 4.\n- Ten millions:5.\n- Millions:1.\n- Hundred thousands:3.\n- Ten thousands:8.\n- Thousands:8.\n- Hundreds:5.\n- Tens:7.\n- Ones:7? Wait, maybe I miscounted. Let me write them out with place values:.\n\nFirst number: 7 4 5 1 3 8 8 5 7.\nSecond number:9 9 6 1 3 7 9 3 1.\n\nWait, actually, perhaps I need to write them digit by digit properly. Let me list each digit from right to left (since addition is from the right):.\n\nFirst number: 745,138,857.\nBreaking down each digit from right (ones) to left (billions):.\n\nPosition: 1 (ones): 7.\n2 (tens):5.\n3 (hundreds):8.\n4 (thousands):8.\n5 (ten thousands):3.\n6 (hundred thousands):1.\n7 (millions):5.\n8 (ten millions):4.\n9 (hundred millions):7.\n\nSecond number:996,137,931.\nPositions:.\n\n1 (ones):1.\n2 (tens):3.\n3 (hundreds):9.\n4 (thousands):7.\n5 (ten thousands):3.\n6 (hundred thousands):1.\n7 (millions):6.\n8 (ten millions):9.\n9 (hundred millions):9.\n\nSo adding each column from right to left:.\n\nStarting at position 1 (ones): 7 + 1 = 8. Write 8, carry 0.\n\nPosition 2 (tens):5 +3=8. Write 8, carry 0.\n\nPosition3 (hundreds):8 +9=17. Write 7, carry 1.\n\nPosition4 (thousands):8 +7=15, plus carry 1 →16. Write 6, carry 1.\n\nPosition5 (ten thousands):3 +3=6 +1=7. Write 7, carry 0.\n\nPosition6 (hundred thousands):1 +1=2. Write 2, carry 0.\n\nPosition7 (millions):5 +6=11. Write 1, carry 1.\n\nPosition8 (ten millions):4 +9=13 +1=14. Write 4, carry1.\n\nPosition9 (hundred millions):7 +9=16 +1=17. So here, since we're at the highest digit, we can't carry over beyond that. So we write 7 and carry over 1. But since there's no higher place, that carryover becomes a new digit at the start.\n\nWait a second, that's a problem. The hundred millions place addition gives 17, but since both numbers are 9-digit, their sum could be up to 1,992,276,788 (if adding 999,999,999 + 999,999,999), which is 1,999,999,998, but in this case, adding 745,138,857 and 996,137,931, their sum should be over 1.7 billion, so the sum would be a 10-digit number. .\n\nSo the hundred millions place addition was 7 (from first) +9 (second) plus the carried 1 from the ten millions place. Wait, let me check again:.\n\nWait, the carry-over from position8 (ten millions) was 1, so when we added position9 (hundred millions):.\n\n7 +9 =16, plus the carried 1 is 17. So the digit here is 7, and carryover 1 to the next (billions) place. Since there are no more digits, that carryover becomes the billions place. So the total sum would have a 1 in the billions place, followed by the 7 from the hundred millions, and so on. .\n\nSo putting it all together, the digits from right to left (positions 1-9 plus the new carryover):.\n\nThe carryover from position9 is 1, so the digits are:.\n\nBillions place:1,.\n\nHundred millions:7,.\n\nTen millions:4,.\n\nMillions:1,.\n\nHundred thousands:2,.\n\nTen thousands:7,.\n\nThousands:6,.\n\nHundreds:7,.\n\nTens:8,.\n\nOnes:8.\n\nWait, let me reconstruct the digits step by step. Let me list all the digits with their positions and the carryover.\n\nStarting from the right (position1 to position9, then carryover):.\n\nLet me make a table:.\n\nPosition | Digit1 | Digit2 | Sum | Carryover | Result Digit.\n---|---|---|---|---|---.\n1 (ones) |7 |1 |8 |0 |8.\n2 (tens)|5 |3 |8 |0 |8.\n3 (hundreds)|8 |9 |17 |1 |7.\n4 (thousands)|8 |7 |15+1=16 |1 |6.\n5 (ten thousands)|3 |3 |6+1=7 |0 |7.\n6 (hundred thousands)|1 |1 |2 |0 |2.\n7 (millions)|5 |6 |11 |1 |1.\n8 (ten millions)|4 |9 |13+1=14 |1 |4.\n9 (hundred millions)|7 |9 |16+1=17 |1 |7.\nCarryover after 9: 1.\n\nSo the total sum is 1 (billions) followed by the digits from position9 to 1:.\n\nBillions:1,.\n\nHundred millions:7,.\n\nTen millions:4,.\n\nMillions:1,.\n\nHundred thousands:2,.\n\nTen thousands:7,.\n\nThousands:6,.\n\nHundreds:7,.\n\nTens:8,.\n\nOnes:8. So, let me directly conclude the answer. The answer is "]]
