digit in the billions place (position10) is the carryover from position9, which was 1 (since 8+6 +1=15, so 5 is in position9 (hundred millions), and carryover1 to position10 (billions). Then the ten billions place (position11) comes from any carryover from position10, but since position10 only had the 1 from carryover (no addition there), it's just 1 in position11 and 5 in position10?\n\nWait, no. Let me recast:\n\nAfter adding the hundred millions place (position9), we had:\n\nOriginal digits in position9 (hundred millions):8 and6.\n\nPlus the carryover from position8 (ten millions place addition).\n\nWait, position8 (ten millions) had:\n\nFirst number's ten millions digit is5, second's is4. Adding those gives5+4=9, plus carryover from position7 (millions place). Wait, this is getting too tangled.\n\nAlternatively, let's construct the number digit by digit from the results we have:\n\nStarting from the ones digit (position1):\n\nPosition1:2\n\nPosition2:1\n\nPosition3:0\n\nPosition4:0\n\nPosition5:1\n\nPosition6:2\n\nPosition7:0\n\nPosition8:0\n\nPosition9:5\n\nPlus the carryover to position10:1.\n\nSo the digits are:\n\nPosition10:1\n\nPosition9:5\n\nPosition8:0\n\nPosition7:0\n\nPosition6:2\n\nPosition5:1\n\nPosition4:0\n\nPosition3:0\n\nPosition2:1\n\nPosition1:2\n\nTherefore, writing the number from highest to lowest digit:\n\nPosition10 (billions):1\n\nPosition9 (hundred millions):5\n\nPosition8 (ten millions):0\n\nPosition7 (millions):0\n\nPosition6 (hundred thousands):2\n\nPosition5 (ten thousands):1\n\nPosition4 (thousands):0\n\nPosition3 (hundreds):0\n\nPosition2 (tens):1\n\nPosition1 (ones):2\n\nSo the number is:\n\n1 (billions), 5 (hundred millions), 0 (ten millions), 0 (millions), 2 (hundred thousands), 1 (ten thousands), 0 (thousands), 0 (hundreds), 1 (tens), 2 (ones).\n\nWait, but that would be written as 1,500,210,012. Because:\n\n- Billions:1 \u2192 1,000,000,000\n\n- Hundred millions:5 \u2192 500,000,000 \u2192 total so far:1,500,000,000\n\n- Ten millions:0 \u2192 stays the same\n\n- Millions:0 \u2192 stays\n\n- Hundred thousands:2 \u2192 200,000 \u2192 total now 1,500,200,000\n\n- Ten thousands:1 \u2192 10,000 \u2192 total 1,500,210,000\n\n- Thousands:0 \u2192 same\n\n- Hundreds:0 \u2192 same\n\n- Tens:1 \u2192 10\n\n- Ones:2 \u2192 2.\n\nTotal is 1,500,210,012.\n\nAh! So earlier when I did the first digit-by-digit method, I must have made an error in one of the steps leading to an incorrect digit. The second method where I broke it into millions and smaller parts gave me 1,500,210,012, which aligns with this corrected digit-by-digit approach.\n\nSo the correct answer is 1,500,210,012.\n\nI must have messed up the millions place in my first digit-by-digit calculation. Let me check where I went wrong initially.\n\nIn the first attempt, I think I miscounted the digits in the millions place. When I listed the steps earlier, I said:\n\n\"Millions place:6 and3. 6+3=9 +1 gives 10. So 0 and carryover1.\" That part is correct.\n\nThen ten millions place:5 and4. 5+4=9 +1=10 \u2192 0 and carryover1. Correct.\n\nHundred millions place:8 and6 \u219214 +1=15 \u21925 and carryover1. Correct.\n\nThen the final carryover gives the ten billions digit. So the digits should be:\n\nTen billions:1\n\nBillions:5\n\nHundred millions:0 (from ten millions place result)\n\nWait, no, the hundred millions digit comes from the addition of the two hundred millions digits (8+6) plus the carryover, which was 15, so the hundred millions digit is5, and the carryover to billions is1. So the billions digit is1, and the ten billions is the carryover from the billions? No, the billions digit is the carryover from the hundred millions place addition, which is1. But since the hundred millions place is the 9th digit from the right (hundred millions is the 9th place), adding that gives a digit in the hundred millions (position9) and a carryover to billions (position10). The ten billions would only come into play if the billions place also overflows,