final_output:  ['', '1,499,000,000 + 1,210,012 = 1,500,210,012? Wait, but earlier when I did digit by digit, I had 1,500,209,012. Hmm, so there\'s a discrepancy here. Which one is correct?\n\nWait, maybe I made a mistake in one of the steps. Let me check the smaller parts again. 564,531 + 645,481. Let me add them numerically:\n\n564,531\n\n+645,481\n\n= ?\n\nLet me add digit by digit:\n\nStarting from the right:\n\nOnes: 1 +1 =2\n\nTens: 3 +8 =11 → write 1, carry 1.\n\nHundreds:5 +4 =9 +1 (carry) =10 → write 0, carry 1.\n\nThousands:4 +5 =9 +1 (carry) =10 → write 0, carry 1.\n\nTen thousands:6 (from 564,531\'s ten thousands place is the second digit from the left in 564,531: 5 (hundred thousands), 6 (ten thousands), 4 (thousands). So yes, 6. The other number\'s ten thousands place is 4 (from 645,481: 6 (hundred thousands), 4 (ten thousands),5 (thousands). So 6+4=10, plus carryover 1 makes 11. So write 1, carryover1.\n\nHundred thousands:5 (from first number) +6 (from second number) =11, plus carryover1 =12. So write 2, carryover1.\n\nMillions place: the carryover1. So that\'s 1, so the total is 1,210,012. So that\'s correct.\n\nTherefore adding to the millions parts: 1,499,000,000 +1,210,012 = 1,500,210,012.\n\nBut my initial digit-by-digit addition gave me 1,500,209,012. There\'s a difference of 1,000 here. Hmm. I must have messed up in the first method. Let me retrace the first approach.\n\nOriginal numbers: 856,564,531 and 643,645,481.\n\nLet me write them out with all digits:\n\nFirst number: 8 5 6 5 6 4 5 3 1\n\nSecond number:6 4 3 6 4 5 4 8 1\n\nWait, perhaps I miscounted the digits. Let me check:\n\nWait, 856,564,531:\n\nBreaking down: 8 (hundred millions), 5 (ten millions), 6 (millions), 5 (hundred thousands), 6 (ten thousands), 4 (thousands), 5 (hundreds), 3 (tens), 1 (ones). So total 9 digits.\n\nSimilarly, 643,645,481 is 6 (hundred millions), 4 (ten millions), 3 (millions), 6 (hundred thousands),4 (ten thousands),5 (thousands),4 (hundreds),8 (tens),1 (ones). Also 9 digits.\n\nSo when I did the digit-by-digit addition earlier, maybe I misaligned the columns.\n\nLet me try again step by step, writing each digit\'s place:\n\nLet me align them properly:\n\nDigit positions (from right, starting at 1):\n\nPosition 1 (ones): 1 and 1 → 2\n\nPosition 2 (tens):3 and8 →11 (1, carry1)\n\nPosition3 (hundreds):5 and4 →9 +1=10 (0, carry1)\n\nPosition4 (thousands):4 and5 →9 +1=10 (0, carry1)\n\nPosition5 (ten thousands):6 and4 →10 +1=11 (1, carry1)\n\nPosition6 (hundred thousands):5 and6 →11 +1=12 (2, carry1)\n\nPosition7 (millions):6 and3 →9 +1=10 (0, carry1)\n\nPosition8 (ten millions):5 and4 →9 +1=10 (0, carry1)\n\nPosition9 (hundred millions):8 and6 →14 +1=15 (5, carry1)\n\nThen the carryover 1 to the ten billions place.\n\nSo putting it all together:\n\nStarting from the right:\n\nPosition1:2\n\nPosition2:1\n\nPosition3:0\n\nPosition4:0\n\nPosition5:1\n\nPosition6:2\n\nPosition7:0\n\nPosition8:0\n\nPosition9:5\n\nPlus the carryover 1 at the beginning (ten billions).\n\nSo the number is 1 (ten billions), 5 (billions), 0 (hundred millions), 0 (ten millions), 2 (millions), 0 (hundred thousands), 1 (ten thousands), 0 (thousands), 0 (hundreds), 1 (tens), 2 (ones). Wait, no, let me structure it properly:\n\nWait, the digits from position 9 (hundred millions) to position1 (ones):\n\nWait, the digits from highest to lowest (ten billions down):\n\nThe carryover is 1 in the ten billions place.\n\nThen the next digits:\n\nBillions place (position9\'s result plus carryover):\n\nWait, let me list each digit place with their results:\n\nTen billions: 1\n\nBillions: 5 (from position9\'s 15, so 5 in billions, carryover 1 to ten billions)\n\nHundred millions:0 (from position8\'s result?)\n\nWait, perhaps I need to track each digit step by step.\n\nWait, let me list each position and the digit in the sum:\n\nPositions (starting from the right, 1 to 9):\n\n1:2\n\n2:1 (with carryover 1 to next)\n\n3:0 (carryover1)\n\n4:0 (carryover1)\n\n5:1 (from