corrected ten thousands:\n\nPositions:\n\n1:2\n\n2:6\n\n3:2\n\n4:1\n\n5:7 (ten thousands)\n\n6:0 (hundred thousands, with carryover1)\n\n7:7 (millions:1+5+1=7)\n\n8:0 (ten millions:4+6=10, so 0 and carryover)\n\n9:2 (hundred millions:3+8 +1=12 →2, carryover1)\n\nThen the final carryover1 is the billions digit.\n\nThus the digits from right to left are:\n\n2 (units),\n\n6 (tens),\n\n2 (hundreds),\n\n1 (thousands),\n\n7 (ten thousands),\n\n0 (hundred thousands),\n\n7 (millions),\n\n0 (ten millions),\n\n2 (hundred millions),\n\n1 (billions).\n\nSo arranging them properly:\n\nBillions:1,\n\nHundred millions:2,\n\nTen millions:0,\n\nMillions:7,\n\nHundred thousands:0,\n\nTen thousands:7,\n\nThousands:1,\n\nHundreds:2,\n\nTens:6,\n\nUnits:2.\n\nWait, let me structure the number:\n\nStarting from the highest digit:\n\n1 (billion),\n\n2 (hundred millions),\n\n0 (ten millions),\n\n7 (millions),\n\n0 (hundred thousands),\n\n7 (ten thousands),\n\n1 (thousands),\n\n2 (hundreds),\n\n6 (tens),\n\n2 (units).\n\nWait, but the million's place is the 7th digit from the right, so millions place is the 7 in the millions position. Let me write the number with commas:\n\nBreaking down:\n\nBillions:1,\n\nHundred millions:2,\n\nTen millions:0,\n\nMillions:7,\n\nHundred thousands:0,\n\nTen thousands:7,\n\nThousands:1,\n\nHundreds:2,\n\nTens:6,\n\nUnits:2.\n\nSo the number is:\n\n1,207,071,262.\n\nWhich matches the alternative method's result. So my initial digit-by-digit had an error in position5 (ten thousands place), where I incorrectly added 6 instead of5 for the first number's ten thousands digit, leading to an incorrect 8 instead of7 there, and subsequent errors.\n\nTherefore, the correct sum is 1,207,071,262.\n\nSo the correct answer is 1,207,071,262.\n\nI need to ensure that in the reasoning steps, this mistake is corrected. Let me retrace:\n\nIn the initial reasoning steps, the user (me) made a mistake in the ten thousands place, adding 6 instead of5, leading to an error. So the cor