llions):5 +5=10? Wait, hold on, maybe I messed up here.\n\nWait the first number is 335,653,241. Let's parse each digit properly:.\n\nLet me write both numbers with commas to clarify:.\n\nFirst number: 335,653,241.\nBreaking down each digit from right:.\nPositions (starting at 1 for ones):.\n1:1 (ones).\n2:4 (tens).\n3:2 (hundreds).\n4:3 (thousands).\n5:5 (ten thousands).\n6:6 (hundred thousands).\n7:5 (millions).\n8:3 (ten millions).\n9:3 (hundred millions).\n\nSecond number:151,751,499.\nBreaking down:.\n1:9 (ones).\n2:9 (tens).\n3:4 (hundreds).\n4:1 (thousands).\n5:5 (ten thousands).\n6:7 (hundred thousands).\n7:1 (millions).\n8:5 (ten millions).\n9:1 (hundred millions).\n\nWait, actually, commas separate every three digits. So 335,653,241 is 3 3 5 , 6 5 3 , 2 4 1. So each comma is thousand, million, etc. So the digits are:.\n\nHundred millions: 3 (first digit).\nTen millions:3.\nMillions:5.\nThen comma, hundred thousands:6, ten thousands:5, thousands:3.\nThen comma, hundreds:2, tens:4, ones:1.\n\nWait maybe I need to list each digit's place correctly. Let me write each number as individual digits:.\n\nFirst number: 3 3 5 6 5 3 2 4 1.\n\nWait no, perhaps better to write each digit position:.\n\nLet me count the digits. The first number is 335,653,241. Let's count the digits: 9 digits. So positions from right (1) to left (9):.\n\nDigit 1 (ones): 1.\nDigit 2 (tens):4.\nDigit3 (hundreds):2.\nDigit4 (thousands):3.\nDigit5 (ten thousands):5.\nDigit6 (hundred thousands):6.\nDigit7 (millions):5.\nDigit8 (ten millions):3.\nDigit9 (hundred millions):3.\n\nSecond number:151,751,499. Similarly:.\n\nDigits: 9 digits.\n\nDigit1 (ones):9.\nDigit2 (tens):9.\nDigit3 (hundreds):4.\nDigit4 (thousands):1.\nDigit5 (ten thousands):5.\nDigit6 (hundred thousands):7.\nDigit7 (millions):1.\nDigit8 (ten millions):5.\nDigit9 (hundred millions):1.\n\nNow adding each digit place by place:.\n\nStarting from digit1 (ones):.\n\nDigit1:1 +9 =10 → 0, carryover 1.\n\nDigit2 (tens):4 +9=13 +1=14 →4, carryover1.\n\nDigit3 (hundreds):2 +4=6 +1=7, no carryover.\n\nDigit4 (thousands):3 +1=4. No carryover.\n\nDigit5 (ten thousands):5 +5=10 →0, carryover1.\n\nDigit6 (hundred thousands):6 +7=13 +1=14 →4, carryover1.\n\nDigit7 (millions):5 +1=6 +1=7, no carryover.\n\nDigit8 (ten millions):3 +5=8.\n\nDigit9 (hundred millions):3 +1=4.\n\nPutting all the digits together from digit1 to digit9:.\n\nDigits:.\n\n1 (ones):0.\n\n2 (tens):4.\n\n3 (hundreds):7.\n\n4 (thousands):4.\n\n5 (ten thousands):0.\n\n6 (hundred thousands):4.\n\n7 (millions):7.\n\n8 (ten millions):8.\n\n9 (hundred millions):4.\n\nSo combining all digits: 4 (hundred millions),8 (ten millions),7 (millions), 4 (hundred thousands),0 (ten thousands),4 (thousands),4 (hundreds?), wait no, let me see:.\n\nWait the digits are from digit9 (leftmost) to digit1 (rightmost):.\n\nSo the final number is:.\n\nDigit9:4, Digit8:8, Digit7:7, Digit6:4, Digit5:0, Digit4:4, Digit3:7, Digit2:4, Digit1:0.\n\nWait arranging them in order:.\n\nHundred millions:4,.\n\nTen millions:8,.\n\nMillions:7,.\n\nHundred thousands:4,.\n\nTen thousands:0,.\n\nThousands:4,.\n\nHundreds:7,.\n\nTens:4,.\n\nOnes:0. So, let me directly conclude the answer. The answer is "]]
