 3 2 4 7 (Wait, perhaps I miscounted the digits earlier?)\n\nWait, actually, let's write both numbers with all digits aligned properly:\n\nFirst number: 840,447,190 is:\n\nDigits (from right): positions 1-9:\n\n1:0 (ones)\n\n2:9 (tens)\n\n3:1 (hundreds)\n\n4:7 (thousands)\n\n5:4 (ten thousands)\n\n6:4 (hundred thousands)\n\n7:0 (millions)\n\n8:4 (ten millions)\n\n9:8 (hundred millions)\n\nSecond number:320,583,247:\n\nDigits:\n\n1:7 (ones)\n\n2:4 (tens)\n\n3:2 (hundreds)\n\n4:3 (thousands)\n\n5:8 (ten thousands)\n\n6:5 (hundred thousands)\n\n7:0 (millions)\n\n8:2 (ten millions)\n\n9:3 (hundred millions)\n\nSo adding column by column:\n\nStarting from position 1 (ones):\n\n0 +7 =7. Carry 0.\n\nPosition2 (tens):9 +4=13. Write 3, carry 1.\n\nPosition3 (hundreds):1 +2=3 +1=4. Carry 0.\n\nPosition4 (thousands):7 +3=10. Write 0, carry 1.\n\nPosition5 (ten thousands):4 +8=12 +1=13. Write 3, carry1.\n\nPosition6 (hundred thousands):4 +5=9 +1=10. Write 0, carry1.\n\nPosition7 (millions):0 +0=0 +1=1. Carry0.\n\nPosition8 (ten millions):4 +2=6. Carry0.\n\nPosition9 (hundred millions):8 +3=11. Write 1, carry1.\n\nNow, since there's a carryover of1 after the hundred millions, we need to add a new digit at the billions place:1.\n\nSo the digits from position1 to position10 (billions):\n\nPosition10:1,\n\nPosition9:1,\n\nPosition8:6,\n\nPosition7:1,\n\nPosition6:0,\n\nPosition5:3,\n\nPosition4:0,\n\nPosition3:4,\n\nPosition2:3,\n\nPosition1:7.\n\nThus, the number is 1 (billions), 1 (hundred millions), 6 (ten millions), 1 (millions), 0 (hundred thousands), 3 (ten thousands), 0 (thousands), 4 (hundreds), 3 (tens), 7 (ones).\n\nSo writing with commas: 1,161,030,437. Yes, that matches the earlier result. So the answer is correct.\n\nTherefore, the sum of 840,447,190 and 320,583,247 is 1,161,030,437.\n\n**Final Answer**\nThe sum of 840447190 and 320583247 is \\boxed{1161030437}.\n"]
