ce is 1? Wait, second number's thousands digit: let's see, in the thousands place (Digit4), second number is 1. So first number's thousands digit is 8, second is 1. So 8+1=9 plus the carryover 1 → 10. So 0 with carryover 1.\n\nWait, hold on, I think I messed up in my previous calculation here. Let me go step by step again carefully.\n\nWait, let me list all digits with their positions properly.\n\nFirst number (906,088,842):\n\nDigit1 (Ones):2.\n\nDigit2 (Tens):4.\n\nDigit3 (Hundreds):8.\n\nDigit4 (Thousands):8.\n\nDigit5 (Ten thousands):8.\n\nDigit6 (Hundred thousands):0.\n\nDigit7 (Millions):6.\n\nDigit8 (Ten millions):0.\n\nDigit9 (Hundred millions):9.\n\nSecond number (950,601,906):\n\nDigit1 (Ones):6.\n\nDigit2 (Tens):0.\n\nDigit3 (Hundreds):9.\n\nDigit4 (Thousands):1.\n\nDigit5 (Ten thousands):0.\n\nDigit6 (Hundred thousands):6.\n\nDigit7 (Millions):0.\n\nDigit8 (Ten millions):5.\n\nDigit9 (Hundred millions):9.\n\nNow, adding each column with carryover:\n\nStart with Digit1 (Ones):\n\n2 + 6 = 8. No carryover.\n\nDigit2 (Tens):\n\n4 +0 =4. No carryover.\n\nDigit3 (Hundreds):\n\n8 +9=17 → write 7, carryover 1.\n\nDigit4 (Thousands):\n\n8 +1 =9, plus carryover 1 → 10. So write 0, carryover 1.\n\nDigit5 (Ten thousands):\n\n8 +0 =8, plus carryover 1 →9. Write 9, no carryover.\n\nDigit6 (Hundred thousands):\n\n0 +6=6. Write 6.\n\nDigit7 (Millions):\n\n6 +0=6. Write6.\n\nDigit8 (Ten millions):\n\n0 +5=5. Write5.\n\nDigit9 (Hundred millions):\n\n9 +9=18. Write8, carryover1.\n\nNow, after the hundred millions place, we have the carryover 1. Since there are no more digits, this becomes a new digit (billions place) at the front.\n\nSo putting it all together, from the highest to the lowest digit:\n\nBillions place:1,\n\nHundred millions:8,\n\nTen millions:5,\n\nMillions:6,\n\nHundred thousands:6,\n\nTen thousands:9,\n\nThousands:0,\n\nHundreds:7,\n\nTens:4,\n\nOnes:8.\n\nSo the number is 1,856,690,748.\n\nWait, but let's check this with another method to ensure accuracy.