igits:.\n\nStart from the right:.\n\nDigit 1 (ones): 4.\n\nDigit 2 (tens):5.\n\nDigit 3 (hundreds):8.\n\nDigit 4 (thousands):0 (since the number is ..854, so the thousands place is 0 in the 890,854 part?).\n\nWait 524,890,854:.\n\nLet me separate commas correctly. In the US numbering system, commas are placed every three digits starting from the right. So 524,890,854 would be 524,890,854 → 524 (million), 890 (thousand), 854. So:.\n\nBreaking down each digit:.\n\nBillions place: 5 (since 524 million is part of the billions? Wait no, 524 million is 524,000,000. Wait 524,890,854 is 524 million, 890 thousand, 854. So the digits are:.\n\nDigit positions (from right, starting at 1):.\n\n1:4 (ones).\n\n2:5 (tens).\n\n3:8 (hundreds).\n\n4:0 (thousands).\n\n5:9 (ten thousands).\n\n6:8 (hundred thousands).\n\n7:4 (millions).\n\n8:2 (ten millions).\n\n9:5 (hundred millions).\n\nSo the second number\'s digits:.\n\nPosition 1:4.\n\nPosition2:5.\n\nPosition3:8.\n\nPosition4:0.\n\nPosition5:9.\n\nPosition6:8.\n\nPosition7:4.\n\nPosition8:2.\n\nPosition9:5.\n\nFirst number\'s digits:.\n\nPosition1:6.\n\nPosition2:4.\n\nPosition3:8.\n\nPosition4:7 (thousands place).\n\nPosition5:6 (ten thousands).\n\nPosition6:6 (hundred thousands).\n\nPosition7:1 (millions).\n\nPosition8:1 (ten millions).\n\nPosition9:1 (hundred millions).\n\nNow, adding column by column:.\n\nStarting from position 1 (ones):.\n\n6 +4 =10 → write 0, carryover 1.\n\nPosition2 (tens):.\n\n4 +5 =9 +1=10 → write 0, carryover1.\n\nPosition3 (hundreds):.\n\n8 +8=16 → 6, carryover1.\n\nPosition4 (thousands):.\n\n7 +0=7 +1=8, no carryover.\n\nPosition5 (ten thousands):.\n\n6 +9=15 →5, carryover1.\n\nPosition6 (hundred thousands):.\n\n6 +8=14 +1=15 →5, carryover1.\n\nPosition7 (millions):.\n\n1 +4=5 +1=6, no carryover.\n\nPosition8 (ten millions):.\n\n1 +2=3.\n\nPosition9 (hundred millions):.\n\n1 +5=6.\n\nWait, but wait, let me check each step again.\n\nPosition 1 (ones): 6+4=10, yes.\n\nPosition2 (tens): 4+5=9, plus carryover 1 →10 → 0 and carryover1.\n\nPosition3 (hundreds): first number\'s hundreds digit is 8, second\'s is 8 → 8+8=16, plus carryover 1? Wait no, the carryover from previous was 1, but in position2 we had a carryover of 1, which was added in position2. Wait, the carryover from position2 is 1, but position3 is separate. Wait, when we add position3, the previous carryover is from position2, so yes:.\n\nPosition3: 8 (first number) +8 (second number) + 1 (carryover from position2) → total 17? Wait wait, no:.\n\nWait let me redo the steps carefully:.\n\nStarting over:.\n\nPosition1 (ones):.\n\n6 (first) +4 (second) =10 → write 0, carryover 1.\n\nPosition2 (tens):.\n\n4 +5 =9, plus carryover 1 → total 10 → write 0, carryover1.\n\nPosition3 (hundreds):.\n\n8 (first) +8 (second) =16, plus carryover1 → 17 → write 7, carryover1? Wait wait, no: 8+8 is 16, plus 1 is 17 → so write 7 and carryover1? Wait, but that would mean the hundreds place is 7 with carryover 1. But that contradicts my previous thought.\n\nWait I think I made a mistake here. Let me recast:.\n\nPosition3 (hundreds place):.\n\nFirst number: 8.\n\nSecond number:8 (from the second number\'s hundreds digit).\n\nAdding them: 8+8=16. Then add the carryover from the previous (position2) which was 1. So total 17. So the digit is 7, carryover1 to the next position (thousands place).\n\nSo Position3 gives 7, carryover1.\n\nPosition4 (thousands):.\n\nFirst number\'s thousands digit is 7, second\'s is 0. So 7+0 =7, plus carryover1 → 8. So 8, no carryover.\n\nPosition5 (ten thousands):.\n\nFirst:6, second:9 →6+9=15. No carryover from previous (since position4 had no carryover). So 15 → write5, carryover1.\n\nPosition6 (hundred thousands):.\n\nFirst:6, second:8 →6+8=14, plus carryover1 →15 → write5, carryover1.\n\nPosition7 (millions):.\n\nFirst:1, second:4 →1+4=5, plus carryover1 →6. No carryover.\n\nPosition8 (ten millions):.\n\nFirst:1, second:2 →1+2=3, no carryover.\n\nPosition9 (hundred millions):.\n\nFirst:1, second:5 →