Wait, that's 16, so I need to write 6 here and carry over 1 to the next (ten millions) place.\n\nTen millions place: first number's 1 and second's 2. 1 +2=3, plus the carried 1 makes 4. So 4 here, no carryover.\n\nHundred millions place: first number's 6 and second's 9. 6+9=15. That's 5 in this place and carry over 1 to the billions place.\n\nBillions place: the first number has 0 (since it's a 9-digit number starting with 6 in the hundred millions) and the second has 9. Wait, actually, the first number is 691,814,791 which is 9 digits, so the billions digit is 6. The second number is 927,053,039, which is also 9 digits, so its billions digit is 9. Adding those: 6 +9 =15. But wait, I had a carryover from the hundred millions place. Wait, let me check again.\n\nWait, maybe I messed up the place values. Let me recount the digits properly. Let's list out each digit position:.\n\nStarting from the right (ones place):.\n\nNumber1: 691,814,791.\nBreakdown:.\nBillions: 6 (position 9).\nHundred millions: 9 (position 8).\nTen millions: 1 (position 7).\nMillions: 8 (position 6).\nHundred thousands: 1 (position 5).\nTen thousands:4 (position4).\nThousands:7 (position3).\nHundreds:9 (position2).\nTens:1 (position1).\nOnes: 9? Wait, maybe I'm getting confused here.\n\nWait, perhaps writing them out digit by digit would help. Let me list each digit from right to left (starting with ones):.\n\nNumber1: 691,814,791.\nDigits (right to left, positions 1 to 9):.\nPosition 1 (ones): 1.\nPosition 2 (tens):9.\nPosition3 (hundreds):7.\nPosition4 (thousands):4.\nPosition5 (ten thousands):1.\nPosition6 (hundred thousands):8.\nPosition7 (millions):1.\nPosition8 (ten millions):9.\nPosition9 (hundred millions):6.\n\nWait, that can't be right. Wait, actually, the number 691,814,791 is structured as:.\n\nBillions: 6 (since 6 is the first digit).\nHundred millions: 9 (second digit).\nTen millions:1 (third digit).\nMillions:8 (fourth digit).\nHundred thousands:1 (fifth digit).\nTen thousands:4 (si