messages:  [["Please act as a math teacher and solve the addition problem in the given template.\n####\n# Question:\nWhat is the sum of <<number1>> and <<number2>>?\n# Reasoning:\nLet's add the two numbers digit by digit.\n1. The ones place: <<digit1>>\n2. The tens place: <<digit2>>\n<<other digits>>\nAnswer:\nTherefore, the final computed sum is <<answer>>.\n####\n# Question:\nWhat is the sum of 390791271 and 647909971?\n# Reasoning:", "<think>\nOkay, let's see, I need to add 390,791,271 and 647,909,971. Hmm, adding large numbers can be tricky, but I'll take it step by step. Let me write them down one on top of the other to align the digits properly.\n\nStarting with the ones place: both numbers have a 1 in the ones digit. 1 + 1 is 2. That's straightforward.\n\nMoving to the tens place: the first number has a 7 and the second also has a 7. 7 + 7 is 14. So I write down 4 and carry over 1 to the next column.\n\nNext, the hundreds place: the first number has a 2 and the second has a 9. Adding those gives 11, plus the carried over 1 makes 12. So I put 2 here and carry over another 1.\n\nNow the thousands place: the first number's digit is 1 and the second's is 9. 1 + 9 is 10, plus the carryover is 11. Write 1 and carry over 1 again.\n\nTens of thousands place: the first number has 9 and the second has 0. 9 + 0 is 9, plus the 1 makes 10. So 0 here with a carryover of 1.\n\nHundreds of thousands place: 7 (from first number) and 0 (second). 7 + 0 is 7, plus the 1 is 8. No carryover here.\n\nMillions place: first number has 0 and second has 9. 0 + 9 =9. No carryover.\n\nTen millions place: first number is 9, second is 7. 9+7=16. So write 6 and carryover 1.\n\nHundred millions place: first is 3 and second is 4. 3+4=7 plus the 1 makes 8.\n\nWait, let me check again. Maybe I missed a digit. Let me count the digits again. 390,791,271 is 9 digits, 647,909,971 is also 9 digits. Let me write them out:.\n\n3 9 0 7 9 1 2 7 1.\n\n6 4 7 9 0 9 9 7 1.\n\nStarting from the right:.\n\nOnes: 1+1=2.\n\nTens:7+7=14, carryover 1.\n\nHundreds:2+9=11 +1=12, carryover 1.\n\nThousands:1+9=10 +1=11, carryover 1.\n\nTen thousands:9 (from first number) +0 (second)=9+1=10? Wait, the ten thousands place: first number's digit is the 5th from the right. Let me see:.\n\nBreaking down 390,791,271:.\n\nDigits: 3 (hundred millions), 9 (ten millions), 0 (millions), 7 (hundred thousands), 9 (ten thousands), 1 (thousands), 2 (hundreds), 7 (tens), 1 (ones).\n\nSimilarly, 647,909,971:.\n\n6 (hundred millions),4 (ten millions),7 (millions),9 (hundred thousands),0 (ten thousands),9 (thousands),9 (hundreds),7 (tens),1 (ones).\n\nSo when adding the ten thousands place: first number has 9, second has 0. So 9+0=9 plus carryover 1 makes 10. So we write 0 and carryover 1.\n\nThen hundred thousands place: first is 7, second is9. 7+9=16, plus carryover 1 is 17. Wait, hold on, I might have messed up earlier steps. Let me redo this step by step properly.\n\nLet me align them properly:.\n\n          3 9 0 7 9 1 2 7 1.\n\n        + 6 4 7 9 0 9 9 7 1.\n\n        -------------------------.\n\nStarting from the rightmost digit (ones):.\n\nOnes place: 1 + 1 = 2. Write 2.\n\nTens place: 7 + 7 = 14. Write 4, carryover 1.\n\nHundreds place: 2 + 9 = 11, plus 1 = 12. Write 2, carryover 1.\n\nThousands place: 1 + 9 = 10, plus 1 = 11. Write 1, carryover 1.\n\nTen thousands place: 9 (from first number) + 0 (second number) =9 + 1 (carryover) = 10. Write 0, carryover 1.\n\nHundred thousands place: 7 (first) +9 (second) =16, plus 1 =17. Write 7, carryover 1.\n\nMillions place: 0 (first) +7 (second) =7 +1 =8. Write 8, no carryover.\n\nTen millions place:9 (first) +4 (second) =13. Write 3, carryover 1.\n\nHundred millions place:3 (first) +6 (second) =9, plus 1 carried over =10. So write 0 and carryover 1. But since there's no higher place, that 1 is added as a new digit at the beginning.\n\nWait, so putting it all together, the sum would be:.\n\nStarting from the right, the digits are:.\n\nOnes:2.\n\nT