number's thousands digit is the 4th from the right: let's see:.\n\nFirst number: digits are (from right):.\n\nPosition1:1 (ones).\n\n2:7 (tens).\n\n3:5 (hundreds).\n\n4:9 (thousands).\n\n5:0 (ten thousands).\n\n6:6 (hundred thousands).\n\n7:3 (millions).\n\n8:2 (ten millions).\n\n9:8 (hundred millions).\n\nSecond number:.\n\nPosition1:0.\n\n2:3.\n\n3:0 (hundreds place).\n\nWait, second number digits:.\n\nFrom right:.\n\nPosition1:0.\n\n2:3 (tens).\n\n3:0 (hundreds).\n\n4:8 (thousands).\n\n5:8 (ten thousands).\n\n6:1 (hundred thousands).\n\n7:7 (millions).\n\n8:1 (ten millions).\n\n9:9 (hundred millions).\n\nSo for thousands place (position4):.\n\nFirst number:9, second:8 → 9+8=17. So 7, carryover1.\n\nWait, so I made a mistake earlier when I thought the thousands digit of the second number was 8. So, yes, the thousands digits are 9 and 8, adding to 17. So that's 7 in thousands place, carryover1.\n\nContinuing.\n\nDigit4 (thousands place):.\n\n9 +8 =17, plus carryover 0 (wait, carryover from previous digit was from hundreds place which had no carryover). Wait, let's retrace.\n\nWait, after hundreds place (digit3) we had a sum of 6, no carryover. So the thousands place (digit4):.\n\nFirst number's digit4:9, second's digit4:8 →9+8=17. So write 7, carryover1.\n\nSo now, moving to ten thousands place (digit5):.\n\nFirst number's digit5:0, second's digit5:8 →0+8=8, plus carryover1 =9. So 9, no carryover.\n\nNext, hundred thousands place (digit6):.\n\nFirst number:6, second:1 →6+1=7, plus 0 →7.\n\nMillions place (digit7):.\n\nFirst number:3, second:7 →3+7=10. So write 0, carryover1.\n\nTen millions place (digit8):.\n\nFirst number:2, second:1 →2+1=3, plus carryover1 →4. No carryover.\n\nHundred millions place (digit9):.\n\nFirst number:8, second:9 →8+9=17. So write 7 and carryover1. But since there are no more digits, that carryover becomes a new digit at the beginning.\n\nSo putting it all together:.\n\nStarting from the right:.\n\nDigit1:1.\n\nDigit2:0.\n\nDigit3:6.\n\nD