rst) +9 (second) =15 → write 5, carryover1.\n\nPosition9 (hundred millions):1 (first) +8 (second) =9, plus the carried over 1 makes 10. So write 0 and carryover1 to the billions place.\n\nSince there's a carryover here, we have a new digit in the billions place:1.\n\nTherefore, the total digits are:.\n\nBillions place:1.\n\nHundred millions:0 (from the 10 in the hundred millions calculation, so 0 with carryover).\n\nWait, let me retrace:.\n\nStarting from position9 (hundred millions):.\n\n1 (from first) +8 (second) =9, plus carryover 1 from position8 (ten millions) gives 10. So hundred millions digit is 0, carryover1 to billions.\n\nThen, the billions place: the carryover1, since both numbers have 0 in billions place (since they are 9-digit numbers). So total billions digit is 1.\n\nPutting all digits together from highest to lowest:.\n\nBillions:1.\n\nHundred millions:0 (from the 10).\n\nTen millions:5 (from position8:6+9+carryover? Wait let me recheck:.\n\nWait, position8 (ten millions):.\n\nFirst number has 6, second has9. 6+9=15, so write 5, carryover1. So the ten millions digit is5, and the carryover1 goes to the hundred millions place.\n\nThen, the hundred millions place calculation (position9):.\n\n1 (first) +8 (second) =9, plus the carryover1 from ten millions gives 10. So hundred millions digit is0, carryover1 to billions.\n\nThus, the digits are:.\n\nBillions:1 (from carryover).\n\nHundred millions:0.\n\nTen millions:5.\n\nMillions:4 (from position7:3+1=4).\n\nHundred thousands:9 (from position6:1+8=9).\n\nTen thousands:5 (position5:2+3=5).\n\nThousands:2 (position4:0+2=2).\n\nHundreds:2 (position3:0+2=2).\n\nTens:8 (position2:3+5=8).\n\nOnes:4 (position1:4+0=4).\n\nSo putting them all together:.\n\n1 (billions), 0 (hundred millions), 5 (ten millions), 4 (millions), 9 (hundred thousands), 5 (ten thousands), 2 (thousands), 2 (hundreds), 8 (tens), 4 (ones).\n\nWait, let me write this out with commas:.\n\nStarting from the billions:.\n\n1,0 (hundred millions)