place (billions due to the carryover):\n\n1 (carryover) followed by:\n\nHundred millions place:1 (from 2+9 +0 carry? Wait, no, the 2+9 in the hundred millions place was 11, so the digit there is 1, and the carryover added a 1 in the billions place.\n\nSo the digits are:\n\nBillions:1,\n\nHundred millions:1,\n\nTen millions:5,\n\nMillions:1,\n\nHundred thousands:7,\n\nTen thousands:3,\n\nThousands:6,\n\nHundreds:7,\n\nTens:0,\n\nOnes:1.\n\nSo putting it all together: 1,151,736,701.\n\nWait let me confirm with another method.\n\nAlternatively, adding the two numbers:\n\n215,890,309\n\n+935,846,392\n\nLet me add them:\n\n215,890,309\n\n+935,846,392\n\n= (200,000,000 + 15,000,000 + 890,309) + (900,000,000 + 35,846,392)\n\n= 200M +900M = 1,100,000,000.\n\n15M +35,846,392 = 15,000,000 +35,846,392 =50,846,392.\n\nThen adding 890,309 to that: 50,846,392 +890,309 =51,736,701.\n\nThen total sum is 1,100,000,000 +51,736,701 =1,151,736,701.\n\nYes, so that matches. So the final answer is 1,151,736,701.\n\nTherefore, the reasoning steps should follow that digit-by-digit addition, making sure each step's carry is accounted for, leading to the final sum with the carryover adding an extra digit at the front.\n"]
