yover 1.\n\nHundred-thousands place: first number has 0 (since ..099,234: the sixth digit from right is 0?), let me check:.\n\nWait, maybe I need to write both numbers digit by digit:.\n\nFirst number: 9 6 7 0 9 9 2 3 4.\n\nWait, let's count the digits properly. 967,099,234 is 9 digits:.\n\nBreaking down:.\n\nDigit positions (from right, starting at 1):.\n\n1 (ones):4.\n\n2 (tens):3.\n\n3 (hundreds):2.\n\n4 (thousands):9.\n\n5 (ten thousands):9.\n\n6 (hundred thousands):0.\n\n7 (millions):7.\n\n8 (ten millions):6.\n\n9 (hundred millions):9.\n\nSecond number:125,931,556 is also 9 digits:.\n\nDigits from right:.\n\n1:6.\n\n2:5.\n\n3:5.\n\n4:1.\n\n5:3.\n\n6:9.\n\n7:5.\n\n8:2.\n\n9:1.\n\nWait, so let me list each digit position for both numbers:.\n\nPosition 1 (ones):.\n\nFirst:4, second:6 → 4+6=10 → 0, carryover1.\n\nPosition2 (tens):.\n\nFirst:3, second:5 →3+5=8 +1=9 →9, no carry.\n\nPosition3 (hundreds):.\n\nFirst:2, second:5 →2+5=7 →7, no carry.\n\nPosition4 (thousands):.\n\nFirst:9, second:1 →9+1=10 →0, carryover1.\n\nPosition5 (ten thousands):.\n\nFirst:9, second:3 →9+3=12 +1=13 →3, carryover1.\n\nPosition6 (hundred thousands):.\n\nFirst:0, second:9 →0+9=9 +1=10 →0, carryover1.\n\nPosition7 (millions):.\n\nFirst:7, second:5 →7+5=12 +1=13 →3, carryover1.\n\nPosition8 (ten millions):.\n\nFirst:6, second:2 →6+2=8 +1=9 →9, no carry.\n\nPosition9 (hundred millions):.\n\nFirst:9, second:1 →9+1=10 →0, carryover1.\n\nNow, the carryover1 from the hundred millions place becomes the next digit (billions place), so we have 1 there.\n\nPutting all together from highest to lowest:.\n\nBillions place:1.\n\nHundred millions:0.\n\nTen millions:9.\n\nMillions:3.\n\nHundred thousands:0.\n\nTen thousands:3.\n\nThousands:0.\n\nHundreds:7.\n\nTens:9.\n\nOnes:0. So, let me directly conclude the answer. The answer is "]]
