:0 (ten thousands)\n6:4 (hundred thousands)\n7:5 (millions)\n8:0 (ten millions)\n9:5 (hundred millions)\n\nWait, actually, the second number is 505,400,045. Let\'s see:\n\n505,400,045:\n\nBreaking down each digit:\n\nStarting from the right:\n\n1:5 (ones)\n2:4 (tens)\n3:0 (hundreds)\n4:0 (thousands)\n5:0 (ten thousands)\n6:4 (hundred thousands)\n7:5 (millions)\n8:0 (ten millions)\n9:5 (hundred millions)\n\nWait, but 505,400,045 has digits as follows:\n\nHundred millions place:5\n\nTen millions:0\n\nMillions:5\n\nHundred thousands:4\n\nTen thousands:0\n\nThousands:0\n\nHundreds:0\n\nTens:4\n\nOnes:5\n\nHmm, maybe my breakdown is off. Let me count:\n\nThe number is 505,400,045.\n\nBreaking into digits:\n\nDigit positions (from right, starting at 1):\n\nPosition 1:5 (ones)\n\nPosition 2:4 (tens)\n\nPosition3:0 (hundreds)\n\nPosition4:0 (thousands)\n\nPosition5:0 (ten thousands)\n\nPosition6:4 (hundred thousands)\n\nPosition7:5 (millions)\n\nPosition8:0 (ten millions)\n\nPosition9:5 (hundred millions)\n\nYes, that\'s correct.\n\nNow the first number is 795,428,473:\n\nDigit positions (from right):\n\nPosition1:3 (ones)\n\nPosition2:7 (tens)\n\nPosition3:4 (hundreds)\n\nPosition4:8 (thousands)\n\nPosition5:2 (ten thousands)\n\nPosition6:4 (hundred thousands)\n\nPosition7:5 (millions)\n\nPosition8:9 (ten millions)\n\nPosition9:7 (hundred millions)\n\nSo when adding, we need to add each corresponding position:\n\nStarting from position1 to position9.\n\nLet me do each step carefully.\n\nPosition1 (Ones):\n\n3 (first number) +5 (second)=8. Write down 8. Carryover 0.\n\nPosition2 (Tens):\n\n7 +4 =11. Write 1, carryover 1.\n\nPosition3 (Hundreds):\n\n4 +0 +1 (carryover)=5. Write 5. Carryover 0.\n\nPosition4 (Thousands):\n\n8 +0=8. Write 8. Carryover 0.\n\nPosition5 (Ten thousands):\n\n2 +0=2. Write 2. Carryover 0.\n\nWait wait, hold on. Wait in the first number\'s position5 is 2 (ten thousands place), and the second number\'s position5 is 0 (ten thousands place). So 2 +0=2. B