xt is the ten millions place. The first number has a 6, and the second has a 9. 6 + 9 = 15. That's another two-digit number, so I write down 5 and carry over 1.\n\nFinally, the hundred millions place. The first number has a 6, and the second has a 6. Adding those gives 12, plus the carried over 1 makes 13. So I write 3 here and carry over 1 to the next place, which is the billions place. Since there's nothing beyond that, the carried 1 becomes the billions digit.\n\nPutting it all together, the sum should be 1,325,002,791. Wait, let me double-check the digits to make sure I didn't miscount any places. Starting from the right again:.\n\nOnes:1+0=1.\n\nTens:4+5=9.\n\nHundreds:3+4=7.\n\nThousands:4+8=12 (carry 1).\n\nTen thousands:7+2+1=10 (carry 1).\n\nHundred thousands:3+6+1=10 (carry1).\n\nMillions:2+3+1=6.\n\nTen millions:6+9=15 (carry1).\n\nHundred millions:6+6+1=13 (carry1).\n\nBillions:1 (from carryover). Wait, but the original numbers are both 9-digit numbers. So adding them gives a 10-digit number? Let me check the billions place again. .\n\nWait, the first number is 632,374,341. So the digits are:.\n\nBillions place: 6 (since it's 6 hundred million), so the first digit is 6. The second number is 693,628,450, so its first digit is 6 (6 hundred million). So adding the hundred millions: 6 + 6 = 12, plus the carried 1 from the ten millions (which was 15, so carryover 1 there). Wait, let's retrace the steps again carefully:.\n\nLet me write down each digit position step by step:.\n\nLet me list both numbers with their digits separated by commas for clarity:.\n\nFirst number: 6,323,74,341 → Wait, actually, commas are placed every three digits. Let me parse each number properly:.\n\n632,374,341:.\n\n- Billions: 6 (since 600,000,000).\n- Hundred millions: 3 (30,000,000).\n- Ten millions: 2 (2,000,000).\n- Millions: 3 (300,000).\n- Hundred thousands: 7 (70,000).\n- Ten thousands: 4 (4,000).\n- Thousands: 3 (300).\n- Hundreds: 4 (40).\n- Tens: 3 (3).\n- Ones: 1 (1).\n\