illions (8).\n\nDigit 3: Millions (3).\n\nDigit 4: Hundred thousands (1).\n\nDigit 5: Ten thousands (3).\n\nDigit 6: Thousands (1).\n\nDigit 7: Hundreds (2).\n\nDigit 8: Tens (2).\n\nDigit 9: Ones (6).\n\nSecond number (914,551,499):.\n\nDigit 1: Hundred millions (9).\n\nDigit 2: Ten millions (1).\n\nDigit 3: Millions (4).\n\nDigit 4: Hundred thousands (5).\n\nDigit 5: Ten thousands (5).\n\nDigit 6: Thousands (1).\n\nDigit 7: Hundreds (4).\n\nDigit 8: Tens (9).\n\nDigit 9: Ones (9).\n\nSo when adding, let's go digit by digit from right (ones) to left (hundred millions):.\n\nPosition 9 (Ones): 6 +9 =15 → 5, carryover 1.\n\nPosition8 (Tens): 2 +9 =11 +1=12 → 2, carryover1.\n\nPosition7 (Hundreds):2 +4 =6 +1=7 →7, no carryover.\n\nPosition6 (Thousands):1 (from first) +1 (from second)=2 →2.\n\nPosition5 (Ten thousands):3 (first) +5 (second)=8 →8.\n\nPosition4 (Hundred thousands):1 (first) +5 (second)=6 →6.\n\nPosition3 (Millions):3 (first) +4 (second)=7 →7.\n\nPosition2 (Ten millions):8 (first) +1 (second)=9 →9.\n\nPosition1 (Hundred millions):4 (first) +9 (second)=13 →13. Wait, so here, since it's the highest place, we can just write 13. So the total sum would be 13 in the hundred millions place plus the rest.\n\nWait, but when adding the hundred millions place (position1), 4 +9 =13. So the total sum is 1,3 followed by the other digits?.\n\nWait, let's see:.\n\nStarting from the right, the digits after each step are:.\n\nPosition9: 5.\n\nPosition8:2.\n\nPosition7:7.\n\nPosition6:2.\n\nPosition5:8.\n\nPosition4:6.\n\nPosition3:7.\n\nPosition2:9 (from 8+1=9?).\n\nWait, let me redo the positions properly, maybe I messed up the order.\n\nWait, perhaps I should write the numbers vertically aligned:.\n\n          4 8 3 1 3 1 2 2 6.\n\n        + 9 1 4 5 5 1 4 9 9.\n\n        -------------------------.\n\nStarting from the right (ones place):.\n\nOnes:6 +9=15 →5, carryover1.\n\nTens:2 +9=11 +1=12 →2, carryover1.\n\nHundreds:2 +4=6 +1=7 →7, carryover0.\n\nThousands:1 (from first number's thousands place) +1 (second's thousands)=2 →2.\n\nTen thousands:3 +5=8 →8.\n\nHundred thousands:1 +5=6 →6.\n\nMillions:3 (first's millions) +4 (second's millions)=7 →7.\n\nTen millions:8 (first's ten millions) +1 (second's ten millions)=9 →9.\n\nHundred millions:4 +9=13 → here, since it's the highest digit, the 13 would mean we have to carryover to a new digit (billions place). So 13 in the hundred millions would be 1 (carryover to billions) and 3 in the hundred millions. So the total sum would be 1 (billions) 3 (hundred millions) followed by the other digits.\n\nSo putting it all together:.\n\nBillions place: 1 (from the carryover of 13).\n\nHundred millions: 3 (from 13-10).\n\nThen the rest:.\n\nTen millions:9.\n\nMillions:7.\n\nHundred thousands:6.\n\nTen thousands:8.\n\nThousands:2.\n\nHundreds:7.\n\nTens:2.\n\nOnes:5. So, let me directly conclude the answer. The answer is "]]
