ver1.\n\nHundreds place:\n\n1 (first) +1 (second) +1 (carry) =3? Wait, first's hundreds is 1, second's hundreds is 1? Wait second number's hundreds digit is 1? Let me check:\n\nSecond number's digits:\n\nStarting from the right (ones):\n\nPosition1:8 (ones)\n\nPosition2:5 (tens)\n\nPosition3:1 (hundreds)\n\nYes, so the hundreds digit is 1. So adding first's hundreds (1) + second's (1) =2, plus carryover1 makes 3. So hundreds digit is 3, carryover 0.\n\nWait, that contradicts my earlier thought. So maybe I made a mistake in the hundreds place earlier.\n\nWait let's re-express:\n\nHundreds place: first number has 1, second has1. So 1+1=2, plus the previous carryover of 1 (from the tens place) makes 3. So total 3 in hundreds place, no carryover.\n\nMoving to thousands place:\n\nFirst number:3 (thousands place)\n\nSecond number:6 (thousands place)\n\n3 +6 =9, plus no carryover →9. So thousands digit is 9, no carryover.\n\nTen thousands place:\n\nFirst number:4 (ten thousands)\n\nSecond number:7 (ten thousands)\n\n4 +7=11. So write 1, carryover1.\n\nHundred thousands place:\n\nFirst number:2 (hundred thousands)\n\nSecond number:3 (hundred thousands)\n\n2 +3=5 plus carryover1 →6. So 6, no carryover.\n\nMillions place:\n\nFirst number:7 (millions)\n\nSecond number:9 (millions)\n\n7 +9=16 →6, carryover1.\n\nTen millions place:\n\nFirst number:3 (ten millions)\n\nSecond number:3 (ten millions)\n\n3 +3=6 plus carryover1 →7. So 7, no carryover.\n\nHundred millions place:\n\nFirst number:9 (hundred millions)\n\nSecond number:4 (hundred millions)\n\n9 +4=13. So write 3, carryover1.\n\nNow, since there are no more digits, the carryover1 becomes the next digit (billions place), so 1.\n\nPutting all together from right to left:\n\nStarting with ones digit:0\n\nTens:1\n\nHundreds:3\n\nThousands:9\n\nTen thousands:1\n\nHundred thousands:6\n\nMillions:6\n\nTen millions:7\n\nHundred millions:3\n\nBillions:1\n\nSo the total is 1,376,619,310?\n\nWait let me write the digits step by step: