ousands, thousands, hundreds, tens, ones.\n\nWait, actually, the standard place values are:.\n\nStarting from the right:.\n\n1 (ones), 2 (tens), 3 (hundreds), 4 (thousands), 5 (ten thousands), 6 (hundred thousands), 7 (millions), 8 (ten millions), 9 (hundred millions).\n\nSo the digits of the sum, starting from the rightmost (ones) to left (hundred millions):.\n\nThe digits we computed are:.\n\nPosition 1 (ones):9.\n\n2 (tens):1.\n\n3 (hundreds):5.\n\n4 (thousands):5.\n\n5 (ten thousands):1.\n\n6 (hundred thousands):9.\n\n7 (millions):2.\n\n8 (ten millions):2.\n\n9 (hundred millions):5.\n\nSo putting them in order from hundred millions down:.\n\n5 (hundred millions), 2 (ten millions), 2 (millions), 9 (hundred thousands), 1 (ten thousands),5 (thousands),5 (hundreds),1 (tens),9 (ones).\n\nWait, that would make the number:.\n\n5 (hundred millions) 2 (ten millions) 2 (millions) → 522, then next three digits are 9 (hundred thousands), 1 (ten thousands),5 (thousands) → 915, then 5 (hundreds),1 (tens),9 (ones) → 519. So, let me directly conclude the answer. The answer is "]]
