:.\n   0 +7 =7. Write7, carryover0.\n\n8. Position8 (ten millions place):.\n   7 +3 =10. Write0, carryover1.\n\n9. Position9 (hundred millions place):.\n   5 +8 =13, plus carryover1 is14. So write 4, carryover1.\n\nNow, after position9, there's still a carryover of1. Since there are no more digits, this carryover becomes a new digit at the beginning. So the total sum is:.\n\n1 (carryover) followed by the digits from position9 to position1:.\n\nWait, let me list all the written digits from position1 to position9, then the carryover.\n\nWait the digits written from position1 to 9 are:.\n\nPosition1:1.\n\nPosition2:0.\n\nPosition3:1.\n\nPosition4:7.\n\nPosition5:5.\n\nPosition6:9.\n\nPosition7:7.\n\nPosition8:0.\n\nPosition9:4.\n\nThen the carryover1 is added as the next digit.\n\nSo the digits from left to right would be:.\n\n1 (the carryover) then position9's digit (4), position8's digit (0), position7's (7), position6's (9), position5's (5), position4's (7), position3's (1), position2's (0), position1's (1). Wait, but when you write numbers, the highest place is first. So the carryover is the highest digit.\n\nSo the digits are:.\n\nCarryover1 (billions place?), then the previous highest digit (position9 was hundred millions, so adding the carryover makes it billions now.\n\nSo putting it all together:.\n\n1 (billion place) 4 (hundred millions), 0 (ten millions), 7 (millions), 9 (hundred thousands), 5 (ten thousands), 7 (thousands), 1 (hundred), 0 (tens), 1 (ones).\n\nWait, let me see:.\n\nWait the written digits from position9 to 1 (excluding the carryover) were:.\n\nPosition9:4 (originally hundred millions, now becomes ten millions because of the carryover?), no, perhaps I need to think in terms of the digit positions.\n\nAlternatively, maybe the final digits are:.\n\nThe first digit (from the carryover) is 1, then the position9 result was 4, so the digits are 1,4, then the position8's 0, position7's7, etc.\n\nWait perhaps the correct way is:.\n\nAfter adding, the