, carryover1.\n\nTens:5+7=12 +1=13. 3, carryover1.\n\nHundreds:9+7=16 +1=17. 7, carryover1.\n\nThousands:4+9=13 +1=14. 4, carryover1.\n\nTen thousands:6+7=13 +1=14. 4, carryover1.\n\nHundred thousands:0 +5=5 +1=6.\n\nSo total: 644,734.\n\nWait, so 64,957 +579,777 = 644,734?.\n\nWait let me compute:\n\n64,957 +579,777:\n\n64,957 +500,000 =564,957.\n\nThen +79,777:\n\n564,957 +79,777 = 644,734. Yes, that's correct.\n\nSo total sum is 1,067,000,000 + 644,734 = 1,067,644,734? Wait, but earlier we had 1,067,646,744.\n\nHmm, discrepancy here. Wait, so there's an error somewhere.\n\nWait, perhaps my first method was wrong. Let me check again.\n\nWait in the first method, the total was 1,067,646,744, but adding the millions and the remaining parts gives 1,067,644,734. So there's a difference of 2,010? Hmm, so I must have made a mistake in the first step-by-step addition.\n\nLet me go back to the digit-by-digit addition and check again.\n\nLet me retrace the steps carefully:\n\nPositions (from right to left):\n\nPosition1 (Ones):\n\n7 +7 =14 → 4, carryover1.\n\nPosition2 (Tens):\n\n5 (first) +7 (second)=12 +1=13 → 3, carryover1.\n\nPosition3 (Hundreds):\n\n9 (first) +7 (second)=16 +1=17 →7, carryover1.\n\nPosition4 (Thousands):\n\nFirst number:4, second:9 →4+9=13 +1=14 →4, carryover1.\n\nPosition5 (Ten thousands):\n\nFirst number:6, second:7 →6+7=13 +1=14 →4, carryover1.\n\nPosition6 (Hundred thousands):\n\nFirst number:0, second:5 →0+5=5 +1=6 →6, no carryover.\n\nPosition7 (Millions):\n\nFirst number:6, second:1 →6+1=7, no carryover.\n\nPosition8 (Ten millions):\n\nFirst number:3, second:3 →3+3=6, no carryover.\n\nPosition9 (Hundred millions):\n\nFirst number:4, second:6 →4+6=10 →0, carryover1.\n\nThen the carryover1 becomes the billions place:1.\n\nSo the digits from left to right (billions to ones):\n\n1 (billions), 0 (hundred millions),6 (ten millions),7 (millions),6 (hundred thousands),4 (ten thousands),4 (thousands),7 (hundreds),3 (tens),4 (ones).\n\nSo the number is 1