to left (positions 1 to 9):.\n\nPosition 1 (ones): 2.\n\nPosition 2 (tens): 8.\n\nPosition 3 (hundreds): 3.\n\nPosition 4 (thousands): 1.\n\nPosition 5 (ten thousands): 1.\n\nPosition 6 (hundred thousands): 5? Wait no, let's parse the number properly.\n\nWait 557,511,382:.\n\nBreaking into parts:.\n\n5 (hundred millions), 5 (ten millions), 7 (millions), 5 (hundred thousands?), wait let's see:.\n\nWait the number is 557,511,382. Let's split by commas:.\n\n557,511,382 → 557,511,382.\n\nSo:.\n\nFirst part: 557 (hundred millions, ten millions, millions).\n\nThen 511 (hundred thousands, ten thousands, thousands).\n\nThen 382 (hundreds, tens, ones).\n\nSo:.\n\nHundred millions: 5.\n\nTen millions: 5.\n\nMillions:7.\n\nHundred thousands:5.\n\nTen thousands:1.\n\nThousands:1.\n\nHundreds:3.\n\nTens:8.\n\nOnes:2.\n\nAh! So my mistake earlier was in the hundred thousands place. The second number (557,511,382) has 5 in the hundred thousands place, not 1. Because the breakdown after the first comma is 511, which is 5 (hundred thousands), 1 (ten thousands), 1 (thousands). So that's where I messed up earlier.\n\nSo correcting that:.\n\nLet me redo the digit positions properly for both numbers:.\n\nFirst number: 320,638,116.\n\nBreaking down:.\n\nHundred millions: 3.\n\nTen millions: 2.\n\nMillions:0.\n\nHundred thousands:6.\n\nTen thousands:3.\n\nThousands:8.\n\nHundreds:1.\n\nTens:1.\n\nOnes:6.\n\nSecond number:557,511,382.\n\nHundred millions:5.\n\nTen millions:5.\n\nMillions:7.\n\nHundred thousands:5 (since the 5 in the next segment after the comma is the hundred thousands place).\n\nTen thousands:1.\n\nThousands:1.\n\nHundreds:3.\n\nTens:8.\n\nOnes:2.\n\nNow adding each column:.\n\nStarting from the ones (rightmost):.\n\nOnes: 6 + 2 = 8. Write 8.\n\nTens: 1 + 8 = 9. Write 9.\n\nHundreds:1 +3 =4. Write4.\n\nThousands:8 +1=9. Write9.\n\nTen thousands:3 +1=4. Write4.\n\nHundred thousands:6 +5=11. Write 1 and carry over 1.\n\nMillions:0 +7 +1 (carry) =8. Write8.\n\nTen millions:2