y) = 3. No carryover here.\n\nTen millions place: 8 (first) + 8 (second) = 16. Wait, the first number's ten millions place is 8, second's is 8? Wait, let me check again:.\n\nWait, in the first number, the digits are:.\n\nPositions (from right, starting at 1):.\n\n1: 0 (ones).\n2:4 (tens).\n3:5 (hundreds).\n4:9 (thousands).\n5:3 (ten thousands).\n6:9 (hundred thousands).\n7:2 (millions).\n8:8 (ten millions).\n9:8 (hundred millions).\n\nSecond number:.\n\nPositions:.\n\n1:0.\n2:0.\n3:0.\n4:0 (thousands).\n5:7 (ten thousands).\n6:6 (hundred thousands).\n7:0 (millions).\n8:8 (ten millions).\n9:1 (hundred millions).\n\nWait, so for the ten millions place, first number has 8 (position 8), second has 8 (position 8). So adding those: 8 +8 = 16. But we had a carryover from the hundred thousands place? Wait, let's retrace step by step.\n\nLet me go step by step again, carefully:.\n\nStarting from the rightmost digit (ones place):.\n\nOnes: 0 + 0 = 0. Write 0.\n\nTens: 4 + 0 =4. Write 4.\n\nHundreds:5+0=5. Write5.\n\nThousands:9 (from first number's thousands place) +0 (second's thousands) =9. Write9.\n\nTen thousands:3 (first's ten thousands) +7 (second's ten thousands)=10. So write 0, carryover 1.\n\nHundred thousands:9 (first's hundred thousands) +6 (second's hundred thousands) +1 (carryover) = 16. So write 6, carryover 1.\n\nMillions place:2 (first's millions) +0 (second's millions) +1 (carryover) =3. Write3, no carryover.\n\nTen millions place:8 (first's ten millions) +8 (second's ten millions) =16. Wait, so here, 8+8=16. But there was no carryover from the previous step (since millions place didn't have a carryover). So 16 here. So write 6 and carryover 1.\n\nHundred millions place:8 (first) +1 (second's hundred millions) +1 (carryover from ten millions) =10. So write 0 and carryover 1.\n\nBut since there are no more digits, that carryover 1 becomes a new digit at the billions place. .\n\nPutting it all together:.\n\nStarting from the ones:.\n\n0 (ones).\n\n4 (tens).\n\n