8.\n\nBreaking down each digit (from right to left):.\n\nPosition: 1: 8 (ones).\n\n2: 6 (tens).\n\n3: 0 (hundreds).\n\n4: 4 (thousands).\n\n5: 0 (ten thousands).\n\n6: 3 (hundred thousands).\n\n7: 2 (millions).\n\n8: 8 (ten millions).\n\n9: 1 (hundred millions).\n\nSecond number: 712,871,993.\n\nBreaking down similarly:.\n\nPosition 1: 3 (ones).\n\n2: 9 (tens).\n\n3: 9 (hundreds).\n\n4: 1 (thousands).\n\n5: 7 (ten thousands).\n\n6: 8 (hundred thousands).\n\n7: 2 (millions).\n\n8: 1 (ten millions).\n\n9: 7 (hundred millions).\n\nWait, let me confirm:.\n\n712,871,993:.\n\nStarting from the right:.\n\nDigit 1: 3 (ones).\n\nDigit 2: 9 (tens).\n\nDigit 3: 9 (hundreds).\n\nDigit 4: 1 (thousands).\n\nDigit 5: 7 (ten thousands).\n\nDigit 6: 8 (hundred thousands).\n\nDigit 7: 2 (millions).\n\nDigit 8: 1 (ten millions).\n\nDigit 9: 7 (hundred millions).\n\nSo when adding, each corresponding digit position should be added.\n\nStarting with position 1 (ones):.\n\n8 + 3 = 11. Write down 1, carryover 1.\n\nPosition 2 (tens):.\n\n6 + 9 = 15, plus carryover 1 = 16. Write 6, carryover 1.\n\nPosition 3 (hundreds):.\n\n0 + 9 = 9, plus 1 = 10. Write 0, carryover 1.\n\nPosition 4 (thousands):.\n\n4 + 1 = 5, plus 1 = 6. Write 6, no carryover.\n\nPosition 5 (ten thousands):.\n\n0 +7 =7, no carryover.\n\nPosition 6 (hundred thousands):.\n\n3 +8 =11. Write 1, carryover 1.\n\nPosition 7 (millions):.\n\n2 +2 =4, plus 1 =5. Write 5, no carryover.\n\nPosition 8 (ten millions):.\n\n8 +1 =9.\n\nPosition 9 (hundred millions):.\n\n1 +7 =8.\n\nPutting it all together, starting from the right, the digits would be:.\n\nPositions 1-9: 1 (carryover?), wait no. Wait, let me list the digits from right to left as we computed:.\n\nPositions:.\n\n1: 1 (from 11, but written as 1, carryover 1).\n\nWait, actually, the digits we wrote down after each step are:.\n\nPosition 1: 1 (with carryover 1).\n\nPosition 2: 6 (carryover 1).\n\nPosition 3: 0 (carryover 1).\n\nPosition 4:6 (no carryover).\n\nPosition5:7 (no).\n\nPosition6:1 (carryover1).\n\nPosition7:5 (no).\n\nPosition8:9.\n\nPosition9:8.\n\nWait, so the digits from right to left are:.\n\n1 (position1), 6 (position2), 0 (position3),6 (4),7 (5),1 (6),5 (7),9 (8),8 (9). But since we have a carryover from position6 (3+8=11, so we had to carryover 1). Wait, let me retrace:.\n\nWait, position6 is hundred thousands place. The first number has 3 here, the second 8. 3+8=11. So we write 1 and carryover 1. That carryover affects the next higher position, which is millions place (position7).\n\nSo the millions place (position7): first number has 2, second has 2. 2+2=4 plus the carried 1 makes 5. So that's correct.\n\nContinuing, the ten millions place (position8): 8 +1 =9 (no carryover). Hundred millions place (position9):1+7=8. No carryover there.\n\nTherefore, putting all digits together from highest to lowest (left to right):.\n\nHundred millions:8, ten millions:9, millions:5, hundred thousands:1, ten thousands:7, thousands:6, hundreds:0, tens:6, ones:1.\n\nWait, but let's arrange them in order:.\n\nStarting from the highest position (position9: 8), then position8:9, position7:5, position6:1, position5:7, position4:6, position3:0, position2:6, position1:1.\n\nSo the number is:.\n\n8 (hundred millions), 9 (ten millions), 5 (millions), 1 (hundred thousands), 7 (ten thousands), 6 (thousands), 0 (hundreds), 6 (tens), 1 (ones). So, let me directly conclude the answer. The answer is "]] 53%|#####2    | 264/500 [6:12:03<3:53:50, 59.45s/it]
