6 is 3, second number's digit6 is1. 3+1=4. Write 4. No carryover.\n\nDigit7 (millions place):\n\nFirst number's digit7 is7, second's digit7 is1. 7+1=8. Write 8. No carryover.\n\nDigit8 (ten millions place):\n\nFirst number has 6, second has7. 6+7=13. Write 3, carryover1.\n\nDigit9 (hundred millions place):\n\nFirst number has6, second has5. 6+5=11, plus carryover1 is12. Write 2, carryover1.\n\nNow, there's a carryover1 to the next (billions) place, which is the 10th digit. Since both numbers only have 9 digits, the total will have 10 digits. So the billions place is 1.\n\nPutting all together, starting from the right:\n\nDigit1:8  \nDigit2:1 (from the tens place we had 11, wrote 1 but that was the digit2, but actually in the digit2 place, we had 2+9=11 so we wrote 1 and carried over. Wait, actually the digits written are:\n\nWait let me list all the digits we've computed:\n\nStarting from the right (units):\n\n1. 8 (units)\n2. 1 (tens place, since 2+9=11, so we wrote 1 there, with carryover)\nWait no, actually in the tens place, after adding, the digit is 1, but the carryover is 1. Wait, let me reconstruct the digits:\n\nLet me list each digit's result step by step:\n\nDigit1 (units): 0+8=8 → 8.  \nDigit2 (tens): 2+9=11 → write 1, carryover 1.  \nDigit3 (hundreds):6+9=15 +1=16 → write 6, carryover1.  \nDigit4 (thousands):5+8=13 +1=14 → write4, carryover1.  \nDigit5 (ten thousands):2+2=4 +1=5 → write5, no carry.  \nDigit6 (hundred thousands):3+1=4 → write4.  \nDigit7 (millions):7+1=8 → write8.  \nDigit8 (ten millions):6+7=13 → write3, carryover1.  \nDigit9 (hundred millions):6+5=11 +1=12 → write2, carryover1.  \nDigit10 (billions): carryover1 → write1.  \n\nSo the digits from right to left (units to billions) are:\n\n8 (units), 1 (tens), 6 (hundreds), 4 (thousands),5 (ten thousands),4 (hundred thousands),8 (millions),3 (ten millions),2 (hundred millions),1 (billions).\n\nTherefore, the number is 1,238,454,618.\n\nWait, let me write that out with commas properly.\n\nS