messages:  [["Please act as a math teacher and solve the addition problem in the given template.\n####\n# Question:\nWhat is the sum of <<number1>> and <<number2>>?\n# Reasoning:\nLet's add the two numbers digit by digit.\n1. The ones place: <<digit1>>\n2. The tens place: <<digit2>>\n<<other digits>>\nAnswer:\nTherefore, the final computed sum is <<answer>>.\n####\n# Question:\nWhat is the sum of 575113071 and 121197155?\n# Reasoning:", "<think>\nOkay, let's see. I need to add 575,113,071 and 121,197,155. Hmm, adding large numbers can be tricky, but I'll take it step by step. First, I should write them down one on top of the other, aligning the digits properly by place value. Let me visualize that:.\n\n       575113071.\n     + 121197155.\n     ------------.\n\nStarting from the rightmost digit, which is the ones place. Let's go digit by digit.\n\nStarting with the ones place: the first number has a 1 in the ones place, and the second number also has a 5 in the ones place. Wait, no, wait. Let me check again. Wait, the first number is 575,113,071. Breaking it down: the digits are 5 (hundred millions), 7 (ten millions), 5 (millions), 1 (hundred thousands), 1 (ten thousands), 3 (thousands), 0 (hundreds), 7 (tens), 1 (ones). The second number is 121,197,155. Breaking that down: 1 (hundred millions), 2 (ten millions), 1 (millions), 1 (hundred thousands), 9 (ten thousands), 7 (thousands), 1 (hundreds), 5 (tens), 5 (ones). Wait, so the ones digits are 1 and 5. Adding those gives 6? Wait, 1 +5 is 6. So ones place is 6. No carryover there.\n\nMoving to the tens place: the first number has a 7, the second has a 5. 7+5=12. So we write down 2 and carry over 1. .\n\nNext, the hundreds place: first number has 0, second has 1. Adding 0 +1 is 1, plus the carried over 1 makes 2. So hundreds digit is 2, no carryover.\n\nThousands place: first number has 3, second has 7. 3+7=10. So write 0 and carry over 1.\n\nTen thousands place: first number has 1, second has 9. 1+9=10, plus the carried 1 is 11. So write 1 and carry over 1.\n\nHundred thousands place: first number has 1, second has 1. 1+1=2 plus the carried 1 makes 3. So 3 here, no carryover.\n\nMillions place: first number has 5, second has 1. 5+1=6. .\n\nTen millions place: first number has 7, second has 2. 7+2=9.\n\nHundred millions place: first number has 5, second has 1. 5+1=6.\n\nWait, but let me check again step by step to make sure I didn't skip any digits. Let me count the digits in each number:.\n\nFirst number: 575,113,071 has 9 digits. Let's count from the right:.\n\nPosition 1 (ones): 1.\n\n2 (tens):7.\n\n3 (hundreds):0.\n\n4 (thousands):3.\n\n5 (ten thousands):1.\n\n6 (hundred thousands):1.\n\n7 (millions):5.\n\n8 (ten millions):7.\n\n9 (hundred millions):5.\n\nSecond number: 121,197,155. Let's break down similarly:.\n\nPosition 1 (ones):5.\n\n2 (tens):5.\n\nWait, hold on. Wait, maybe I messed up the breakdown earlier. Let me write both numbers with commas to clarify:.\n\nFirst number: 575,113,071 is 575,113,071. So commas separate every three digits. So that's 575 (million), 113 (thousand), 071. So the digits are:.\n\nDigit positions from right (ones is position 1):.\n\nPosition 1: 1 (ones).\n\nPosition 2: 7 (tens).\n\nPosition 3: 0 (hundreds).\n\nPosition 4: 3 (thousands).\n\nPosition 5: 1 (ten thousands).\n\nPosition 6: 1 (hundred thousands).\n\nPosition 7: 5 (millions).\n\nPosition 8: 7 (ten millions).\n\nPosition 9: 5 (hundred millions).\n\nSecond number: 121,197,155. Breaking down: 121 (million), 197 (thousand), 155. So digits:.\n\nPosition 1 (ones):5.\n\nPosition 2 (tens):5.\n\nWait, hold on, 155 is the last three digits. Let me write the second number as digits:.\n\n1 (hundred millions), 2 (ten millions), 1 (millions), 1 (hundred thousands), 9 (ten thousands), 7 (thousands), 1 (hundreds), 5 (tens), 5 (ones). Wait, that's correct. Wait, but when written as 121,197,155:.\n\nBreaking down each comma section:.\n\n121,197,155:.\n\n- The first comma separates millions: 121 (million), then 197 (thousand), then 155. So the digits from left to right are:.\n\n1 (hundred millions), 2 (ten millions), 1 (millions), 1 (hundred thousands), 9 (ten thousands), 7 (thousands), 1 (hundreds), 5 (tens), 5 (ones). .\n\nWait, so the tens place in the second number is 5 (position 2) and the ones is 5 (position1). .\n\nWait, so going back to the addition:.\n\nStarting from the rightmost digit (ones place):.\n\nFirst number's ones digit:1, second's ones digit:5. 1+5=6. So ones place is 6. Correct.\n\nTens place: first number has 7, second has 5. 7+5=12. So 2 with carryover 1. .\n\nHundreds place: first has 0, second has1. 0+1=1 plus carryover 1 is 2. So hundreds digit 2.\n\nThousands place: first has 3, second has7. 3+7=10. So 0 with carryover 1.\n\nTen thousands place: first has1, second has9. 1+9=10 plus 1 carried is 11. So 1 in that place, carryover 1.\n\nHundred thousands place: first has1, second has1. 1+1=2 plus 1 carried is 3. So 3.\n\nMillions place: first has5, second has1. 5+1=6.\n\nTen millions place: first has7, second has2. 7+2=9.\n\nHundred millions place: first has5, second has1. 5+1=6.\n\nSo putting all together, the digits from right to left (starting from ones):.\n\n6 (ones), 2 (tens), 2 (hundreds), 0 (thousands), 1 (ten thousands), 3 (hundred thousands), 6 (millions), 9 (ten millions), 6 (hundred millions). .\n\nWait, so writing the number from highest to lowest place: 6 (hundred millions), 9 (ten millions), 6 (millions), 3 (hundred thousands), 1 (ten thousands), 0 (thousands), 2 (hundreds), 2 (tens), 6 (ones). Wait, no, actually, the digits should be ordered as follows:.\n\nStarting from the highest place (hundred millions):.\n\nHundred millions:6.\n\nTen millions:9.\n\nMillions:6.\n\nHundred thousands:3.\n\nTen thousands:1.\n\nThousands:0.\n\nHundreds:2.\n\nTens:2.\n\nOnes:6. So, let me directly conclude the answer. The answer is "]]
