:6.\n\nSecond:3.\n\nSum:6+3=9. Write 9. Carryover 0.\n\nPosition4 (thousands):.\n\nFirst:3.\n\nSecond:3.\n\nSum:3+3=6. Write 6. Carryover 0.\n\nPosition5 (ten thousands):.\n\nFirst:8.\n\nSecond:4.\n\nSum:8+4=12. Write 2, carryover1.\n\nPosition6 (hundred thousands):.\n\nFirst:9.\n\nSecond:4.\n\nSum:9+4=13, plus carryover1 →14. Write 4, carryover1.\n\nPosition7 (millions):.\n\nFirst:1 (since first number's millions digit is the third digit from left: 5 (hundred millions), 1 (ten millions), 1 (millions). Wait, the digits from left are:.\n\nFirst number:.\n\nDigit1 (billions? No, since it's 9 digits, the first digit is hundred millions). Wait, for a 9-digit number, the places are:.\n\nDigit1: hundred millions (10^8).\n\nDigit2: ten millions (10^7).\n\nDigit3: millions (10^6).\n\nDigit4: hundred thousands (10^5).\n\nDigit5: ten thousands (10^4).\n\nDigit6: thousands (10^3).\n\nDigit7: hundreds (10^2).\n\nDigit8: tens (10^1).\n\nDigit9: ones (10^0).\n\nSo for the first number 511,983,601:.\n\nDigit1:5 (hundred millions).\n\nDigit2:1 (ten millions).\n\nDigit3:1 (millions).\n\nDigit4:9 (hundred thousands).\n\nDigit5:8 (ten thousands).\n\nDigit6:3 (thousands).\n\nDigit7:6 (hundreds).\n\nDigit8:0 (tens).\n\nDigit9:1 (ones).\n\nSecond number 941,443,398:.\n\nDigit1:9 (hundred millions).\n\nDigit2:4 (ten millions).\n\nDigit3:1 (millions).\n\nDigit4:4 (hundred thousands).\n\nDigit5:4 (ten thousands).\n\nDigit6:3 (thousands).\n\nDigit7:3 (hundreds).\n\nDigit8:9 (tens).\n\nDigit9:8 (ones).\n\nSo back to position7 (millions place):.\n\nFirst number's digit3 (millions) is 1.\n\nSecond number's digit3 (millions) is1.\n\nSum:1 +1 =2, plus any carryover from position6 (hundred thousands place which had a carryover1). Wait, the carryover from position6 is added to position7?.\n\nWait yes, the carryover from the previous position (position6) is added to the current position's sum.\n\nWait position6 (hundred thousands):.\n\nSum was 9+4=13 + carryover0 (since position5 had a carryover1 to position6, but wait, position5's sum was 12, which had a carryover1 to position6.\n\nWait let me clarify:.\n\nPosition5 (ten thousands):.\n\nSum was 8+4=12, so carryover1 to position6.\n\nPosition6 (hundred thousands):.\n\nFirst digit4 (hundred thousands place) is9 (from first number), second digit4 is4 (from second number). So 9+4=13, plus carryover1 → total 14. So write 4, carryover1 to position7.\n\nPosition7 (millions):.\n\nFirst digit3 is1 (first number), second digit3 is1 (second number). So 1+1=2 plus carryover1 → 3. So write 3, carryover0.\n\nPosition8 (ten millions):.\n\nFirst digit2 (ten millions) is1, second digit2 is4. 1+4=5. No carryover (since previous carryover was 0). So write5.\n\nPosition9 (hundred millions):.\n\nFirst digit1 is5, second digit1 is9. 5+9=14. Write4, carryover1.\n\nNow, since we've reached the highest digit (hundred millions), the carryover1 becomes a new digit in the billions place.\n\nSo the digits from highest to lowest (billions down to ones):.\n\nBillions:1.\n\nHundred millions:4.\n\nTen millions:5.\n\nMillions:3.\n\nHundred thousands:4.\n\nTen thousands:2.\n\nThousands:6.\n\nHundreds:9.\n\nTens:9.\n\nOnes:9. So, let me directly conclude the answer. The answer is "]]
