e first number is 828,185,719. So breaking into millions:\n\n828,000,000 +185,719.\n\nBut the second number is 558,128,080, which is 558,000,000 +128,080.\n\nAdding the millions parts: 828,000,000 +558,000,000 =1,386,000,000.\n\nAdding the remaining parts: 185,719 +128,080.\n\nWait, 185,719 +128,080:\n\n185,719 +100,000 =285,719\n\n285,719 +28,080 =313,799.\n\nYes, so total is 1,386,000,000 +313,799 =1,386,313,799.\n\nBut according to digit-by-digit, it\'s 1,380,313,799. There\'s a difference of 6,000,000.\n\nAh, I see the mistake now. In the digit-by-digit breakdown, the millions place is position7.\n\nFirst number\'s millions digit is the seventh from the right, which in 828,185,719 is the 8 in the millions place (since 828,185,719 is 8 (hundred millions), 2 (ten millions), 8 (millions), 1 (hundred thousands), 8 (ten thousands),5 (thousands),7 (hundreds),1 (tens),9 (ones). Wait, hold on, perhaps my digit positions were miscounted.\n\nLet me clarify the digit positions properly.\n\nLet\'s write the first number 828,185,719 with commas: 828,185,719.\n\nBreaking it down:\n\n- 8 (hundred millions place),\n\n- 2 (ten millions),\n\n- 8 (millions),\n\n- 1 (hundred thousands),\n\n- 8 (ten thousands),\n\n- 5 (thousands),\n\n- 7 (hundreds),\n\n- 1 (tens),\n\n- 9 (ones).\n\nSimilarly, the second number 558,128,080 is:\n\n-5 (hundred millions),\n\n-5 (ten millions),\n\n-8 (millions),\n\n-1 (hundred thousands),\n\n-2 (ten thousands),\n\n-8 (thousands),\n\n-0 (hundreds),\n\n-8 (tens),\n\n-0 (ones).\n\nWait a second! Here\'s the error.\n\nIn the second number, 558,128,080:\n\nThe millions place is the third digit from the left, which is 8 (since it\'s 5 (hundred millions), 5 (ten millions),8 (millions), etc.). Wait, let\'s parse it correctly.\n\nBreaking down 558,128,080:\n\nStarting from the left:\n\nDigit1 (hundred millions):5\n\nDigit2 (ten millions):5\n\nDigit3 (millions):8\n\nDigit4 (hundred thousands):1\n\nDigit5 (ten thousands):2\n\nDigit6 (thousands):8\n\nDigit7 (hundred