ext digit, which is the trillions place.\n\nPutting it all together, starting from the right, the digits are:.\n\nTrillions: 1, then billions: 9, hundred millions: 9, ten millions: 3, millions: 5, hundred thousands: 1, ten thousands: 6, thousands: 4, hundreds: 8, tens: 5, ones: 0. Wait, maybe I messed up the order. Let me recount.\n\nWait, when I added the billions place (the 9 and 9 plus the carryover from the hundred millions place), that actually would be:.\n\nWait, let's redo the steps more carefully. Maybe I made a mistake in the places. Let me write each digit position step by step properly.\n\nStarting from the right:.\n\nNumbers are:.\n\nFirst number: 987,039,576 → breaking down each digit:.\n\n- 9 (hundred millions).\n- 8 (ten millions).\n- 7 (millions).\n- 0 (hundred thousands).\n- 3 (ten thousands).\n- 9 (thousands).\n- 5 (hundreds).\n- 7 (tens).\n- 6 (ones).\n\nWait, actually, let me parse the numbers correctly. Let me write both numbers digit by digit:.\n\nFirst number: 987,039,576.\n\nBreaking into digits from right to left (since addition starts from the right):.\n\nPositions (starting at 0 for ones):.\n\n0: 6 (ones).\n\n1: 7 (tens).\n\n2: 5 (hundreds).\n\n3: 9 (thousands).\n\n4: 3 (ten thousands).\n\n5: 0 (hundred thousands).\n\n6: 7 (millions).\n\n7: 8 (ten millions).\n\n8: 9 (hundred millions).\n\nSecond number: 948,125,274.\n\nDigits:.\n\n0: 4 (ones).\n\n1: 7 (tens).\n\n2: 2 (hundreds).\n\n3: 5 (thousands).\n\n4: 2 (ten thousands).\n\n5: 1 (hundred thousands).\n\n6: 8 (millions).\n\n7: 4 (ten millions).\n\n8: 9 (hundred millions).\n\nWait, actually, let's list them properly:.\n\nFirst number: 9 8 7 0 3 9 5 7 6.\n\nWait, perhaps better to write each digit position:.\n\nLet me write both numbers digit by digit with their place values:.\n\nFirst number (987,039,576):.\n\n- 9 (hundred millions place, 8th digit from the right).\n- 8 (ten millions).\n- 7 (millions).\n- 0 (hundred thousands).\n- 3 (ten thousands).\n- 9 (thousands).\n- 5 (hundreds).\n- 7 (tens).\n- 6 (ones).\n\nSecond number (948,125,274):.\n\n- 9 (hundred millions place).\n- 4 (ten millions).\n- 8 (millions).\n- 1 (hundred thousands).\n- 2 (ten thousands).\n- 5 (thousands).\n- 2 (hundreds).\n- 7 (tens).\n- 4 (ones).\n\nWait, perhaps I should write each digit from right to left with their positions:.\n\nPosition 0 (ones):.\n\nFirst number: 6.\n\nSecond number:4.\n\nPosition 1 (tens):.\n\nFirst:7, Second:7.\n\nPosition 2 (hundreds):.\n\nFirst:5, Second:2.\n\nPosition 3 (thousands):.\n\nFirst:9, Second:5.\n\nPosition 4 (ten thousands):.\n\nFirst:3, Second:2.\n\nPosition 5 (hundred thousands):.\n\nFirst:0, Second:1.\n\nPosition 6 (millions):.\n\nFirst:7, Second:8.\n\nPosition 7 (ten millions):.\n\nFirst:8, Second:4.\n\nPosition 8 (hundred millions):.\n\nFirst:9, Second:9.\n\nNow adding each position, starting from position 0:.\n\nStarting with carryover = 0.\n\nPosition 0:.\n\n6 + 4 = 10. Sum digit: 0, carryover becomes 1.\n\nPosition 1:.\n\n7 +7 =14, plus carryover 1 =15. Digit:5, carryover 1.\n\nPosition 2:.\n\n5 +2 =7 +1 (carry) =8. Digit 8, carryover 0.\n\nPosition3:.\n\n9+5=14, +0 →14. Digit4, carryover1.\n\nPosition4:.\n\n3+2=5 +1 →6. Digit6, carryover0.\n\nPosition5:.\n\n0+1=1. Digit1, carryover0.\n\nPosition6:.\n\n7+8=15. Digit5, carryover1.\n\nPosition7:.\n\n8+4=12 +1=13. Digit3, carryover1.\n\nPosition8:.\n\n9+9=18 +1=19. Digit9, carryover1.\n\nNow, since we've reached the highest position (position8, hundred millions), but there's a carryover of 1. So we need to add a new digit at position9 (billions place?), which is 1.\n\nSo the total digits are:.\n\nPosition9:1.\n\nPosition8:9.\n\nPosition7:3.\n\nPosition6:5.\n\nPosition5:1.\n\nPosition4:6.\n\nPosition3:4.\n\nPosition2:8.\n\nPosition1:5.\n\nPosition0:0.\n\nPutting them all together from position9 down to 0:.\n\n1 (position9), 9 (position8), 3 (position7),5 (position6),1 (position5),6 (position4),4 (position3),8 (position2),5 (position1),0 (position0). So, let me directly conclude the answ