4:5 (thousands).\n\n5:5 (ten thousands).\n\n6:7 (hundred thousands).\n\n7:1 (millions).\n\n8:3 (ten millions).\n\n9:1 (hundred millions).\n\nSecond number: 603,928,666:.\n\nDigits (positions 1 to 9):.\n\n1:6 (ones).\n\n2:6 (tens).\n\n3:6 (hundreds).\n\n4:8 (thousands).\n\n5:2 (ten thousands).\n\n6:9 (hundred thousands).\n\n7:3 (millions).\n\n8:0 (ten millions).\n\n9:6 (hundred millions).\n\nWait, that's better. Let's list each digit position:.\n\nPosition 1 (ones):.\n\nFirst num:3, second:6 → total 9.\n\nPosition 2 (tens):.\n\nFirst:7, second:6 →13. Write 3, carry 1.\n\nPosition3 (hundreds):.\n\nFirst:8, second:6 →14 + carry 1 →15 →5, carry1.\n\nPosition4 (thousands):.\n\nFirst:5, second:8 →13 +1 →14 →4, carry1.\n\nPosition5 (ten thousands):.\n\nFirst:5, second:2 →7 +1 (carry) →8? Wait 5+2 is 7 plus carryover 1 makes 8? Wait, no, the carryover from the previous step (position4) was 1, so that's added here. Wait, no, the carryover from position4 (thousands) is 1, so position5 (ten thousands) adds 5+2 +1=8. So no carryover here. Wait but wait, the previous step (position4) had 5+8 + carryover? Wait let me recheck:.\n\nWait position4 (thousands place):.\n\nFirst number's thousands digit is 5 (since position4 is thousands), second's thousands is 8. So 5+8=13, plus the carryover from position3 (hundreds) which was 1. Wait no, the carryover from position3 is 1, so 5+8 +1 (carry from hundreds) =14. So 14 here, so 4 with carryover1. So that's correct. So the carryover from position4 is 1. So position5 (ten thousands):.\n\nFirst number's ten thousands digit is 5 (position5), second's is 2 (position5). So 5+2 +1 (carry from thousands) =8. So no carryover here. .\n\nPosition6 (hundred thousands):.\n\nFirst number's hundred thousands digit is 7 (position6), second's is9 (position6). So 7+9=16. No carryover from previous (position5 had no carry). So 16 →6, carryover1.\n\nPosition7 (millions):.\n\nFirst number's millions digit is1 (position7), second's is3 (position7). 1+3 +1 (carry from hundred thousands) =5. No carryover.\n\nPosition8 (ten millions):.\n\nFirst number's ten millions digit is3 (position8), second's is0 (position8). 3+0=3. No carryover.\n\nPosition9 (hundred millions):.\n\nFirst number's is1, second's is6 →1+6=7.\n\nPutting all together:.\n\nStarting from the right, the digits are:.\n\nPosition1:9.\n\nPosition2:3.\n\nPosition3:5.\n\nPosition4:4.\n\nPosition5:8.\n\nPosition6:6 (since 16, so 6 here with carryover1 from hundred thousands?).\n\nWait hold on. Let me retrace.\n\nWait position6 (hundred thousands):.\n\n7 (first) +9 (second) =16. Adding any carry? The previous position (position5) had a carryover of 0 (since it was 8). So 16 →6 with carryover1.\n\nThen position7 (millions):.\n\n1+3 +1 (carry) =5. So no carryover.\n\nPosition8:3+0=3.\n\nPosition9:1+6=7.\n\nSo the digits from right to left (positions1-9):.\n\n9 (position1), 3 (2),5 (3),4 (4),8 (5),6 (6),5 (7),3 (8),7 (9).\n\nSo the number is 7 (hundred millions), 3 (ten millions),5 (millions),8 (hundred thousands?), wait no, let's arrange correctly.\n\nWait the digits in order from highest to lowest place would be:.\n\nPosition9 (hundred millions):7.\n\nPosition8 (ten millions):3.\n\nPosition7 (millions):5.\n\nPosition6 (hundred thousands):6.\n\nPosition5 (ten thousands):8.\n\nPosition4 (thousands):4.\n\nPosition3 (hundreds):5.\n\nPosition2 (tens):3.\n\nPosition1 (ones):9. So, let me directly conclude the answer. The answer is "]]
