s:0, Ten thousands:3, Thousands:2, Hundreds:1, Tens:2, Ones:6.\n\nSo when adding, let's align each digit column-wise:.\n\nHundred millions: 4 +7.\nTen millions:9 +1.\nMillions:1 +2.\nHundred thousands:6 +0.\nTen thousands:3 +3.\nThousands:2 +2.\nHundreds:3 +1.\nTens:4 +2.\nOnes:7 +6.\n\nWait, but addition is done from the right (ones) to the left (highest place). Let me reverse the order for easier processing:.\n\nStarting from the ones digit (rightmost):.\n\nOnes place:7 +6 =13. Write 3, carryover 1.\n\nTens place:4 +2 =6, plus the carryover 1 is 7. Write 7, no carryover.\n\nHundreds place:3 +1 =4. Write 4.\n\nThousands place:2 +2 =4. Write 4.\n\nTen thousands place:3 +3 =6. Write 6.\n\nHundred thousands place:6 +0 =6. Write 6.\n\nMillions place:1 +2 =3. Write 3.\n\nTen millions place:9 +1 =10. Write 0, carryover 1.\n\nHundred millions place:4 +7 =11, plus the carried over 1 makes 12. So write 2 and carryover 1. Since there are no more digits, that carryover 1 becomes a new digit at the beginning.\n\nPutting all together, the digits from right to left (with carries):.\n\nStarting from the ones:.\n\n3 (ones), 7 (tens), 4 (hundreds), 4 (thousands), 6 (ten thousands), 6 (hundred thousands), 3 (millions), 0 (ten millions), 2 (hundred millions), and then the carried over 1 in the billions place.\n\nSo the final number is 1 (billions) 2 (hundred millions), 0 (ten millions), 3 (millions), 6 (hundred thousands), 6 (ten thousands), 4 (thousands), 4 (hundreds), 7 (tens), 3 (ones).\n\nWait, let me reconstruct the number properly:.\n\nStarting from the highest digit:.\n\nThe carryover 1 is in the billions place. Then:.\n\nBillions:1.\n\nHundred millions:2 (since 4+7+1carryover? Wait, no, let me check the hundred millions place again:.\n\nWait when adding the hundred millions digits (4 +7 =11) plus the carried over 1 from the ten millions place (which was 9+1=10, so carryover 1 to hundred millions):.\n\nWait, perhaps I made a mistake here. Let me redo the addition step by step 