nd) = 14. Hmm, so here we have 14, which means we write down 4 and carry over 1 to the billions place. .\n\nBut since there are no digits beyond the hundred millions place, that carried over 1 becomes a 1 in the billions place. .\n\nPutting it all together: the billions place is 1, then 4 in the hundred millions, 9 in the ten millions, 0 in the millions, 3 in the hundred thousands, 0 in the ten thousands, 7 in the thousands, 3 in the hundreds, 3 in the tens, and 3 in the ones. Wait, let me check that again because I might have messed up the order.\n\nWait, maybe I should go step by step more carefully. Let me list each digit position:.\n\nStarting from the right:.\n\nOnes place: 4 + 9 = 13. Write 3, carry 1.\n\nTens: 9 + 3 + 1 = 13. Write 3, carry 1.\n\nHundreds: 2 + 0 + 1 = 3. Write 3, no carry.\n\nThousands: 6 + 1 = 7. Write 7, no carry.\n\nTen thousands: 0 + 0 = 0. Write 0.\n\nHundred thousands: 2 + 1 = 3. Write 3.\n\nMillions: 6 + 4 = 10. Write 0, carry 1.\n\nTen millions: 5 + 3 + 1 = 9. Write 9.\n\nHundred millions: 9 + 5 = 14. Write 4, carry 1.\n\nBillions place: The carryover 1, so we add a 1 at the beginning. So, let me directly conclude the answer. The answer is "]]
