messages:  [["Please act as a math teacher and solve the addition problem in the given template.\n####\n# Question:\nWhat is the sum of <<number1>> and <<number2>>?\n# Reasoning:\nLet's add the two numbers digit by digit.\n1. The ones place: <<digit1>>\n2. The tens place: <<digit2>>\n<<other digits>>\nAnswer:\nTherefore, the final computed sum is <<answer>>.\n####\n# Question:\nWhat is the sum of 935131196 and 169164213?\n# Reasoning:", '<think>\nOkay, let\'s tackle this addition problem. The numbers are 935,131,196 and 169,164,213. Hmm, I need to add them digit by digit, starting from the rightmost ones place. Let me write them down to visualize better.\n\nFirst, aligning the numbers by their place values:.\n\n        935131196.\n      + 169164213.\n      ------------.\n\nStarting with the ones place: the rightmost digits are 6 and 3. Adding those gives 9. That\'s straightforward.\n\nNext, the tens place: 9 and 1. That adds up to 10. Wait, so I write down 0 and carry over 1 to the next column.\n\nMoving to the hundreds place: 1 (from the first number) plus 2 (from the second) plus the carried-over 1. That totals 4. No carryover here.\n\nNow the thousands place: 1 and 4. That\'s 5. Still no carryover.\n\nTens of thousands place: 3 and 6. That adds to 9. Easy.\n\nHundreds of thousands place: 5 and 1. That\'s 6.\n\nMillions place: 3 and 6. Hmm, 3 + 6 is 9.\n\nTen millions place: 9 and 9. Wait, 9 + 9 is 18. So I write down 8 and carry over 1.\n\nHundred millions place: 3 (from the first number) and 1 (from the second) plus the carried-over 1. 3 + 1 + 1 = 5.\n\nBillions place: The first number has a 9, and the second has 1. Adding those gives 10. So I write 0 and carry over 1. Since there are no more digits, that carried-over 1 becomes another digit at the beginning.\n\nPutting it all together, starting from the right: 9, then 0 (with the carried-over 1 making it 10 in the billions place). Wait, let me check again step by step to make sure I didn\'t mix up any digits.\n\nWait, maybe I should write each step carefully:.\n\nLet me list each digit position:.\n\nStarting from the right:.\n\n1. Ones place: 6 + 3 = 9.\n2. Tens: 9 + 1 = 10 (write 0, carry 1).\n3. Hundreds: 1 + 2 + 1 (carry) = 4.\n4. Thousands: 1 + 4 = 5.\n5. Ten thousands: 3 + 6 = 9.\n6. Hundred thousands: 5 + 1 = 6.\n7. Millions: 3 + 6 = 9.\n8. Ten millions: 9 + 9 = 18 (write 8, carry 1).\n9. Hundred millions: 3 + 1 + 1 (carry) = 5.\n10. Billions: 9 + 1 = 10 (write 0, carry 1).\n11. The final carryover 1 becomes the next digit.\n\nSo the digits from right to left would be:.\n\n9 (ones), 0 (tens), 4 (hundreds), 5 (thousands), 9 (ten thousands), 6 (hundred thousands), 9 (millions), 8 (ten millions), 5 (hundred millions), 0 (billions), and then the 1 in the next place. Wait, so combining all:.\n\nStarting from the highest digit: 1 (from the carried-over 1), then 0 (billions place), then 5 (hundred millions), 8 (ten millions), 9 (millions), 6 (hundred thousands), 9 (ten thousands?), Wait, maybe I messed up the order here.\n\nWait, perhaps I should structure the positions properly. Let me count the digits:.\n\nFirst number: 935,131,196 has 9 digits. Let\'s break it down:.\n\nBillions place: 9 (1st digit from the left).\nHundred millions: 3.\nTen millions: 5.\nMillions: 1.\nHundred thousands: 3.\nTen thousands: 1.\nThousands: 1.\nHundreds: 9.\nTens: 1.\nOnes: 6. Wait, actually, commas are placed every three digits. So 935,131,196 is 9 3 5 , 1 3 1 , 1 9 6. So the digits are:.\n\n9 (billions), 3 (hundred millions), 5 (ten millions), 1 (millions), 3 (hundred thousands), 1 (ten thousands), 1 (thousands), 9 (hundreds), 1 (tens), 6 (ones). Wait no, that\'s 10 digits? Wait 935,131,196: Let\'s count:.\n\n9 (1), 3 (2), 5 (3), comma, 1 (4), 3 (5), 1 (6), comma, 1 (7), 9 (8), 6 (9). So 9 digits total. Wait, commas are placed after every three digits starting from the right. So 935,131,196 is 9 (hundred millions?), wait maybe I should think in terms of positions:.\n\nThe number 935,131,1