 8 → 8.\n4. Thousands: 9 + 9 = 18 → 8, carry 1.\n5. Ten-thousands: 2 + 0 = 2 +1 =3 →3.\n6. Hundred thousands: 2 +4 =6 →6.\n7. Millions:4 +9=13 →3, carry1.\n8. Ten millions:1 +7=8 +1=9 →9.\n9. Hundred millions:3 +6=9 →9.\n\nWait, but the first number is 314,229,068. Let me check the digits again:.\n\nBreaking down 314,229,068:.\n\nDigits from right (ones place):.\n\nPosition 1 (ones): 8.\n\n2 (tens):6.\n\n3 (hundreds):0.\n\n4 (thousands):9.\n\n5 (ten thousands):2.\n\n6 (hundred thousands):2.\n\n7 (millions):4.\n\n8 (ten millions):1.\n\n9 (hundred millions):3.\n\nSecond number 679,409,818:.\n\nPositions:.\n\n1:8.\n\n2:1.\n\n3:8.\n\n4:9.\n\n5:0.\n\n6:4.\n\n7:7.\n\n8:9.\n\n9:6.\n\nWait, so when adding the hundred thousands place (position 6), the first number has 2 and the second has 4? Let me confirm:.\n\nFirst number: 3 (hundred millions), 1 (ten millions), 4 (millions), 2 (hundred thousands), 2 (ten thousands), 9 (thousands), 0 (hundreds), 6 (tens), 8 (ones). Wait a second, maybe I mixed up the places.\n\nWait, perhaps I need to write the numbers out properly to avoid confusion.\n\nLet me write both numbers with commas:.\n\nFirst number: 314,229,068. Breaking down:.\n\n3 1 4 2 2 9 0 6 8.\n\nBut commas are placed every three digits. So 314,229,068 is 314,229,068. So the digits from left to right are:.\n\nHundred millions: 3.\n\nTen millions:1.\n\nMillions:4.\n\nHundred thousands:2.\n\nTen thousands:2.\n\nThousands:9.\n\nHundreds:0.\n\nTens:6.\n\nOnes:8.\n\nSecond number: 679,409,818.\n\nBreaking down:.\n\n6 (hundred millions), 7 (ten millions), 9 (millions), 4 (hundred thousands), 0 (ten thousands), 9 (thousands), 8 (hundreds), 1 (tens), 8 (ones).\n\nSo when adding:.\n\nLet me align them properly:.\n\n          3 1 4 2 2 9 0 6 8.\n\n        + 6 7 9 4 0 9 8 1 8.\n\n        ---------------------.\n\nStarting from the right (ones place):.\n\nOnes: 8 +8 =16 →6 carryover 1.\n\nTens:6 +1 =7 plus carryover 1 →8.\n\nHundreds:0 +8 =8.\n\nThousands:9 +9=18 →8 carryover1.\n\nTen