y, let's count the digits. Both numbers are 9 digits:.\n\n911305121 is 9 digits:.\n\nDigits from right (position 1 to 9):.\n\nPosition 1 (ones): 1.\n\nPosition 2 (tens): 2.\n\nPosition 3 (hundreds):5.\n\nPosition 4 (thousands):0.\n\nPosition 5 (ten thousands):3.\n\nPosition 6 (hundred thousands):1.\n\nPosition 7 (millions):1.\n\nPosition 8 (ten millions):1.\n\nPosition 9 (hundred millions):9.\n\nSecond number 401932634:.\n\nDigits from right:.\n\nPosition 1:4.\n\nPosition 2:3.\n\nPosition 3:6.\n\nPosition 4:2.\n\nPosition 5:3.\n\nPosition 6:9.\n\nPosition 7:1.\n\nPosition 8:0.\n\nPosition 9:4.\n\nSo adding each position:.\n\nStarting at position 1 (ones):.\n\n1 +4=5. Write down 5.\n\nPosition 2 (tens): 2+3=5. Write 5.\n\nPosition3:5+6=11. Wait, 5 (from first) +6 (second) =11. So write 1 and carry over 1.\n\nWait, I think I made a mistake earlier here. Let me go step by step again properly.\n\nLet me list each digit place by place:.\n\nLet's write each number's digits in order from right to left (to make it easier to add starting from ones):.\n\nFirst number: 911305121.\n\nBreaking down each digit from right (units) to left (hundred millions):.\n\nPosition 1 (units): 1.\n\nPosition 2 (tens): 2.\n\nPosition 3 (hundreds):1.\n\nPosition 4 (thousands):0.\n\nPosition 5 (ten thousands):3.\n\nPosition 6 (hundred thousands):1.\n\nPosition 7 (millions):1.\n\nPosition 8 (ten millions):1.\n\nPosition 9 (hundred millions):9.\n\nWait, hold on, maybe I messed up the positions earlier. Let's parse the number correctly.\n\nWait, 911,305,121:.\n\nBreaking it down:.\n\nStarting from the right (units):.\n\nThe number is 911,305,121.\n\nSo the digits are:.\n\nDigit 1 (units place): 1.\n\nDigit 2 (tens): 2.\n\nDigit 3 (hundreds): 1.\n\nDigit 4 (thousands):5.\n\nDigit 5 (ten thousands):0.\n\nDigit 6 (hundred thousands):3.\n\nDigit 7 (millions):1.\n\nDigit 8 (ten millions):1.\n\nDigit 9 (hundred millions):9.\n\nWait, perhaps I need to split each digit properly.\n\nWait, let me write the number as:.\n\nDigits of 911,305,121:.\n\nStarting from the left, it's 9 (hundred millions), 1 (ten millions), 1 (millions), 3 (hundred thousands), 0 (ten thousands), 5 (thousands), 1 (hundreds), 2 (tens), 1 (units).\n\nSo when written out digit positions from right (units) to left (hundred millions):.\n\nPosition 1: units: 1.\n\nPosition 2: tens: 2.\n\nPosition 3: hundreds:1.\n\nPosition 4: thousands:5.\n\nPosition 5: ten thousands:0.\n\nPosition 6: hundred thousands:3.\n\nPosition 7: millions:1.\n\nPosition 8: ten millions:1.\n\nPosition 9: hundred millions:9.\n\nSimilarly for the second number: 401,932,634.\n\nBreaking down:.\n\nDigits from right:.\n\nPosition 1 (units):4.\n\nPosition 2 (tens):3.\n\nPosition 3 (hundreds):6.\n\nPosition 4 (thousands):2.\n\nPosition 5 (ten thousands):3.\n\nPosition 6 (hundred thousands):9.\n\nPosition 7 (millions):1.\n\nPosition 8 (ten millions):0.\n\nPosition 9 (hundred millions):4.\n\nSo now adding each position step by step:.\n\nStarting with Position 1 (units):.\n\nFirst number:1, second:4 → 1+4=5. Write 5, carryover 0.\n\nPosition 2 (tens):.\n\n2 +3 =5. Write 5, carryover 0.\n\nPosition3 (hundreds):.\n\n1 +6 =7. Write 7, carryover 0.\n\nPosition4 (thousands):.\n\n5 (from first) +2 (second)=7. Write 7, carryover 0.\n\nPosition5 (ten thousands):.\n\n0 +3 =3. Write 3, carryover 0.\n\nPosition6 (hundred thousands):.\n\n3 +9=12. So write 2, carryover 1.\n\nPosition7 (millions):.\n\n1 (first) +1 (second) + carryover 1 = 3. Write 3, carryover 0.\n\nPosition8 (ten millions):.\n\n1 (first) +0 (second) =1. Write 1, carryover 0.\n\nPosition9 (hundred millions):.\n\n9 +4 =13. So write 3 and carryover 1. Wait but since this is the highest digit, we need to add the carryover. So actually, the total would be 13 in the hundred millions place, meaning we have to add another digit. Wait, so the total sum will have a 10-digit number here.\n\nWait, let me redo this step carefully:.\n\nPosition9 (hundred millions):.\n\nFirst number:9, second:4. Addi