 first number is 2, second is 1. Adding the carried 1: 2 + 1 + 1 = 4. So that's 4 in the hundred millions place.\n\nPutting it all together, starting from the highest digit: 4 (hundred millions), then 2 (ten millions), 1 (millions), 6 (hundred thousands), 8 (ten thousands), 0 (thousands), 1 (hundreds), 6 (tens), 6 (ones). Wait, let me check again because I might have messed up the positions. Let me recount the digits step by step.\n\nWait, maybe I should do this step by step more carefully. Let's break down each digit position properly.\n\nLet me write both numbers with their place values:.\n\nFirst number: 269,540,554.\nBreaking down each digit from right to left (starting at ones):.\n- Ones: 4.\n- Tens: 5.\n- Hundreds: 5.\n- Thousands: 0.\n- Ten thousands: 4.\n- Hundred thousands: 5.\n- Millions: 9.\n- Ten millions: 6.\n- Hundred millions: 2.\n\nSecond number: 152,139,612.\nBreaking down similarly:.\n- Ones: 2.\n- Tens: 1.\n- Hundreds: 6.\n- Thousands: 9.\n- Ten thousands: 3.\n- Hundred thousands: 1.\n- Millions: 2.\n- Ten millions: 5.\n- Hundred millions: 1.\n\nNow let's add each column starting from the right (ones):.\n\n1. Ones place: 4 + 2 = 6. Write down 6. No carryover.\n2. Tens place: 5 + 1 = 6. Write 6. No carryover.\n3. Hundreds place: 5 + 6 = 11. Write down 1, carryover 1.\n4. Thousands place: 0 + 9 + 1 (carryover) = 10. Write 0, carryover 1.\n5. Ten thousands place: 4 + 3 + 1 = 8. Write 8, no carryover.\n6. Hundred thousands place:5 +1 =6. Write 6.\n7. Millions place:9 +2 =11. Write 1, carryover 1.\n8. Ten millions place:6 +5 +1 (carryover) =12. Write 2, carryover 1.\n9. Hundred millions place:2 +1 +1 (carryover) =4. Write 4.\n\nPutting all the digits together from right to left (since we started from ones):.\n\nThe digits we wrote are:.\nOnes:6, Tens:6, Hundreds:1, Thousands:0, Ten thousands:8, Hundred thousands:6, Millions:1, Ten millions:2, Hundred millions:4. So, let me directly conclude the answer. The answer is "]]
