add each digit starting from the ones:.\n\nPosition 1 (ones):6 +5 =11. Write 1, carryover 1.\n\nPosition 2 (tens):9 +0 +1=10. Write 0, carryover1.\n\nPosition3 (hundreds):2 +3=5 +1=6. Write6, no carryover.\n\nPosition4 (thousands):8 +2=10. Write0, carryover1.\n\nPosition5 (ten thousands):7 +8=15 +1=16. Write6, carryover1.\n\nPosition6 (hundred thousands):3 +1=4 +1=5. Write5, no carryover.\n\nPosition7 (millions):9 +1=10. Write0, carryover1.\n\nPosition8 (ten millions):7 +3=10 +1=11. Write1, carryover1.\n\nPosition9 (hundred millions):8 +1=9 +1=10. Write0, carryover1.\n\nNow, since there are no more digits, the carryover1 becomes the billions place.\n\nSo putting it all together, the result is 1 (billions) 0 (hundred millions) 1 (ten millions) 0 (millions) 5 (hundred thousands) 6 (ten thousands) 0 (thousands) 6 (hundreds) 0 (tens) 1 (ones). Wait, let me check again step by step.\n\nWait let me list each position's result:.\n\nStarting from the right:.\n\nPosition 1 (ones):1.\n\nPosition2 (tens):0.\n\nPosition3 (hundreds):6.\n\nPosition4 (thousands):0 (with a carryover).\n\nWait let's go step by step again carefully:.\n\nStarting from Position1:.\n\n1. Ones: 6 +5 =11 → digit 1, carryover 1.\n\n2. Tens:9 +0 +1=10 → digit 0, carryover1.\n\n3. Hundreds:2 +3=5 +0 (no carryover? Wait, previous carryover was 1, but after step2, the carryover was from step1's 1. Wait in step3, after step2, the carryover is 1 from step2's addition? Wait no:.\n\nWait in step2 (tens place), the sum was 10, so carryover1 to the next (hundreds place). So step3 (hundreds):.\n\n2 (from first number) +3 (second) +1 (carryover) =6. So write 6, no carryover.\n\n4. Thousands place: first has 8, second has2. 8+2=10. So write 0, carryover1.\n\n5. Ten thousands: first 7 + second8 =15 +1 (carryover) =16. So write6, carryover1.\n\n6. Hundred thousands: first3 + second1 =4 +1 (carryover) =5. Write5, no carryover.\n\n7. Millions: first9 + second1 =10. Write0, carryover1.\n\n8. Ten millions: first7 + second3 =10 +1 (carryover) =11. So write1, carryover1.\n\n9. Hundred millions: first8 + second1 =9 +1 (carryover) =10. Write0, carryover1.\n\n10. Billions place: the carryover1, so we write1 here.\n\nNow, compiling the digits from right to left:.\n\nPositions:.\n\n1 (ones):1.\n\n2 (tens):0.\n\n3 (hundreds):6.\n\n4 (thousands):0.\n\n5 (ten thousands):6.\n\n6 (hundred thousands):5.\n\n7 (millions):0.\n\n8 (ten millions):1.\n\n9 (hundred millions):0.\n\n10 (billions):1.\n\nSo putting it all together, the number is 1,001,060,601? Wait let's arrange the digits properly.\n\nStarting from the highest place:.\n\nBillions:1.\n\nHundred millions:0.\n\nTen millions:0 (Wait, no, let's list each digit's place:.\n\nPosition10 (billions):1.\n\nPosition9 (hundred millions):0.\n\nPosition8 (ten millions):1.\n\nPosition7 (millions):0.\n\nPosition6 (hundred thousands):5.\n\nPosition5 (ten thousands):6.\n\nPosition4 (thousands):0.\n\nPosition3 (hundreds):6.\n\nPosition2 (tens):0.\n\nPosition1 (ones):1.\n\nWait, so from left to right (billions to ones):.\n\n1 (billions), 0 (hundred millions), 1 (ten millions), 0 (millions),5 (hundred thousands),6 (ten thousands),0 (thousands),6 (hundreds),0 (tens),1 (ones). So, let me directly conclude the answer. The answer is "]]
