.\n- 5 (hundred thousand).\n- 0 (ten thousand).\n- 2 (thousand).\n- 9 (hundred).\n- 1 (ten).\n- 2 (one).\n\nSecond number (331,062,010):.\n- 3 (hundred million).\n- 3 (ten million).\n- 1 (million).\n- 0 (hundred thousand).\n- 6 (ten thousand).\n- 2 (thousand).\n- 0 (hundred).\n- 1 (ten).\n- 0 (one).\n\nSo adding each column from right to left (starting at ones):.\n\nOnes: 2 + 0 = 2.\nTens: 1 + 1 = 2.\nHundreds: 9 + 0 = 9.\nThousands: 2 + 2 = 4.\nTen thousands: 0 + 6 = 6.\nHundred thousands: 5 + 0 = 5.\nMillions: 8 + 1 = 9.\nTen millions: 6 + 3 = 9.\nHundred millions: 3 + 3 = 6.\n\nWait, that seems straightforward, but let me check each step again to make sure I didn't skip any columns or miscount.\n\nStarting over:.\n\nOnes place: 2 + 0 = 2. Correct.\n\nTens place: 1 (from first number) + 1 (from second) = 2. Correct.\n\nHundreds place: 9 + 0 = 9. Right.\n\nThousands place: 2 (from first number's thousands place) + 2 (from second's thousands place) = 4. Correct.\n\nTen thousands place: 0 (first number) + 6 (second) = 6. Yes.\n\nHundred thousands place: 5 (first) + 0 (second) =5. Correct.\n\nMillions place: 8 (first) +1 (second's millions place) = 9. Yes.\n\nTen millions place: 6 (first) +3 (second's ten millions) = 9. Correct.\n\nHundred millions place: 3 (first) +3 (second) =6. Right.\n\nSo putting all those digits together: 6 (hundred millions), 9 (ten millions), 9 (millions), 5 (hundred thousands), 6 (ten thousands), 4 (thousands), 9 (hundreds), 2 (tens), 2 (ones). So, let me directly conclude the answer. The answer is "]]
