re, carry over 1.\n\nHundred millions place: The first number has 2 and the second has 6. 2 +6 =8, plus the carried 1 makes 9. .\n\nWait, let me check that again. Wait, the first number is 275,769,705. Breaking down the places:.\n\nLet me count the digits again to make sure. Let's see, 275,769,705 is 9 digits: 2 (hundred millions), 7 (ten millions), 5 (millions), 7 (hundred thousands), 6 (ten thousands), 9 (thousands), 7 (hundreds), 0 (tens), 5 (ones). Wait, maybe I messed up the place values earlier. Let me confirm each digit's position properly.\n\nWait, perhaps I should write both numbers with their place values to avoid confusion. Let's do that:.\n\nFirst number: 275,769,705.\n\nBreaking down:.\n\n- Hundred millions: 2.\n- Ten millions: 7.\n- Millions: 5.\n- Hundred thousands: 7.\n- Ten thousands: 6.\n- Thousands: 9.\n- Hundreds: 7.\n- Tens: 0.\n- Ones: 5.\n\nSecond number: 659,353,361.\n\nBreaking down:.\n\n- Hundred millions: 6.\n- Ten millions: 5.\n- Millions: 9.\n- Hundred thousands: 3.\n- Ten thousands: 5.\n- Thousands: 3.\n- Hundreds: 3.\n- Tens: 6.\n- Ones: 1.\n\nWait a second, so when adding, each corresponding place should be added. Let me start over step by step, digit by digit from right to left (starting at ones):.\n\nOnes place: 5 (from first) +1 (from second) =6. Write down 6. No carryover.\n\nTens place: 0 +6 =6. Write 6. Still no carryover.\n\nHundreds place:7 (from first) +3 (from second)=10. So write 0, carryover 1.\n\nThousands place:9 (first) +3 (second) =12, plus the carried 1 makes 13. So write 3, carryover 1.\n\nTen thousands place:6 (first) +5 (second)=11, plus 1 =12. Write 2, carryover1.\n\nHundred thousands place:7 (first) +3 (second)=10, plus 1 =11. Write 1, carryover1.\n\nMillions place:5 (first's millions place is 5? Wait, let me check again:.\n\nWait the first number's millions place is 5 (since it's 275, so the millions digit is 5?), but let me confirm:.\n\nWait the first number is 275,769,705. The breakdown:.\n\n2 (hundred millions), 7 (ten millions), 5 (millions), 7 (hundred thousands), 6 (ten thousands), 9 (thousands), 7 (hundreds), 0 (tens), 5 (ones). So the millions place is the third digit from the left, which is 5.\n\nThe second number's millions place is the third digit from the left, which is 9 (since it's 659, so 9 in millions). .\n\nSo millions place: 5 (first) +9 (second) =14, plus the carried over 1 (from hundred thousands place) would be 15. So that's 5 in millions, carryover 1.\n\nWait, but in my previous step, the hundred thousands place was 7 +3 (first and second numbers) which is 10 plus 1 carried over? Wait, let me retrace.\n\nWait the hundred thousands place: first number has 7 (hundred thousands), second has 3 (hundred thousands). So 7+3=10, plus the carryover from the ten thousands place (which was 1 from the 12 in ten thousands). Wait, the ten thousands place was 6 (first) +5 (second) =11, so that would carryover 1, so when adding the hundred thousands, we have to add that carryover?.\n\nWait, perhaps I need to list each step carefully:.\n\nStarting over step by step, adding each digit position with the carryover:.\n\nPositions (from right to left, starting at ones):.\n\n1. Ones: 5 +1 =6. Write 6, carryover 0.\n\n2. Tens: 0 +6 =6. Write 6, carryover 0.\n\n3. Hundreds:7 +3 =10. Write 0, carryover 1.\n\n4. Thousands:9 +3 =12 + 1 (carryover) =13. Write 3, carryover 1.\n\n5. Ten thousands:6 +5 =11 +1 (carryover) =12. Write 2, carryover 1.\n\n6. Hundred thousands:7 +3 =10 +1 (carryover) =11. Write 1, carryover 1.\n\n7. Millions:5 +9 =14 +1 (carryover) =15. Write 5, carryover 1.\n\n8. Ten millions:7 (from first number's ten millions place) +5 (from second number's ten millions place) =12 +1 (carryover)=13. Write 3, carryover 1.\n\n9. Hundred millions:2 (first) +6 (second) =8 +1 (carryover) =9. Write 9, no carryover.\n\nPutting all the digits together from last to first (since we started from ones):.\n\nThe digits written in each place are:.\n\nOnes:6, Tens:6, Hundreds:0, Thousands:3, Ten thousands:2, Hundred thousands:1, Millions:5, Ten millions:3, Hundred millions:9.\n\nSo the number is 9 (hundred millions), 3 (ten millions), 5 (millions), 1 (hundred thousands), 2 (ten thousands), 3 (thousands), 0 (hundreds), 6 (tens), 6 (ones). So, let me directly conclude the answer. The answer is "]]
