t down digit by digit (from right to left):.\n\nPosition 1 (ones): 1.\n\nPosition 2 (tens): 6.\n\nPosition 3 (hundreds): 6.\n\nPosition 4 (thousands): 2.\n\nPosition 5 (ten thousands): 6.\n\nPosition 6 (hundred thousands): 7.\n\nPosition 7 (millions): 0.\n\nPosition 8 (ten millions): 0.\n\nPosition 9 (hundred millions): 9.\n\nWait, actually, let me count the digits properly. Let's see:.\n\nThe first number is 900,762,661. Let's count the digits without commas:.\n\n9 0 0 7 6 2 6 6 1. So that's 9 digits. So the places are:.\n\nBillions (position 9): 9.\n\nHundred millions (8): 0.\n\nTen millions (7): 0.\n\nMillions (6):7.\n\nHundred thousands (5):6.\n\nTen thousands (4):2.\n\nThousands (3):6.\n\nHundreds (2):6.\n\nTens (1):1.\n\nWait, maybe I mixed up the order here. Let's clarify:.\n\nStarting from the right (ones place as position 1):.\n\nPosition 1: 1 (ones).\n\nPosition 2: 6 (tens).\n\nPosition 3: 6 (hundreds).\n\nPosition 4: 2 (thousands).\n\nPosition 5: 6 (ten thousands).\n\nPosition 6:7 (hundred thousands).\n\nPosition 7:0 (millions).\n\nPosition 8:0 (ten millions).\n\nPosition 9:9 (hundred millions).\n\nSimilarly, the second number is 365,892,163.\n\nBreaking it down digit by digit (without commas):.\n\n3 6 5 8 9 2 1 6 3.\n\nWait, that's 9 digits as well. Let me list them from right to left (positions 1 to 9):.\n\nPosition 1:3 (ones).\n\nPosition 2:6 (tens).\n\nPosition 3:1 (hundreds).\n\nPosition 4:2 (thousands).\n\nPosition 5:9 (ten thousands).\n\nPosition 6:8 (hundred thousands).\n\nPosition 7:5 (millions).\n\nPosition 8:6 (ten millions).\n\nPosition 9:3 (hundred millions).\n\nWait, let me confirm that again. Let's write the second number as 365,892,163:.\n\nBreaking down with commas:.\n\n3 (hundred millions), 65 (ten millions and millions?), Wait, commas are placed every three digits. So 365,892,163 is:.\n\n3 (hundred millions), 65 (ten millions and millions?), actually:.\n\n3 6 5 , 8 9 2 , 1 6 3.\n\nSo the digits from left to right are:.\n\n3 (hundred millions), 6 (ten millions), 5 (millions), 8 (hundred thousands), 9 (ten thousands), 2 (thousands), 1 (hundreds), 6 (tens), 3 (ones). Wait, that would be:.\n\nPosition 1 (ones): 3.\n\nPosition 2 (tens):6.\n\nPosition 3 (hundreds):1.\n\nPosition 4 (thousands):2.\n\nPosition 5 (ten thousands):9.\n\nPosition 6 (hundred thousands):8.\n\nPosition 7 (millions):5.\n\nPosition 8 (ten millions):6.\n\nPosition 9 (hundred millions):3.\n\nYes, that's correct. So now, adding each column starting from position 1:.\n\nLet's list each digit position for both numbers:.\n\nPosition | Number1 Digit | Number2 Digit | Sum | Carry.\n---|---|---|---|---.\n1 (ones) | 1 | 3 | 4 | 0.\n2 (tens) |6 |6 |12 |1 (carryover 1).\n3 (hundreds)|6 |1 |7 + carryover 1 =8 |0.\n4 (thousands)|2 |2 |4 |0.\n5 (ten thousands)|6 |9 |15 |1.\n6 (hundred thousands)|7 |8 |15 +1 (carryover?) Wait, need to check.\n\nWait, let me go step by step.\n\nStarting with Position 1 (ones):.\n\nNumber1: 1, Number2:3 → 1+3=4. Write 4, carryover 0.\n\nPosition 2 (tens):.\n\n6 + 6 = 12. Write down 2, carryover 1.\n\nPosition 3 (hundreds):.\n\n6 (from number1) +1 (number2) =7, plus the carryover 1 makes 8. So 8, no carryover.\n\nPosition4 (thousands):.\n\n2 (number1) + 2 (number2)=4. Write 4, no carryover.\n\nPosition5 (ten thousands):.\n\n6 (number1) +9 (number2)=15. So write 5, carryover 1.\n\nPosition6 (hundred thousands):.\n\n7 (number1) +8 (number2)=15, plus carryover 1 → 16. So write 6, carryover 1.\n\nPosition7 (millions):.\n\n0 (number1) +5 (number2)=5, plus carryover 1 →6. Write 6, no carryover.\n\nPosition8 (ten millions):.\n\n0 (number1) +6 (number2)=6. Write 6, no carryover.\n\nPosition9 (hundred millions):.\n\n9 (number1) +3 (number2)=12. So write 2 and carryover 1. But since this is the highest digit, we need to add that carryover as a new digit at the beginning.\n\nWait, so the hundreds of millions place addition gives 9+3=12, plus no carryover from the previous step (since position8 had no carryover). Wait, let's che