on4:1\n\nPosition5:0\n\nPosition6:6\n\nPosition7:3 (from 2+0+1 carryover?)\n\nWait let's see:\n\nThe millions place after adding:\n\nMillions digit (position6) was 6 (from 16, so write6, carryover1).\n\nTen millions digit (position7): 2 (from first number's ten millions) +0 (second's ten millions) +1 (carryover) =3.\n\nSo the number is:\n\n3 (ten millions), 6 (millions),0 (hundred thousands),1 (ten thousands),5 (thousands),0 (hundreds),9 (tens),8 (ones).\n\nSo that's 36,015,098. Thus total is 1,000,000,000 +36,015,098 =1,036,015,098. So that matches the breakdown method.\n\nSo the digit-by-digit method must have an error.\n\nLooking back at the digit-by-digit calculation:\n\nAfter adding all the digits, the result was 1,030,150,908.\n\nBut according to the other methods, it should be 1,036,015,098. So there's a mistake in the digit-by-digit.\n\nAh! I see the mistake now. In the digit-by-digit calculation, when adding the hundred millions place (position8), which is the 8th digit from the right (since positions start at 0), the first number has 2 in the hundred millions place, and the second has8, so 2+8=10, so we write 0 and carryover1 to the next (billions) place. That's correct. But the problem is in the millions place.\n\nWait let me go through the digit-by-digit again with more attention:\n\nPosition6 (millions place):\n\nFirst number's digit at position6 is6.\n\nSecond number's digit at position6 is9 (since in the second number, the digits are:\n\nPosition0:7, 1:9, 2:8, 3:6,4:1,5:2,6:9,7:0,8:8.\n\nSo position6 is the millions place of the second number, which is9.\n\nSo adding position6:6 (first) +9 (second) plus any carryover from position5.\n\nPosition5 (hundred thousands):\n\nFirst number:7, second:2. So 7+2=9 plus carryover from position4.\n\nWait let's go step by step:\n\nStarting from position0 to position8:\n\nPosition0:1+7=8 → no carryover.\n\nPosition1:0+9=9 → no.\n\nPosition2:2+8=10 → carryover1.\n\nPosition3:8+6=14 +1=15 → carryover1.\n\nPosition4:9+1=10 +1=11 → carryover1.\n\nPosition5:7+2=9 +1=10 → carryover1.\n\nPosition6:6+9=15 +1=16 → carryover1.\n\nPosition7:2+0=2 +1=3 → no carryover.\n\nPosition8:2+8=10 → carryover1.\n\nSo the carryover at position6 is correct.\n\nNow, the millions digit (position6) is16 →6 with carryover1.\n\nThen position7 (ten millions):\n\n2 (first) +0 (second) +1 (carryover) =3 → no carryover.\n\nPosition8 (hundred millions):2+8=10 →0 with carryover1.\n\nSo the digits are:\n\nPosition9 (billions):1.\n\nPosition8:0.\n\nPosition7:3.\n\nPosition6:6.\n\nPosition5:0 (from position5's total 10, so 0 with carryover).\n\nPosition4:1 (from position4's total 11 →1 with carryover).\n\nPosition3:5 (from 15).\n\nPosition2:0 (from10).\n\nPosition1:9.\n\nPosition0:8.\n\nSo the number is 1,030,150,908.\n\nWait but according to the other methods, it should be 1,036,015,098.\n\nWait, the difference is in the millions digit. In the digit-by-digit, the millions digit is6, but in the breakdown method, the millions part was 36,015,098, so the millions digit is6 (as in 36 million), so that's consistent. Wait, but the total in breakdown is1,036,015,098, which is:\n\n1,036,015,098 → breakdown:\n\n1,000,000,000 +\n\n36,000,000 +\n\n15,000 +\n\n98.\n\nWait, so the millions part is36 million, so the millions digit (position6) is6 (since 36 million is 3 in ten millions,6 in millions). So the digit-by-digit calculation has 3 in ten millions (position7) and6 in millions (position6). That matches. So then why the discrepancy in the total?\n\nWait, let's write the digit-by-digit result as 1,030,150,908. Wait that's 1,030 million plus 150,908. But according to the other method, it's1,036 million plus15,098.\n\nAh! Here's the mistake.\n\nWait the digit-by-digit result is 1,030,150,908. Let me parse that:\n\nBreaking down 1,030,150,908:\n\n1,000,000,000 (1 billion)\n\n+30,000,000 (30 million)\n\n+150,908.\n\nTotal:1,030,150,908.\n\nBut according to the other methods, it should be1,036,015,098.\n\nWait the difference is in the ten millions place: digit-by-digit has 30 million (3 in ten millions place), but the correct is36 million (3 in ten millions,6 in millions). Wait no, the ten millions place in the digit-by-digit is3 (position7), which is the ten millions digit. So 3 ten millions is 30 million. The millions digit is6, which is6 million. So total from those two places is36 million. So the total is1,036,015,098.\n\nWait wait a second. The digit-by-digit result is1,030,150,908.\n\nWait how is that structured?\n\nLet me write the number digit by digit:\n\nPosition9 (billions):1 → 1,000,000,000.\n\nPosition8 (hundred millions):0 →0.\n\nPosition7 (ten millions):3 →30,000,000.\n\nPosition6 (millions):6 →6,000,000.\n\nPosition5 (hundred thousands):0 →0.\n\nPosition4 (ten thousands):1 →10,000.\n\nPosition3 (thousands):5 →5,000.\n\nPosition2 (hundreds):0 →0.\n\nPosition1 (tens):9 →90.\n\nPosition0 (ones):8 →8.\n\nSo adding all together:\n\n1,000,000,000\n\n+30,000,000\n\n+6,000,000\n\n+10,000\n\n+5,000\n\n+90\n\n+8 =\n\n1,000,000,000 +36,000,000 =1,036,000,000.\n\nThen +15,000 (10,000+5,000) =1,036,015,000.\n\nThen +98 →1,036,015,098.\n\nWait a minute! Oh! I see now. The digit-by-digit method was written as 1,030,150,908, which is incorrect. The correct number should be 1,036,015,098.\n\nThe mistake was in the way I wrote the digits. Let me see:\n\nThe digits from position9 to position0 are:\n\nPosition9:1 (billions place)\n\nPosition8:0 (hundred millions)\n\nPosition7:3 (ten millions)\n\nPosition6:6 (millions)\n\nPosition5:0 (hundred thousands)\n\nPosition4:1 (ten thousands)\n\nPosition3:5 (thousands)\n\nPosition2:0 (hundreds)\n\nPosition1:9 (tens)\n\nPosition0:8 (ones).\n\nSo writing this number:\n\n1 (billion), 0 (hundred millions), 3 (ten millions),6 (millions),0 (hundred thousands),1 (ten thousands),5 (thousands),0 (hundreds),9 (tens),8 (ones).\n\nWhich is 1,036,015,098.\n\nAh! I see. My mistake was in the initial interpretation of the digits after calculation. I incorrectly wrot