ousands), 9 (ten thousands), 8 (hundred thousands), 7 (millions), 3 (ten millions), 3 (hundred millions? Wait, no, let me count again.\n\nWait, 378,191,471:\n\nStarting from the right:\n\nPosition 1: 1 (ones)\n\n2: 7 (tens)\n\n3: 4 (hundreds)\n\n4: 1 (thousands)\n\n5: 9 (ten thousands)\n\n6: 8 (hundred thousands)\n\n7: 7 (millions)\n\n8: 3 (ten millions)\n\nWait, but the number is 378,191,471. Let me parse it with commas:\n\n378,191,471 is 3 hundred seventy-eight million, one hundred ninety-one thousand, four hundred seventy-one.\n\nSo breaking down each digit:\n\nBillions place: 3 (since it's 3 hundred million)\n\nHundred millions: 7\n\nTen millions: 8\n\nMillions place: 1\n\nHundred thousands: 9\n\nTen thousands: 1\n\nThousands: 4\n\nHundreds: 7\n\nTens: 4? Wait, maybe I'm getting confused here. Let me write each digit position step by step.\n\nWait, perhaps a better approach is to write both numbers digit by digit, aligning them properly.\n\nLet me write both numbers with their digits separated:\n\nFirst number: 3 7 8 1 9 1 4 7 1\n\nSecond number:2 9 6 7 3 3 9 2 5\n\nWait, but maybe the numbers have different lengths? Wait, both are 9 digits. Let me confirm:\n\n378,191,471 is 3 (hundred million), 7 (ten million), 8 (million), 1 (hundred thousand), 9 (ten thousand), 1 (thousand), 4 (hundred), 7 (ten), 1 (one).\n\nSecond number: 296,733,925 is 2 (hundred million), 9 (ten million), 6 (million), 7 (hundred thousand), 3 (ten thousand), 3 (thousand), 9 (hundred), 2 (ten), 5 (one).\n\nSo when adding, align each digit:\n\nHundred millions: 3 + 2 = 5?\n\nWait, let me do it step by step from the right (ones digit):\n\nOnes place:\n\nFirst number: 1\n\nSecond number:5\n\n1+5=6. Write down 6.\n\nTens place:\n\n7 + 2 =9. Write down 9.\n\nHundreds place:\n\n4 +9 =13. Wait, wait, hold on. Wait the hundreds digit in the first number is 4 (from 471), and the hundreds digit in the second number is 9 (from 925). So 4 +9 =13. So write down 3, carry over 1.\n\nThousands place: First number's thousands digit is 1 (from 1,471) and second number's thousands digit is 3 (from 3,925). So 1 + 3 =4 plus the carried 1 makes 5. Write down 5, no carryover.\n\nTen thousands place: First number has 9 (from 19,1471?), wait let me recheck.\n\nWait, let's parse each number properly:\n\nFirst number: 3 7 8 1 9 1 4 7 1\n\nBreaking down each digit from left to right (hundred millions to ones):\n\n1. Hundred millions: 3\n\n2. Ten millions:7\n\n3. Millions:8\n\n4. Hundred thousands:1\n\n5. Ten thousands:9\n\n6. Thousands:1\n\n7. Hundreds:4\n\n8. Tens:7\n\n9. Ones:1\n\nWait, that can't be right. Let's see: 378,191,471\n\nBreaking into groups:\n\n378 (million), 191 (thousand), 471.\n\nSo:\n\n- 3 (hundred million)\n\n- 7 (ten million)\n\n- 8 (million)\n\n- 1 (hundred thousand)\n\n- 9 (ten thousand)\n\n- 1 (thousand)\n\n- 4 (hundred)\n\n- 7 (ten)\n\n- 1 (one)\n\nYes, that's correct.\n\nSecond number:296,733,925\n\nBreaking down:\n\n2 (hundred million)\n\n9 (ten million)\n\n6 (million)\n\n7 (hundred thousand)\n\n3 (ten thousand)\n\n3 (thousand)\n\n9 (hundred)\n\n2 (ten)\n\n5 (one)\n\nSo now, adding each digit position from right (ones) to left (hundred millions):\n\nStarting with ones:\n\n1 (from first) +5 (second) =6\n\nTens:7 +2=9\n\nHundreds:4 +9=13. Write 3, carryover 1.\n\nThousands:1 (first) +3 (second) +1 (carryover)=5. Write 5, no carryover.\n\nTen thousands:9 (first) +3 (second)=12. Write 2, carryover 1.\n\nHundred thousands:1 (first) +7 (second)=8 + carryover 1 =9. Write 9.\n\nMillions:8 (first) +6 (second)=14. Write 4, carryover 1.\n\nTen millions:7 (first) +9 (second)=16 +1 (carryover)=17. Write 7, carryover1.\n\nHundred millions:3 +2=5 +1=6. Write 6.\n\nPutting it all together:\n\nStarting from the right, the digits are:\n\nOnes:6\n\nTens:9\n\nHundreds:3\n\nThousands:5\n\nTen thousands:2\n\nHundred thousands:9\n\nMillions:4\n\nTen millions:7\n\nHundred millions:6\n\nWait, so the total would be 6 (hundred millions), 7 (ten millions), 4 (millions), 9 (hundred