 (ones).\n\n2:4 (tens).\n\n3:9 (hundreds).\n\n4:2 (thousands).\n\n5:4 (ten thousands).\n\n6:6 (hundred thousands).\n\n7:6 (millions).\n\n8:7 (ten millions).\n\n9:9 (hundred millions).\n\nSecond number (374,119,888):.\n\nPositions:.\n\n1:8 (ones).\n\n2:8 (tens).\n\n3:8 (hundreds).\n\n4:9 (thousands).\n\n5:1 (ten thousands).\n\n6:1 (hundred thousands).\n\n7:4 (millions).\n\n8:7 (ten millions).\n\n9:3 (hundred millions).\n\nWait, so let's go step by step:.\n\nPosition 1 (ones):.\n\n9 (first) +8 (second) =17 → write 7, carryover 1.\n\nPosition 2 (tens):.\n\n4 (first) +8 (second) =12 +1 (carry) =13 → write 3, carryover 1.\n\nPosition3 (hundreds):.\n\n9 (first) +8 (second) =17 +1 (carry) =18 → write 8, carryover1.\n\nPosition4 (thousands):.\n\n2 (first) +9 (second)=11 +1=12 → write 2, carryover1.\n\nPosition5 (ten thousands):.\n\n4 (first) +1 (second)=5 +1=6 → write 6, no carryover.\n\nPosition6 (hundred thousands):.\n\n6 (first) +1 (second)=7 → write7.\n\nPosition7 (millions):.\n\n6 (first) +4 (second)=10 → write0, carryover1.\n\nWait, 6+4 is 10, so yes, write 0, carryover1.\n\nPosition8 (ten millions):.\n\n7 (first) +7 (second)=14 +1 (carry)=15 → write5, carryover1.\n\nPosition9 (hundred millions):.\n\n9 (first) +3 (second)=12 +1 (carry)=13 → write 3, carryover1. But since there are no more digits, we need to add that carryover as a new digit.\n\nWait, so the total would be 1 3 followed by the rest. Wait let me check again.\n\nWait, the highest digit is hundred millions (position9). Adding their digits plus any carryover from position8.\n\nSo position9: 9 +3 +1 (carry from position8) =13. So that gives 3 in the hundred millions place, and a carryover of 1 to a new billion place.\n\nTherefore, the total sum would have a 1 in the billions place, followed by the 3 from the hundred millions, then the previous digits.\n\nPutting it all together:.\n\nStarting from the right:.\n\nPositions:.\n\n1:7.\n\n2:3.\n\n3:8.\n\n4:2.\n\n5:6.\n\n6:7.\n\n7:0 (from millions place).\n\n8:5 (