us the carryover 1 makes 9. So 9, no carryover. That's correct. So no carryover here.\n\nSo Position4: 9, carryover 0.\n\nPosition5 (ten thousands place): first number's ten thousands digit is 9, second's is8. So 9+8=17. Since there's no carryover from previous, it's 17. So write 7 and carryover1.\n\nPosition6 (hundred thousands place): first number's hundred thousands digit is8, second's is3. 8+3=11, plus the carryover1 makes 12. So write 2, carryover1.\n\nPosition7 (millions place): first number's millions digit is9, second's is9. 9+9=18, plus 1 →19. So write 9, carryover1.\n\nPosition8 (ten millions place): first number's ten millions digit is8, second's is4. 8+4=12, plus carryover1 →13. So write 3, carryover1.\n\nPosition9 (hundred millions place): first number's hundred millions digit is1, second's is2. 1+2=3 plus carryover1 →4. So 4.\n\nPutting all together from right to left:\n\nPositions 1-9 digits are:\n\nPosition1 (ones): 2\n\nPosition2 (tens):7\n\nPosition3 (hundreds):1\n\nPosition4 (thousands):9\n\nPosition5 (ten thousands):7\n\nPosition6 (hundred thousands):2\n\nPosition7 (millions):9\n\nPosition8 (ten millions):3\n\nPosition9 (hundred millions):4\n\nSo combining these digits: 4 (hundred millions), 3 (ten millions), 9 (millions), 2 (hundred thousands), 7 (ten thousands), 9 (thousands), 1 (hundreds), 7 (tens), 2 (ones). Wait, let me arrange them properly:\n\nThe digits from highest to lowest (hundred millions to ones):\n\n4 (hundred millions), 3 (ten millions), 9 (millions), 2 (hundred thousands), 7 (ten thousands), 9 (thousands), 1 (hundreds), 7 (tens), 2 (ones).\n\nWait, but let me check the order again:\n\nPosition9 is hundred millions (4)\n\nPosition8 is ten millions (3)\n\nPosition7 is millions (9)\n\nPosition6 is hundred thousands (2)\n\nPosition5 is ten thousands (7)\n\nPosition4 is thousands (9)\n\nPosition3 is hundreds (1)\n\nPosition2 is tens (7)\n\nPosition1 is ones (2)\n\nSo the number is 439,279,172.\n\nWait, let's see:\n\nBreaking into the 