1:6.\n\nNumber2:0 → 6+0=6.\n\nPosition2 (Tens):.\n\nNumber1:3.\n\nNumber2:3 → 3+3=6.\n\nPosition3 (Hundreds):.\n\nNumber1:1.\n\nNumber2:6 →1+6=7.\n\nPosition4 (Thousands):.\n\nNumber1:0.\n\nNumber2:7 →0+7=7.\n\nPosition5 (Ten thousands):.\n\nNumber1:3.\n\nNumber2:4 →3+4=7.\n\nPosition6 (Hundred thousands):.\n\nNumber1:1.\n\nNumber2:5 →1+5=6.\n\nPosition7 (Millions):.\n\nNumber1:2.\n\nNumber2:1 →2+1=3.\n\nPosition8 (Ten millions):.\n\nNumber1:8.\n\nNumber2:0 →8+0=8.\n\nPosition9 (Hundred millions):.\n\nNumber1:2.\n\nNumber2:6 →2+6=8.\n\nWait, but when adding, do we have any carries over?.\n\nWait, hold on! I think I made a mistake here. I assumed that each digit adds without any carryover. But in reality, when adding digits, if the sum is 10 or more, we need to carry over to the next higher place.\n\nWait, in my previous steps, I just added each digit pair and wrote the sum, but maybe some of those sums are 10 or more. Let me recheck each position with possible carries.\n\nStarting over, step by step, considering carries.\n\nStarting from the rightmost digit (position1, ones place):.\n\nPosition1 (Ones):.\n\n6 (from first number) + 0 (second) =6. No carryover. Write 6.\n\nPosition2 (Tens):.\n\n3 +3=6. Still 6. No carryover. Write 6.\n\nPosition3 (Hundreds):.\n\n1 +6=7. Write 7. No carryover.\n\nPosition4 (Thousands):.\n\n0 +7=7. Write 7. No carryover.\n\nPosition5 (Ten thousands):.\n\n3 +4=7. Write 7. No carryover.\n\nPosition6 (Hundred thousands):.\n\n1 +5=6. Write 6. No carryover.\n\nPosition7 (Millions):.\n\n2 +1=3. Write 3. No carryover.\n\nPosition8 (Ten millions):.\n\n8 +0=8. Write 8. No carryover.\n\nPosition9 (Hundred millions):.\n\n2 +6=8. Write 8. No carryover.\n\nWait, so in this case, none of the digits added up to 10 or more, so no carries were needed. That's interesting. So, let me directly conclude the answer. The answer is "]]
