1 is 11. So write 1, carry over 1.\n\nTen-millions place: First number has a 7, second has a 9. 7 + 9 = 16, plus the carried 1 makes 17. So write down 7 and carry over 1.\n\nHundred-millions place: The first number has a 2, and the second has a 4. 2 + 4 = 6, plus the carried 1 equals 7. So that's 7 in the hundred-millions place.\n\nWait, let me check if there are any more digits. The first number is 276,903,495 which is 9 digits, and the second is 449,467,824, also 9 digits. So we should be done. Let me recount each digit step to make sure I didn't make a mistake.\n\nStarting over step by step:.\n\nNumbers:.\n276,903,495.\n+449,467,824.\n-------------.\nLet's break it down digit by digit from right to left (ones to hundred-millions):.\n\n1. Ones place: 5 + 4 = 9. Correct.\n2. Tens: 9 + 2 = 11. Write 1, carryover 1. Good.\n3. Hundreds: 4 + 8 = 12 + 1 =13. Write 3, carryover 1. Right.\n4. Thousands: 3 +7 =10 +1=11. Write 1, carryover 1. Okay.\n5. Ten-thousands: 0 +6 =6 +1=7. No carry. That's right.\n6. Hundred-thousands: 9 +4 =13. Write 3, carryover 1. Wait, the first number's hundred-thousands place is 9? Let me confirm the digits again. Let's parse each number:.\n\nFirst number: 276,903,495. Breaking down:.\n\nDigits from right (position 1 to 9):.\n\nPosition 1 (ones):5.\n2:9.\n3:4.\n4:3.\n5:0.\n6:9.\n7:6.\n8:7.\n9:2.\n\nWait, maybe I messed up the places. Let me write them out properly. Let's write each number digit by digit:.\n\nFirst number: 2 7 6 9 0 3 4 9 5.\n\nWait, actually, when written as 276,903,495, commas separate every three digits. So:.\n\n2 (hundred millions), 7 (ten millions), 6 (millions), 9 (hundred thousands), 0 (ten thousands), 3 (thousands), 4 (hundreds), 9 (tens), 5 (ones). So the places are:.\n\nDigit positions (from right, starting at 1):.\n\nPosition 1 (ones):5.\n\nPosition 2 (tens):9.\n\nPosition 3 (hundreds):4.\n\nPosition 4 (thousands):3.\n\nPosition 5 (ten thousands):0.\n\nPosition 6 (hundred thousands):9.\n\nPosition 7 (millions):6.\n\nPosition 8 (ten millions):7.\n\nPosition 9 (hundred millions):2.\n\nSecond number: 449,467,824.\n\nBreaking down:.\n\nDigits: 4 (hundred millions), 4 (ten millions), 9 (millions), 4 (hundred thousands), 6 (ten thousands), 7 (thousands), 8 (hundreds), 2 (tens), 4 (ones). So positions:.\n\nPosition 1 (ones):4.\n\nPosition 2 (tens):2.\n\nPosition 3 (hundreds):8.\n\nPosition 4 (thousands):7.\n\nPosition 5 (ten thousands):6.\n\nPosition 6 (hundred thousands):4.\n\nPosition 7 (millions):9.\n\nPosition 8 (ten millions):4.\n\nPosition 9 (hundred millions):4.\n\nOkay, so let's redo the addition step by step with correct digit positions:.\n\nStarting from position 1 (ones):.\n\n1. Ones: 5 +4 =9. Correct.\n\n2. Tens:9 (from first) +2 (from second) =11. Write 1, carryover 1.\n\n3. Hundreds:4 +8 =12 +1=13. Write 3, carryover1.\n\n4. Thousands:3 +7 =10 +1=11. Write1, carryover1.\n\n5. Ten thousands:0 +6 =6 +1=7. No carry.\n\n6. Hundred thousands:9 +4 =13. Write3, carryover1.\n\n7. Millions:6 +9 =15 +1=16. Write6, carryover1.\n\nWait, 6 (first number's millions place) plus 9 (second's millions place) is 15, plus the carryover 1 makes 16. So write 6, carryover1. Hmm, that's different from before. Wait, in my previous calculation, I thought the hundred thousands place was 9, but here, the millions place is next. Let me clarify.\n\nWait, position 7 is millions. First number's millions digit is 6, second's is 9. So 6+9=15, plus carryover 1 is 16. So millions place becomes 6, carryover 1. Then moving to the ten millions place.\n\n8. Ten millions:7 (first) +4 (second) =11 + carryover1=12. So write 2, carryover1.\n\n9. Hundred millions:2 +4 =6 + carryover1=7. So 7. .\n\nPutting all together, the digits from right to left (positions 1 to 9):.\n\nPosition 1:9.\n\n2:1.\n\n3:3.\n\n4:1.\n\n5:7.\n\n6:3.\n\n7:6.\n\n8:2.\n\n9:7.\n\nSo combining these digits: 7 (hundred millions), 2 (ten millions), 6 (millions), 3 (hundred thousands), 7 (ten thousands), 3 (thousands), 1 (hundreds), 1 (tens), 9 (o