millions digits are 4 and 1. Adding those: 4 +1 =5, plus the carried over 1 from the previous step (which was from the ten millions place addition). Wait, let me retrace.\n\nWait, the ten millions place was 4 (from first number's ten millions place?) Wait no, let me actually write down each digit step by step properly.\n\nWait maybe I messed up the place values earlier. Let me break down each number digit by digit:.\n\nFirst number: 452,287,775.\n\nBreaking into digits from right to left (since addition is done from right):.\n\nPosition 1 (ones): 5.\n\nPosition 2 (tens): 7.\n\n3 (hundreds):7.\n\n4 (thousands):7.\n\n5 (ten thousands):8.\n\n6 (hundred thousands):2.\n\n7 (millions):2.\n\n8 (ten millions):5.\n\n9 (hundred millions):4.\n\nSecond number:182,792,995.\n\nDigits:.\n\nPosition 1:5.\n\n2:9.\n\n3:9.\n\n4:2.\n\n5:7.\n\n6:9.\n\n7:8.\n\n8:1 (ten millions?).\n\nWait, let's list them properly:.\n\nFirst number: 4 5 2 2 8 7 7 7 5.\n\nWait maybe it's better to write each digit position:.\n\nLet's consider each number as 9 digits:.\n\nFirst number: 4 (hundred millions), 5 (ten millions), 2 (millions), 2 (hundred thousands), 8 (ten thousands), 7 (thousands), 7 (hundreds), 7 (tens), 5 (ones).\n\nSecond number:1 (hundred millions), 8 (ten millions), 2 (millions),7 (hundred thousands),9 (ten thousands),2 (thousands),9 (hundreds),9 (tens),5 (ones).\n\nSo aligning them:.\n\nHundred millions: 4 +1.\n\nTen millions:5 +8.\n\nMillions:2 +2.\n\nHundred thousands:2 +7.\n\nTen thousands:8 +9.\n\nThousands:7 +2.\n\nHundreds:7 +9.\n\nTens:7 +9.\n\nOnes:5 +5.\n\nWait, but when adding, we start from the ones and move left, carrying over as needed. Let me start over with the step-by-step addition:.\n\nStarting with the ones place:.\n\nOnes: 5 +5 =10 → write 0, carryover 1.\n\nTens:7 +9 =16, plus 1 →17 → write 7, carryover 1.\n\nHundreds:7 +9 =16 +1 →17 → write7, carryover1.\n\nThousands:7 +2=9 +1 →10 → write0, carryover1.\n\nTen thousands:8 +9=17 +1 →18 → write8, carryover1.\n\nHundred thousands:2 +7=9 +1 →10 → write0, carryover1.\n\nMillions:2 +2=4 +1 →5 → write5, no carryover.\n\nTen millions:5 +8=13 → write3, carryover1.\n\nHundred millions:4 +1=5 +1 (carryover) →6 → write6.\n\nWait, hold on, let me check each step again carefully:.\n\nStarting over:.\n\nOnes digit:.\n\nFirst number:5, second:5 →5+5=10. So 0, carryover 1.\n\nTens digit:.\n\nFirst number's tens digit is 7, second's is 9 →7+9=16. Add the carried 1 →17. So 7, carryover 1.\n\nHundreds digit:.\n\nFirst number's hundreds is7, second's hundreds is9 →7+9=16. Add 1 →17 →7, carryover1.\n\nThousands digit:.\n\nFirst number's thousands digit is7, second's thousands digit is2 →7+2=9. Add the carried 1 →10 →0, carryover1.\n\nTen thousands digit:.\n\nFirst number has 8 (ten thousands place), second has9 →8+9=17. Add 1 →18 →8, carryover1.\n\nHundred thousands digit:.\n\nFirst number has2 (hundred thousands place?), let me confirm:.\n\nWait, first number breakdown again:.\n\nFirst number: 452,287,775.\n\nBreaking down:.\n\nDigits from right:.\n\nPosition 1:5 (ones).\n\n2:7 (tens).\n\n3:7 (hundreds).\n\n4:7 (thousands).\n\n5:8 (ten thousands).\n\n6:2 (hundred thousands).\n\n7:2 (millions).\n\n8:5 (ten millions).\n\n9:4 (hundred millions).\n\nSo the hundred thousands place is the 6th digit from the right, which is 2 in first number, and in the second number:.\n\nSecond number:182,792,995.\n\nDigits from right:.\n\nPosition 1:5.\n\n2:9 (tens).\n\n3:9 (hundreds).\n\n4:2 (thousands).\n\n5:7 (ten thousands).\n\n6:9 (hundred thousands).\n\nWait, second number's hundred thousands place (6th digit from right):.\n\nLet's see, the second number is 182,792,995. Let's write digits:.\n\nDigits from left to right:1 (hundred millions),8 (ten millions),2 (millions),7 (hundred thousands),9 (ten thousands),2 (thousands),9 (hundreds),9 (tens),5 (ones).\n\nWait, so the hundred thousands place is the 4th digit from the left? Hmm, perhaps I should list each digit's place clearly.\n\nAlternatively, let's list each digit's place for both numbers:.\n\nFirst number (452,287,775):.\n\nDigit positions (from right, starting at 1):.\n\n1:5 (ones).\n\n2:7 (tens).\n\n3:7 (hundreds).\n\n4:7 (thousands).\n\n5:8 (ten thousands).\n\n6:2 (hundred thousands).\n\n7:2 (millions).\n\n8:5 (ten millions).\n\n9:4 (hundred millions).\n\nSecond number (182,792,995):.\n\nDigit positions (from right):.\n\n1:5 (ones).\n\n2:9 (tens).\n\n3:9 (hundreds).\n\n4:2 (thousands).\n\n5:7 (ten thousands).\n\n6:9 (hundred thousands).\n\n7:2 (millions).\n\n8:8 (ten millions).\n\n9:1 (hundred millions).\n\nSo, going back to the addition:.\n\nContinuing from Ten thousands place (position5):.\n\nFirst number's ten thousands:8, second's ten thousands:7 →8+7=15? Wait no, wait second's ten thousands digit is position5 from right: in second number, position5 is ten thousands place, which is 7 (since the second number's digits from the right are:.\n\nPosition5: ten thousands digit is the fifth from the right, which is the '7' in 182,792,995. Wait the number is 1 (hundred millions),8 (ten millions),2 (millions),7 (hundred thousands),9 (ten thousands),2 (thousands),9 (hundreds),9 (tens),5 (ones). Wait, perhaps I made an error here.\n\nWait the second number is 182,792,995.\n\nBreaking it down:.\n\n- 1 hundred million (100,000,000).\n\n- 8 ten millions (80,000,000).\n\n- 2 millions (2,000,000).\n\n- 7 hundred thousands (700,000).\n\n- 9 ten thousands (90,000).\n\n- 2 thousands (2,000).\n\n- 9 hundreds (900).\n\n- 9 tens (90).\n\n- 5 ones (5).\n\nTherefore, the ten thousands place (which is the 5th digit from the right) is 9 (since the ten thousands place is the fifth digit from the right, which is the '9' in the number). Wait, let's count:.\n\nStarting from the right (ones as position1):.\n\nPosition1:5 (ones).\n\nPosition2:9 (tens).\n\nPosition3:9 (hundreds).\n\nPosition4:2 (thousands).\n\nPosition5:9 (ten thousands?) Wait no, ten thousands is 10,000, so the fifth digit from the right would be the ten thousands place. Wait, let's see:.\n\nNumber is 1 8 2 7 9 2 9 9 5.\n\nWait written as:.\n\nDigits from left to right:.\n\n1 (hundred millions).\n\n8 (ten millions).\n\n2 (millions).\n\n7 (hundred thousands).\n\n9 (ten thousands).\n\n2 (thousands).\n\n9 (hundreds).\n\n9 (tens).\n\n5 (ones).\n\nSo the ten thousands place is the 5th digit from the left (since hundred millions is first). Wait maybe this is getting confusing. Let me instead list each digit's place clearly for both numbers.\n\nFirst number: 452,287,775.\n\nDigits from left (highest to lowest place):.\n\n4 - hundred millions.\n\n5 - ten millions.\n\n2 - millions.\n\n2 - hundred thousands.\n\n8 - ten thousands.\n\n7 - thousands.\n\n7 - hundreds.\n\n7 - tens.\n\n5 - ones.\n\nSecond number:182,792,995.\n\nDigits from left:.\n\n1 - hundred millions.\n\n8 - ten millions.\n\n2 - millions.\n\n7 - hundred thousands.\n\n9 - ten thousands.\n\n2 - thousands.\n\n9 - hundreds.\n\n9 - tens.\n\n5 - ones.\n\nSo when adding, let's pair each digit's place:.\n\nStarting from the ones:.\n\nOnes: 5 +5 =10 → 0, carryover1.\n\nTens:7 (first) +9 (second) =16 +1 →17 →7, carryover1.\n\nHundreds:7 +9=16 +1 →17 →7, carryover1.\n\nThousands:7 (first) +2 (second) =9 +1 →10 →0, carryover1.\n\nTen thousands:8 (first) +9 (second) =17 +1 →18 →8, carryover1.\n\nHundred thousands:2 (first) +7 (second) =9 +1 →10 →0, carryover1.\n\nMillions:2 (first) +2 (second) =4 +1 →5, no carryover.\n\nTen millions:5 (first) +8 (second) =13 →3, carryover1.\n\nHundred millions:4 (first) +1 (second) =5 +1 (carryover) →6, no further digits.\n\nPutting all together:.\n\nStarting from the right, the digits are:.\n\nOnes:0.\n\nTens:7.\n\nHundreds:7.\n\nThousands:0.\n\nTen thousands:8.\n\nHundred thousands:0.\n\nMillions:5.\n\nTen millions:3.\n\nHundred millions:6. So, let me directly conclude the answer. The answer is "]]
