tion3:2\n\nPosition4:2\n\nPosition5:1\n\nPosition6:5\n\nPosition7:2\n\nPosition8:6\n\nPosition9:7\n\nPlus the carryover 1 as the next digit (Position10:1)\n\nTherefore, writing the number from left to right (starting with the highest position):\n\nPosition10:1,\n\nPosition9:7,\n\nPosition8:6,\n\nPosition7:2,\n\nPosition6:5,\n\nPosition5:1,\n\nPosition4:2,\n\nPosition3:2,\n\nPosition2:3,\n\nPosition1:7.\n\nWait, that might be confusing. Let me structure it properly.\n\nThe result digits after each position are:\n\nStarting from the right (ones place):\n\nPosition1:7\n\nPosition2:3\n\nPosition3:2\n\nPosition4:2\n\nPosition5:1\n\nPosition6:5\n\nPosition7:2\n\nPosition8:6\n\nPosition9:7\n\nPlus the carryover 1 from Position9 becomes the new Position10.\n\nSo the full number from Position10 to Position1 is:\n\n1 (Position10),\n\n7 (Position9),\n\n6 (Position8),\n\n2 (Position7),\n\n5 (Position6),\n\n1 (Position5),\n\n2 (Position4),\n\n2 (Position3),\n\n3 (Position2),\n\n7 (Position1).\n\nSo writing this out as a number, left to right (starting from the highest place):\n\n1 followed by 7,6,2,5,1,2,2,3,7.\n\nWait, let me count the digits:\n\nPosition10:1,\n\nPosition9:7 → 10 digits total.\n\nSo the number is 1,762,512,237?\n\nWait, let's see:\n\nBreaking down the digits:\n\nPosition10:1 (billions place)\n\nPosition9:7 (hundred millions)\n\nPosition8:6 (ten millions)\n\nPosition7:2 (millions)\n\nPosition6:5 (hundred thousands)\n\nPosition5:1 (ten thousands)\n\nPosition4:2 (thousands)\n\nPosition3:2 (hundreds)\n\nPosition2:3 (tens)\n\nPosition1:7 (ones)\n\nSo putting commas:\n\n1,762,512,237.\n\nWait, let me confirm:\n\n1 (billion), 762 (million), 512 (thousand), 237.\n\nYes, that's 1,762,512,237.\n\nBut let me check with another method to be sure.\n\nAlternatively, adding the two numbers numerically:\n\nFirst number: 881,921,042\n\nSecond number:801,591,195\n\nAdding them:\n\nLet me do this step by step:\n\n881,921,042\n\n+801,591,195\n\n= ?\n\nLet me add the millions first