y:.\n\nFirst number: 8 1 5 0 6 6 4 5 0.\n\nBut let's see:.\n\nWait actually, the first number is 815,066,450. Let's break down each digit position:.\n\nStarting from the right (units):.\n\nPosition 1 (units): 0.\n\nPosition 2 (tens):5.\n\nPosition 3 (hundreds):4.\n\nPosition 4 (thousands):6.\n\nPosition 5 (ten thousands):6.\n\nPosition 6 (hundred thousands):0.\n\nPosition 7 (millions):5.\n\nPosition 8 (ten millions):1.\n\nPosition 9 (hundred millions):8.\n\nSecond number: 518,824,319.\n\nBreaking down:.\n\nPosition 1:9.\n\nPosition 2:1.\n\nPosition3:3.\n\nPosition4:4.\n\nPosition5:2.\n\nPosition6:8.\n\nPosition7:8.\n\nPosition8:1.\n\nPosition9:5.\n\nWait, but the second number is 518,824,319. Let me count the digits:.\n\n5 (hundred millions place), 1 (ten millions), 8 (millions), 8 (hundred thousands), 2 (ten thousands),4 (thousands),3 (hundreds),1 (tens),9 (ones). So:.\n\nPosition 1 (ones):9.\n\nPosition 2 (tens):1.\n\nPosition3 (hundreds):3.\n\nPosition4 (thousands):4.\n\nPosition5 (ten thousands):2.\n\nPosition6 (hundred thousands):8.\n\nPosition7 (millions):8.\n\nPosition8 (ten millions):1.\n\nPosition9 (hundred millions):5.\n\nSo now adding each position:.\n\nStarting from position1 (ones):.\n\n0 +9 =9, no carryover.\n\nPosition2 (tens):5 +1=6, no carry.\n\nPosition3 (hundreds):4+3=7, no carry.\n\nPosition4 (thousands):6+4=10. So write 0, carryover 1.\n\nPosition5 (ten thousands):6 (from first) +2 (second) +1 (carry)= 9. So 9, no carry.\n\nPosition6 (hundred thousands):0 +8=8, no carry.\n\nPosition7 (millions):5 (first) +8 (second) =13. So write 3, carryover 1.\n\nPosition8 (ten millions):1 (first) +1 (second) +1 (carry) =3. So 3, no carry.\n\nPosition9 (hundred millions):8 +5 =13. So write 3, carryover 1. But since there are no more digits, that carryover 1 becomes a new digit at the next higher place (billions place).\n\nPutting it all together:.\n\nStarting from the right, the digits are:.\n\nPosition1:9.\n\nPosition2:6.\n\nPosition3:7.\n\nPosition4:0.\n\nPosition5:9.\n\nPosition6:8.\n\nPosition7:3 (since millions place was 13, so 3 with carryover 1 to the next).\n\nWait, let me go step by step again.\n\nWait, let's do each position step by step carefully:.\n\nPosition 1 (ones): 0 +9 =9. Write down 9.\n\nPosition2 (tens):5+1=6. Write 6.\n\nPosition3 (hundreds):4+3=7. Write 7.\n\nPosition4 (thousands):6+4=10. Write 0, carryover 1.\n\nPosition5 (ten thousands):6 (from first number) +2 (second) +1 (carry) = 9. Write 9, no carry.\n\nPosition6 (hundred thousands):0 (first) +8 (second) =8. Write 8.\n\nPosition7 (millions):5 (first) +8 (second) =13. Write 3, carryover1.\n\nPosition8 (ten millions):1 (first) +1 (second) +1 (carry) =3. Write 3, no carry.\n\nPosition9 (hundred millions):8 (first) +5 (second) =13. Write 3, carryover1.\n\nNow, since there's a carryover of 1 from the hundred millions addition, we need to add a new digit at the billions place: 1.\n\nSo the final digits from right to left (positions 1 to 9 plus the new billion):.\n\nThe digits would be:.\n\nBillions place:1.\n\nHundred millions:3 (from 8+5 + carryover? Wait wait, let me see:.\n\nWait, the carryover from the hundred millions (position9) addition was 1. So the total at position9 is 13, so digit 3, carryover1. That carryover adds to the billions place, so the billions digit is 1. So the total number is 1,3 (hundred millions), then the rest.\n\nSo putting all together from highest to lowest:.\n\nBillions:1.\n\nHundred millions:3.\n\nTen millions:3.\n\nMillions:3 (Wait, need to check each position again step by step.).\n\nWait perhaps it's better to list all the digits in order from highest to lowest.\n\nWait let's reconstruct the sum step by step.\n\nStarting from the right (position1 to position9, then the carryover):.\n\nThe result digits from each position:.\n\nPosition1:9.\n\nPosition2:6.\n\nPosition3:7.\n\nPosition4:0.\n\nPosition5:9.\n\nPosition6:8.\n\nPosition7:3 (with a carryover1 to position8).\n\nPosition8:3 (from 1+1+1 carryover?).\n\nWait let me go step by step again for positions 7 and 8:.\n\nPosition7 (millions place):.\n\nFirst number's millions digit is 5 (since 815,066,450 has 5 in the millions place? Wait original first number is 815,066,450.\n\nBreaking down 815,066,450:.\n\n- 8 (hundred millions).\n\n- 1 (ten millions).\n\n- 5 (millions).\n\n- 0 (hundred thousands).\n\n- 6 (ten thousands).\n\n- 6 (thousands).\n\n- 4 (hundreds).\n\n- 5 (tens).\n\n- 0 (ones).\n\nWait so the millions digit is 5, correct. The second number's millions digit is 8 (since 518,824,319 is 5 (hundred millions),1 (ten millions),8 (millions)..). So yes, millions place is 5+8=13. So we write 3, carryover1.\n\nPosition8 (ten millions place):.\n\nFirst number has 1 (ten millions), second has1 (ten millions), plus the carryover1. So 1+1+1=3. So digit is 3, no carryover.\n\nPosition9 (hundred millions place):.\n\nFirst number has 8, second has5. 8+5=13. So write 3, carryover1.\n\nNow, the carryover1 from the hundred millions place goes to the next higher place, which is the billions place. Since there are no more digits, we just add that 1 there.\n\nSo the billions digit is 1, followed by the hundred millions digit 3, then ten millions 3, millions 3, etc.\n\nPutting all the digits together from highest to lowest:.\n\nBillions:1.\n\nHundred millions:3.\n\nTen millions:3.\n\nMillions:3 (Wait, position7 is millions place, which we had 3.\n\nWait let me list all the digits in order from the highest place to the lowest (left to right):.\n\nStarting with the billions digit:1.\n\nThen hundred millions:3.\n\nThen ten millions:3.\n\nMillions:3 (position7).\n\nHundred thousands:8 (position6).\n\nTen thousands:9 (position5).\n\nThousands:0 (position4).\n\nHundreds:7 (position3).\n\nTens:6 (position2).\n\nOnes:9 (position1).\n\nSo the number is 1,3 (hundred millions), 3 (ten millions), 3 (millions), 8 (hundred thousands), 9 (ten thousands), 0 (thousands),7 (hundreds),6 (tens),9 (ones).\n\nPutting that all together:. So, let me directly concl