is wrong because the millions digit should be 4, so the ten millions is 3, but the hundred millions is 1, so the digits up to hundred millions are 1 (billions),1 (hundred millions),3 (ten millions),4 (millions). So when written as 1,134,073,157. \n\nWait, maybe I messed up the digit positions when I first wrote them. Let me write out all the digits in order from left to right (highest to lowest):\n\nBillions:1\n\nHundred millions:1\n\nTen millions:3\n\nMillions:4\n\nHundred thousands:0\n\nTen thousands:7\n\nThousands:3\n\nHundreds:1\n\nTens:5\n\nOnes:7\n\nTherefore the number is 1,134,073,157. \n\nHmm, so where did I get 1,133,073,157 before? Probably miscalculating the ten millions place. Let me check that again. Ten millions place: first number has 7, second has5, plus carryover1 from millions place (which was 14, so carryover1). So 7+5+1=13 → so ten millions digit is 3, carryover1. So that's correct. So the ten millions digit is indeed 3, so the digits up to hundred millions are 1 (hundred millions) then 3 (ten millions), so the first four digits (billions, hundred millions, ten millions, millions) are 1,1,3,4. So that would be 1,134... So the correct total is 1,134,073,157. \n\nTherefore, my initial mistake was probably a typo when I first wrote 1,133,073,157, but after rechecking, it should be 1,134,073,157. Let me confirm once more with another method.\n\nAlternatively, adding the two numbers:\n\n378,659,453 + 755,413,704.\n\nLet me add 378,659,453 + 700,000,000 = 1,078,659,453.\n\nThen add 55,413,704 to that.\n\n1,078,659,453 + 55,413,704.\n\nFirst add 50,000,000: 1,078,659,453 +50,000,000 = 1,128,659,453.\n\nThen add 5,413,704:\n\n1,128,659,453 +5,413,704.\n\nAdding 5,000,000: 1,128,659,453 +5,000,000 = 1,133,659,453.\n\nThen add 413,704:\n\n1,133,659,453 +413,704.\n\nBreakdown: 400,000 +13,704.\n\nAdding 400,000: 1,133,659,453 +400,000 =1,134,059,453.\n\nThen add 13,704: 1,134,059,453 +13,704 =1,134,073,157.\n\nYes! So that confirms it. The correct sum is 1