llions), comma 3 (hundred thousands), 0 (ten thousands), 5 (thousands), comma 5 (hundreds), 0 (tens), 6 (ones). Yes, that's correct. So the first number's places:.\n\nHundred millions:1, Ten millions:2, Millions:0, Hundred thousands:3, Ten thousands:0, Thousands:5, Hundreds:5, Tens:0, Ones:6.\n\nSecond number is 166,917,337: Hundred millions:1, Ten millions:6, Millions:6, Hundred thousands:9, Ten thousands:1, Thousands:7, Hundreds:3, Tens:3, Ones:7.\n\nSo when adding, let's align all digits properly:.\n\nStarting from the right (ones place):.\n\nFirst number: 6.\n\nSecond number:7.\n\nSum: 13. Write 3, carryover 1.\n\nTens place:.\n\nFirst:0, second:3, plus carryover 1 → total 4. Write 4, no carryover.\n\nHundreds:.\n\nFirst:5, second:3 → 8. Write 8.\n\nThousands:.\n\nFirst:5, second:7 → 12. Write 2, carryover 1.\n\nTen thousands:.\n\nFirst:0, second:1, plus carryover 1 → 2. Write 2.\n\nHundred thousands:.\n\nFirst:3, second:9 →12. Write 2, carryover 1.\n\nMillions:.\n\nFirst:0 (since the first number has 0 in millions place?), Wait, wait, let me check again:.\n\nWait, first number's millions place is digit 7 (from the right?), let me recount:.\n\nWait, perhaps it's better to write both numbers digit by digit with their place values.\n\nLet me write both numbers with their place values aligned:.\n\nFirst number (120,305,506):.\n\n- Hundred millions: 1.\n\n- Ten millions: 2.\n\n- Millions: 0.\n\n- Hundred thousands: 3.\n\n- Ten thousands: 0.\n\n- Thousands: 5.\n\n- Hundreds: 5.\n\n- Tens: 0.\n\n- Ones: 6.\n\nSecond number (166,917,337):.\n\n- Hundred millions: 1.\n\n- Ten millions: 6.\n\n- Millions: 6.\n\n- Hundred thousands: 9.\n\n- Ten thousands: 1.\n\n- Thousands: 7.\n\n- Hundreds: 3.\n\n- Tens: 3.\n\n- Ones:7.\n\nNow adding each column starting from the right (ones):.\n\nOnes: 6 +7 =13 → 3, carryover 1.\n\nTens: 0 +3 +1 =4 →4.\n\nHundreds:5 +3=8 →8.\n\nThousands:5 +7=12 →2, carryover1.\n\nTen thousands:0 +1 +1 (carry) =2 →2.\n\nHundred thousands:3 +9=12 →2, carryover1.\n\nMillions:0 +6 +1 (carry) =7 →7.\n\nTen millions:2 +6 =8 →8.\n\nHundred millions:1 +1 =2 →2.\n\nWait, but wait, let's check the millions place again:.\n\nWait, the first number has millions as 0, and the second has millions as6. So adding 0+6=6, but there was a carryover from the hundred thousands place which was 1. So 0+6 +1 =7. That's correct.\n\nTen millions place: first number has 2, second has6 →2+6=8. No carryover.\n\nHundred millions:1+1=2.\n\nSo putting all together:.\n\nStarting from the right, the digits are:.\n\nOnes:3.\n\nTens:4.\n\nHundreds:8.\n\nThousands:2.\n\nTen thousands:2.\n\nHundred thousands:2.\n\nMillions:7.\n\nTen millions:8.\n\nHundred millions:2.\n\nSo the total sum is 2 (hundred millions),8 (ten millions),7 (millions),2 (hundred thousands),2 (ten thousands),2 (thousands),8 (hundreds),4 (tens),3 (ones). So, let me directly conclude the answer. The answer is "]]
