git). So when adding the second number's thousands digit (2) to the first's (0), we should get 2, but in the step-by-step addition, the 3,000 is coming from the remaining part's thousands digit (3), which is incorrect.\n\nThe error is in the breakdown method's assumption that the remaining parts can be added independently, but their digits beyond the millions can affect lower places. However, the digit-wise addition correctly accounts for all digit positions. The step-by-step addition is flawed because when adding the remaining parts (223,061), it treats the 223,000 as adding to the hundred thousands place, but in reality, the thousands digit of the second number is 2, not 3. The remaining part's thousands digit is 3 because it's part of the 223,000, but in the full number, the thousands digit is 2 because the breakdown of the number's digits shows it's 2. Therefore, the step-by-step addition incorrectly added 3,000, but should have added 2,000.\n\nWait, this is getting too tangled. The correct result must be from the digit-wise addition since it's a straightforward column addition. Therefore, the correct answer is 1,190,322,415.\n\nBut why does the step-by-step addition give a different result? Let me re-examine that:\n\nThe second number is 359,223,061. Breaking it down into its components:\n\n300,000,000 (3 hundred millions),\n\n50,000,000 (5 ten millions),\n\n9,000,000 (9 millions),\n\n200,000 (2 hundred thousands),\n\n20,000 (2 ten thousands),\n\n2,000 (2 thousands),\n\n300 (3 hundreds),\n\n0 (tens),\n\n1 (ones).\n\nSo when adding this to the first number:\n\nStarting with 831,100,114:\n\nAdding 300,000,000 \u2192 1,131,100,114,\n\n+50,000,000 \u2192 1,181,100,114,\n\n+9,000,000 \u21921,190,100,114,\n\n+200,000 \u21921,190,300,114,\n\n+20,000 \u21921,190,320,114,\n\n+2,000 \u21921,190,322,114,\n\n+300 \u21921,190,322,414,\n\n+0 \u2192 no change,\n\n+1 \u21921,190,322,415.\n\nAh! There's the mistake! In the earlier step-by-step, I incorrectly added 3,000 instead