ryover somewhere.\n\nWait, let me re-express the digit-wise addition step-by-step with all positions:\n\nStarting from the rightmost digit (position1):\n\nPosition1 (ones):4 +1=5 → digit5.\n\nPosition2 (tens):1 +0=1 → digit1.\n\nPosition3 (hundreds):1 +3=4 → digit4.\n\nPosition4 (thousands):0 +2=2 → digit2.\n\nPosition5 (ten thousands):0 +2=2 → digit2.\n\nPosition6 (hundred thousands):1 +2=3 → digit3.\n\nPosition7 (millions):1 (first number) +9 (second number) =10 → digit0, carryover1.\n\nPosition8 (ten millions):3 (first number) +5 (second number) +1 (carryover) =9 → digit9, no carryover.\n\nPosition9 (hundred millions):8 (first) +3 (second) =11 → digit1, carryover1.\n\nPosition10 (billions): carryover1 → digit1.\n\nSo the digits from position10 to1 are:\n\nPosition10:1,\n\nPosition9:1,\n\nPosition8:9,\n\nPosition7:0,\n\nPosition6:3,\n\nPosition5:2,\n\nPosition4:2,\n\nPosition3:4,\n\nPosition2:1,\n\nPosition1:5.\n\nThus, the number is 1,190,322,415.\n\nBut step-by-step addition gives 1,190,323,175.\n\nWait a second, I think I see the mistake now. In the digit-wise addition, the millions place (position7) has a result of 0 with a carryover, but in the step-by-step, when adding 9,000,000 to 831,100,114, it becomes 1,190,100,114. The millions place here is 1 (from the original 1 million) plus 9 million gives 10 million, so the millions digit is 0 with a carryover, which is correctly accounted for. So the digit-wise is correct.\n\nHowever, in the step-by-step addition, after adding 9 million, we get 1,190,100,114. Then adding 200,000 (hundred thousands place) gives 1,190,300,114. Then adding 20,000 (ten thousands) →1,190,320,114. Adding 3,000 (thousands) →1,190,323,114.\n\nWait, but the thousands digit here is 3, but according to the digit-wise addition, the thousands digit should be 2.\n\nAh! Here's the mistake. The step-by-step addition is adding the 200,000 (hundred thousands place), which is part of the second number's 223,061. The 200,000 is adding to the hundred 