the first number is 800,133,575. Breaking it down: the digits are 8,0,0,1,3,3,5,7,5. Wait, maybe I miscounted the places earlier. Let me verify the positions again to make sure I didn't shift any digits.\n\nWait, perhaps I should write out each digit position clearly. Let me list each digit from right to left with their places:.\n\nStarting from the right (ones place):.\n\nPosition 1 (ones): 5 (from first number) + 0 (second) = 5.\nPosition 2 (tens): 7 + 0 =7.\nPosition3 (hundreds):5+2=7.\nPosition4 (thousands):3+3=6.\nPosition5 (ten thousands):1+2=3.\nPosition6 (hundred thousands):3+8=11 → write 1, carryover 1.\nPosition7 (millions):0 +4 +1 (carry) =5.\nPosition8 (ten millions):0 +0=0? Wait, hold on, maybe my numbering is off here. Let me see the actual digits.\n\nWait, let's parse each number digit by digit properly.\n\nFirst number: 800,133,575. Breaking it down:.\n\nDigits from right to left (positions 1 to 9):.\n\nPosition 1: 5 (ones).\nPosition 2: 7 (tens).\nPosition3:5 (hundreds).\nPosition4:3 (thousands).\nPosition5:3 (ten thousands).\nPosition6:1 (hundred thousands).\nPosition7:0 (millions).\nPosition8:0 (ten millions).\nPosition9:8 (hundred millions).\n\nSecond number: 440,823,201. Breaking down similarly:.\n\nPosition1:1 (ones).\nPosition2:0 (tens).\nPosition3:2 (hundreds).\nPosition4:3 (thousands).\nPosition5:2 (ten thousands).\nPosition6:8 (hundred thousands).\nPosition7:4 (millions).\nPosition8:4 (ten millions).\nPosition9:0 (hundred millions).\n\nWait, hold on, the second number is 440,823,201. Let's confirm:.\n\nBreaking down 440,823,201:.\n\nStarting from the right:.\n\nDigits are 1 (ones), 0 (tens), 2 (hundreds), 3 (thousands), 2 (ten thousands), 8 (hundred thousands), 4 (millions), 4 (ten millions), 0 (hundred millions). Wait, but the number is 440,823,201. Let me write it as 4 4 0 8 2 3 2 0 1. Wait, commas are placed every three digits. So:.\n\n440,823,201 is 4 (hundred millions), 4 (ten millions), 0 (millions), 8 (hundred thousands), 2 (ten thousands), 3 (thousands), 2 (hundreds), 0 (tens), 1 (ones). Wait, maybe I need to parse correctly.\n\nWait perhaps it's better to write both numbers without commas:.\n\nFirst number: 800133575. Let's count digits:.\n\n8 0 0 1 3 3 5 7 5. So that's 9 digits: positions 1 to 9 from right:.\n\nPosition1:5, pos2:7, pos3:5, pos4:3, pos5:3, pos6:1, pos7:0, pos8:0, pos9:8.\n\nSecond number:440823201: digits are 4 4 0 8 2 3 2 0 1. Wait, no, let me see:.\n\nWait 440,823,201 without commas is 440823201. Breaking down each digit from right:.\n\nPosition1:1 (ones).\nPosition2:0 (tens).\nPosition3:2 (hundreds).\nPosition4:3 (thousands).\nPosition5:2 (ten thousands).\nPosition6:8 (hundred thousands).\nPosition7:0 (millions?) Wait, no, let's see:.\n\nWait, the number is 440,823,201. Breaking down:.\n\n- The first comma separates millions: 440, so that's 440 million. Then 823 thousand, 201. Wait, actually:.\n\nWait 440,823,201 is 440 million, 823 thousand, 201. So the digits are:.\n\n4 (hundred millions), 4 (ten millions), 0 (millions), 8 (hundred thousands), 2 (ten thousands), 3 (thousands), 2 (hundreds), 0 (tens), 1 (ones). So the digits from left to right are:.\n\n4 (hundred millions), 4 (ten millions), 0 (millions), 8 (hundred thousands), 2 (ten thousands), 3 (thousands), 2 (hundreds), 0 (tens), 1 (ones).\n\nSo when written as a 9-digit number, positions from right (ones) are:.\n\nPosition1:1.\n\nPosition2:0.\n\nPosition3:2 (hundreds).\n\nPosition4:3 (thousands).\n\nPosition5:2 (ten thousands).\n\nPosition6:8 (hundred thousands).\n\nPosition7:0 (millions).\n\nPosition8:4 (ten millions).\n\nPosition9:4 (hundred millions).\n\nWait, that seems conflicting. Maybe I need to list them properly.\n\nAlternatively, let's write both numbers digit by digit from the right (ones to the left):.\n\nFirst number (800133575):.\n\nStarting from the right (position 1):.\n\nPosition1:5 (ones).\n\nPosition2:7 (tens).\n\nPosition3:5 (hundreds).\n\nPosition4:3 (thousands).\n\nPosition5:3 (ten thousands).\n\nPosition6:1 (hundred thousands).\n\nPosition7:0 (millions).\n\nPosition8:0 (ten millions).\n\nPosition9:8 (hundred millions).\n\nSecond number (440823201):.\n\nPosition1:1 (ones).\n\nPosition2:0 (tens).\n\nPosition3:2 (hundreds).\n\nPosition4:3 (thousands).\n\nPosition5:2 (ten thousands).\n\nPosition6:8 (hundred thousands).\n\nPosition7:0 (millions).\n\nPosition8:4 (ten millions).\n\nPosition9:4 (hundred millions).\n\nWait, hold on, that can't be right because 440,823,201 has the first digit as 4 (hundred millions place). So in the second number, the digits from right are:.\n\nDigit 1:1 (position1).\n\nDigit2:0 (position2).\n\nDigit3:2 (position3).\n\nDigit4:3 (position4).\n\nDigit5:2 (position5).\n\nDigit6:8 (position6).\n\nDigit7:0 (position7).\n\nDigit8:4 (position8).\n\nDigit9:4 (position9).\n\nYes, that's correct.\n\nNow, let's add each position step by step:.\n\nStarting with Position1 (ones):.\n\n5 (from first) + 1 (from second) = 6. So ones place is 6. Wait, earlier I thought the second number had 0 in ones place. Wait, no! Wait, in the second number, the ones digit is 1, not 0. Oh no! I made a mistake earlier. Let me correct that.\n\nWait the second number is 440,823,201. The last digit is 1, so Position1 is 1. Therefore, in my initial calculation, I incorrectly said the second number's ones place was 0. That's an error. So I need to fix that.\n\nSo starting over:.\n\nPosition1 (ones): 5 (first number) + 1 (second) = 6. So ones digit is 6. No carryover.\n\nPosition2 (tens): 7 (first) + 0 (second) =7. Tens digit 7.\n\nPosition3 (hundreds):5 +2 =7. Hundreds digit 7.\n\nPosition4 (thousands):3 +3=6. Thousands digit 6.\n\nPosition5 (ten thousands):3 +2=5. Ten thousands digit 5.\n\nPosition6 (hundred thousands):1 +8=9. So 9, no carryover.\n\nPosition7 (millions):0 +0=0. Wait, first number has 0 in millions place (position7) and second number has 0? Wait, second number's position7 is millions place? Let me check.\n\nWait in the second number, position7 (millio